{"abstract":"Accurate prediction of wave run-up and loads from extreme waves on offshore structures are important for the design of the structure itself but also to secure safe secondary structures such as boat landings and service platforms. We have recently developed a new method called the Direct Surface Description (DSD) method in OpenFOAM (Qwist & Christensen, 2023)[1]. Here it was shown that the DSD method is superior compared to the existing official release OpenFOAM solver, InterFoam, with respect to prediction of wave kinematics and surface elevation. In this paper, our recently developed Direct Surface Description (DSD) solution algorithm in OpenFOAM simulates wave runup on circular mono-piles. The results are compared to results from InterIsoFoam (two-phase) that has been a part of OpenFOAM for many years. This is a step towards improved predictions of the forces on secondary structures such as boat landings and service platforms for offshore wind turbines.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Christensen, Erik Damgaard","Belza, Sigrid Tanghus","Nielsen, Louise Nørgaard","Qwist, Jesper Roland Kjærgaard","Mandviwalla, Xerxes"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-827635"],"event":{"end":"2025-06-27","place":"Vancouver, ca","start":"2025-06-22","title":"44th International Conference on Ocean, Offshore and Arctic Engineering","year":"2025"},"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Vattenfall"],"keyword":["DSD method","OpenFOAM","Wave run up"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"numerical prediction of wave runup on a monopile with the direct surface description method","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1115/OMAE2025-157440","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-827635","identifier":[{"id":"105015299465","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-63a9685e-3f55-4841-a3c0-aeb124af58b9"],"keyword":["DSD-method","OpenFOAM","Wave run-up"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-17CA6FC96699FA0B696598C4335E2FEC"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/413274672/v003t06a015-omae2025-157440.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1115/OMAE2025-157440","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Civil and Mechanical Engineering","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Civil and Mechanical Engineering, Fluid Mechanics, Coastal and Maritime Engineering"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"se","key":"vattenfall","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Vattenfall","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Erik Damgaard","key":"christensenpau","last":"Christensen","locid":"c2692ef3-b38f-4a15-8662-446ba0db8cc5","name":"Christensen, Erik Damgaard","orcid":"0000-0002-5225-5828","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Sigrid Tanghus","key":"belzapau","last":"Belza","name":"Belza, Sigrid Tanghus","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Louise Nørgaard","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"3729d3a9-c6a6-4d44-8039-3aae3f30a7d1","name":"Nielsen, Louise Nørgaard","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jesper Roland Kjærgaard","key":"qwistpau","last":"Qwist","locid":"9d9be0f2-0127-441b-8f8f-d37177fce308","name":"Qwist, Jesper Roland Kjærgaard","orcid":"0000-0002-4686-7270","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Xerxes","key":"mandviwallapau","last":"Mandviwalla","name":"Mandviwalla, Xerxes","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1115/omae2025-157440","eisbn":["9780791888926"],"publisher":"The American Society of Mechanical Engineers (ASME)","status":"p","subtitle":"(OMAE2025)","title":"Proceedings of ASME 2025 44th International Conference on Ocean, Offshore and Arctic Engineering","volume":"3: Ocean Engineering; Polar and Arctic Sciences and Technology"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105015299465"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-63a9685e-3f55-4841-a3c0-aeb124af58b9"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Numerical Prediction of Wave-Runup on a Monopile with the Direct Surface Description Method","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-827635","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Background: Ataxia, characterized by incoordination of movement, presents a diverse etiological spectrum, including genetic forms such as spinocerebellar ataxias (SCAs), Friedreich’s ataxia (FRDA), and other hereditary ataxias. Identifying and understanding the distribution of the genetic subtypes in specific populations are crucial for clinical management, genetic counseling, and prognostication. Objective: This study is aimed at investigating the genetic epidemiology of hereditary ataxias in a clinical cohort from Eastern Denmark, focusing on the prevalence and distribution of the genetic ataxias. Methods: We conducted a chart review of 297 patients diagnosed with ataxia from the two major referral centers in Eastern Denmark between 2018 and 2023. Diagnoses were divided into groups: confirmed genetic ataxia, presumed genetic ataxia (no genetic variant identified, positive family history) and possible genetic ataxia (debut before the age of 40, no family history), sporadic adult-onset ataxia (SAOA) (debut after the age of 40, no family history), and multiple system atrophy–cerebellar type (MSA-C)). Data collected included demographics, clinical features, age of onset, and results of genetic testing. Results: Of the 297 patients, 144 (48.5%) had a confirmed genetic ataxia, 26 (8.8%) were classified as presumed genetic ataxia, and 19 (6.4%) were categorized as possible genetic ataxia. The most common subtypes were SCA6, SCA2, and SCA3. The study revealed notable differences in the prevalence of specific ataxia subtypes compared to global patterns. Conclusion: This study provides an overview of the epidemiology and genetic landscape of hereditary ataxias in Denmark. The high prevalence of SCA6 and unique distribution patterns emphasizes the need for population-specific data to guide clinical practice. Ongoing trials for SCA1 and SCA3 highlight the importance of understanding the epidemiology of ataxias across different countries to establish trial-ready cohorts and address future treatment needs.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Kayser, Jesper Dybdal","Waerling, Rosa Dam","Lindquist, Suzanne Granhøj","Duno, Morten","Nielsen, Jørgen Erik","Vinther-Jensen, Tua"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-828980"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Denmark","Genetic epidemiology","Spinocerebellar ataxia"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"hereditary ataxias a genetic epidemiological study of a danish clinical cohort","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1155/ane/1614771","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-828980","identifier":[{"id":"105010658090","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-5d8f5845-d998-453e-86ce-020bce8f933e"],"keyword":["Denmark","genetic epidemiology","hereditary ataxia","spinocerebellar ataxia"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-CD589D1A65FA8889B845B9DB2C3B1BF6"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/457735983/Acta_Neurologica_Scandinavica_-_2025_-_Kayser_-_Hereditary_Ataxias_A_Genetic_Epidemiological_Study_of_a_Danish_Clinical.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1155/ane/1614771","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Clinical Genetics","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Jesper Dybdal","key":"kayserpau","last":"Kayser","name":"Kayser, Jesper Dybdal","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Rosa Dam","key":"waerlingpau","last":"Waerling","name":"Waerling, Rosa Dam","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Suzanne Granhøj","key":"lindquistpau","last":"Lindquist","locid":"9c4e6ac3-cfb2-4ca6-ba64-8ec673ec68e6","name":"Lindquist, Suzanne Granhøj","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Morten","key":"dunopau","last":"Duno","name":"Duno, Morten","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Jørgen Erik","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"eefef282-eb8c-4a7f-a85e-4b77dd779a3c","name":"Nielsen, Jørgen Erik","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Tua","key":"vintherjensenpau","last":"Vinther-Jensen","name":"Vinther-Jensen, Tua","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1155/ane/1614771","issn":["00016314","16000404"],"issn_org":["00016314"],"issue":"1","journal":"Acta Neurologica Scandinavica","paperid":"1614771","status":"p","title":"Acta Neurologica Scandinavica","volume":"2025"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105010658090"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-5d8f5845-d998-453e-86ce-020bce8f933e"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Hereditary Ataxias : A Genetic Epidemiological Study of a Danish Clinical Cohort","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-828980","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"INTRODUCTION: The purpose of this study is to examine line manager perspectives on two workplace-based efforts to reduce sickness absence (uniform procedures for managing sickness absence and initiating preventive actions), two components from an intervention to reduce sickness absence in public sector workplaces in Denmark. METHODS: We performed 19 semi-structured interviews with line managers from four public sector workplaces. The interviews were analysed using thematic analysis (TA). Normalization Process Theory (NPT) was used as theoretical framework. RESULTS: Uniform sickness absence procedures are meaningful and provide clear expectations for line managers and employees about roles and responsibilities during sick leave. Line managers expressed a desire for flexibility in adjusting the procedures to the individual needs of the employees. They also reported a need for proactive preventive actions that prevent sickness absence from occurring in the first place. The line managers reported lacking competencies to ensure appropriate sick leave management and that their own well-being was often overlooked. CONCLUSION: Future interventions should focus on improving the work environment instead of focusing solely on absenteeism. It is essential to consider the well-being of the line managers and provide adequate training, as this can affect their capability to ensure the well-being of, and reduce sickness absence, among their employees.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Rasmussen, Lene","Nielsen, Maj Britt Dahl","Garde, Anne Helene","Kristiansen, Jesper"],"cuid":["S02-099.92-814122"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Rasmussen, Lene"],"keyword":["Absenteeism","Adult","Denmark","Female","Humans","Intervention","Interviews as Topic","Line manager","Male","Mellemledere","Middle Aged","Normalization process theory","Occupational health","Public sector","Qualitative Research","Sick Leave","Sygefravær","Trivsel","Workplace"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-WORK"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-WORK","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"line manager perspectives on workplace based efforts to reduce sickness absence a qualitative study","src":["nfa","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-GV-WORK","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/17482631.2025.2510560","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-099.92-814122","identifier":[{"id":"105007026496","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC12128142","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"40445040","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["nfa-4212a94a-29d9-40c9-bc33-15975a1db245","sdu-028b4f06-b1d6-49ba-9e8a-81dacc4a10c0"],"keyword":["Absenteeism","Adult","Denmark","Female","Humans","Intervention","Interviews as Topic","Male","Mellemledere","Middle Aged","Occupational Health","Public Sector","Qualitative Research","Sick Leave","Sygefravær","Trivsel","Workplace","line manager","normalization process theory","sickness absence intervention"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-C8FE93E7E0AC0A71B7D252A91FC03556"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/17482631.2025.2510560","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/291591526/Line_manager_perspectives_on_workplace-based_efforts_to_reduce_sickness_absence_a_qualitative_study.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"nfa national research centre for the working environment","l2":"DI-GV","l2n":"Governmental Institutions","l3":"DI-GV-WORK","l3nn":"NFA National Research Centre for the Working Environment","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"NFA National Research Centre for the Working Environment","namevar":["The National Research Centre for the Working Environment","The National Research Centre for the Working Environment, Directors, Psychosocial Working Environment and Working Accidents and Safety Culture"],"nora_code":"DI-GV-WORK","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, National Institute of Public Health","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, National Institute of Public Health, Mental health"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lene","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","locid":"0d8ad03f-8294-49a2-8bb7-fea40eccf25a","name":"Rasmussen, Lene","orcid":"0000-0002-7864-4178","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Maj Britt Dahl","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"9dfb8e84-90f3-4531-b486-2df21e9ecd3a","name":"Nielsen, Maj Britt Dahl","orcid":"0000-0002-5758-8031","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anne Helene","key":"gardepau","last":"Garde","locid":"8e894baf-467c-45d3-8abe-41a805b30b22","name":"Garde, Anne Helene","orcid":"0000-0002-1553-2291","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jesper","key":"kristiansenpau","last":"Kristiansen","locid":"2b90a97c-15d6-468c-a8d6-1362e731d2bc","name":"Kristiansen, Jesper","orcid":"0000-0002-1637-8900","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40445040"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1080/17482631.2025.2510560","issn":["17482623","17482631"],"issue":"1","journal":"International Journal of Qualitative Studies on Health and Well-Being","pages":"2510560","publisher":"Co-Action Publishing","status":"p","title":"International Journal of Qualitative Studies on Health and Well-Being","title_org":"International Journal of Qualitative Studies on Health and Well-being","volume":"20"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105007026496"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["nfa-4212a94a-29d9-40c9-bc33-15975a1db245","sdu-028b4f06-b1d6-49ba-9e8a-81dacc4a10c0"],"src":["NFA National Research Centre for the Working Environment","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Line manager perspectives on workplace-based efforts to reduce sickness absence : a qualitative study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-099.92-814122","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"INTRODUCTION: Respiratory tract infections (RTIs) are a leading cause of morbidity and mortality in children worldwide. Aetiologic virus and bacteria vary geographically. To date, no systematic data on RTI aetiologies have been published from Kyrgyzstan, a country with a high under-five mortality from lower RTIs and a widespread overuse of antibiotics. We aimed to identify the aetiologies of RTI in children in Kyrgyzstan and to assess the pneumococcal conjugate vaccine (PCV) vaccination rate and the correlation with the detection of Streptococcus pneumoniae in our population. METHODS: We collected samples from children aged 6 months to 12 years presenting with symptoms of acute RTI for outpatients or diagnosed with pneumonia for inpatients. Samples were collected from November 2022 to June 2023 and were analysed using standard culture methods and real-time polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR) to identify bacterial, viral, and atypical bacterial pathogens. We obtained the vaccination status through vaccination records and parents' recollections. RESULTS: Positive bacteriologic cultures were found for 294 (25.0%) of 1174 outpatients and 83 (27.7%) of 300 inpatients. S. pneumoniae was the most commonly found bacterial pathogen in both groups, making up 44.8% of all cultured bacteria. Furthermore, 445 (64.1%) of 694 outpatients with RT-PCR performed had one or more viral pathogens identified, while this was the case for 162 (54.7%) of 296 inpatients. Mycoplasma pneumoniae was identified in 15.8% of inpatients possibly representing an epidemic in the spring of 2023. Approximately 20% of both in- and outpatients had a viral-bacterial simultaneous detection. 87.1% of outpatients and 75.9% of inpatients had been vaccinated with PCV. We found a higher proportion of unvaccinated children among those under five years of age and a higher carriage rate of S. pneumoniae among unvaccinated inpatients. CONCLUSION: In this study, we found a high prevalence of viral infections and viral-bacterial simultaneous detections among Kyrgyz children with respiratory tract infections (RTI). The findings from this study can inform new clinical guidelines for local health workers and lead to more targeted use of antibiotics and improved health outcomes for children in Kyrgyzstan. CLINICAL TRIAL: Not applicable.","author_count":20,"contributors":["Osmonbaeva, Nargiza","Bloch, Joakim","Erkinbaeva, Aidai","Tilebalieva, Aigerim","Tolobekova, Raisa","Zhaparova, Meerim","Rogbo-Bengtsson, Aichatou","Isaeva, Elvira","Akylbekov, Azamat","Mademilov, Maamed","Baltabaeva, Erkingul","Esengeldieva, Gulbarchyn","Jensen, Christian Salgård","Nielsen, Alex Christian Yde","Kirkby, Nikolai Søren","Skov, Robert","Poulsen, Anja","Kjærgaard, Jesper","Sooronbaev, Talant","Kurtzhals, Jørgen A L"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-804560"],"filters":{"added":"2025-04","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Asia, Central","Asia, South"],"corresponding_author":["Bloch, Joakim"],"country":["India","Kyrgyzstan"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Department of Disease Prevention and State Sanitary and Epidemiological Surveillance","National Center of Maternity and Childhood Care","National Centre of Cardiology and Internal Medicine Named After Academician M. Mirrakhimov","National Centre of Maternity and Childhood Care","Satkynbai Tentishev Memorial Asian Medical Institute"],"keyword":["Child","Child Preschool","Female","Humans","Infant","Kyrgyzstan epidemiology","Male","Pneumococcal Vaccines administration dosage","Respiratory Tract Infections microbiology","Streptococcus pneumoniae isolation purification","Vaccination statistics numerical data"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"OT":["Department of Disease Prevention and State Sanitary and Epidemiological Surveillance"],"U":["DI-U-