,"first":"Robert","key":"skovpau","last":"Skov","name":"Skov, Robert","rank":16,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Anja","key":"poulsenpau","last":"Poulsen","name":"Poulsen, Anja","rank":17,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Jesper","key":"kjærgaardpau","last":"Kjærgaard","name":"Kjærgaard, Jesper","rank":18,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Talant","key":"sooronbaevpau","last":"Sooronbaev","name":"Sooronbaev, Talant","rank":19,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 8","first":"Jørgen A L","key":"kurtzhalspau","last":"Kurtzhals","locid":"e0731c1b-1543-4836-bb5a-6f05c9795754","name":"Kurtzhals, Jørgen A L","orcid":"0000-0003-2760-8713","rank":20,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40011805"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1186/s12879-025-10668-1","issn":["14712334"],"journal":"BMC Infectious Diseases","paperid":"280","publisher":"BioMed Central","status":"p","title":"BMC Infectious Diseases","volume":"25"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85218910819"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-c785e8e2-f415-413d-b3dd-95a45a5bb18f","sdu-fb5cb2ef-3a8e-47ab-a480-eaf66e06f675"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Microbiological aetiology of paediatric respiratory tract infections in Kyrgyzstan","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-804560","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"settingsOrder Article Reprints Open AccessArticle Implementation Outcomes and Recommendations of Two Physical Activity Interventions: Results from the Danish ACTIVE SCHOOL Feasibility Study by Lise Sohl Jeppesen 1,2,*ORCID,Jesper Sandfeld 3ORCID,Søren Smedegaard 4,Glen Nielsen 1ORCID,Mathias Brekke Mandelid 5,6ORCID,Malene Norup 2,Jacob Wienecke 1ORCID andAnna Bugge 2 1 Department of Nutrition, Exercise and Sports, University of Copenhagen, DK-2200 Copenhagen, Denmark 2 Department of Midwifery, Physiotherapy, Occupational Therapy and Psychomotor Therapy, University College Copenhagen, DK-2200 Copenhagen, Denmark 3 Faculty of Teacher Education, University College Copenhagen, DK-1799 Copenhagen, Denmark 4 Faculty of Education and Social Sciences, UCL University College, DK-5230 Odense, Denmark 5 Department of Pedagogy, Social Science and Religion, Faculty of Education, Arts and Sports, Western Norway University of Applied Sciences, NO-6856 Sogndal, Norway 6 Department of Sports, Physical Education and Outdoor Studies, Faculty of Humanities, Sports and Educational Science, University of South-Eastern Norway, NO-3800 Bø, Norway * Author to whom correspondence should be addressed. Int. J. Environ. Res. Public Health 2025, 22(1), 67; https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph22010067 Submission received: 15 November 2024 / Revised: 20 December 2024 / Accepted: 31 December 2024 / Published: 7 January 2025 (This article belongs to the Special Issue Physical Activity Interventions for Sedentary Behavior Change) Downloadkeyboard_arrow_down Browse Figures Versions Notes Abstract Physical activity (PA) should be an essential part of all children’s lives, as it can promote physical and mental health, enhance general well-being, and positively impact learning outcomes. Schools offer an ideal setting to encourage physical activity during the school day, as nearly all children attend school. However, schools present a complex environment for implementing PA, and sedentary behavior is common in classroom teaching. This study explores the feasibility of two types of school-based physical activity interventions: one based on research in exercise and cognition (Run, Jump & Fun) and another grounded in embodied learning (Move & Learn). Run, Jump & Fun can be conceptualized as extra non-curricular physical activity implemented into the school day while Move & Learn is integrated into curricular time. The 8-week study involved third-grade students and their teachers from seven schools, with educational strategies applied to support adoption. Quantitative and qualitative data were collected before, during, and after the 8-week period. The results indicated that both interventions were feasible to implement, leading to a series of recommendations for further refinement. These recommendations can guide the development of future school-based PA interventions and inspire other researchers to assess and improve their implementation strategies.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Jeppesen, Lise Sohl","Sandfeld, Jesper","Smedegaard, Søren","Nielsen, Glenn","Mandelid, Mathias Brekke","Norup, Malene","Wienecke, Jacob","Bugge, Anna"],"cuid":["S03-090.54-804405"],"filters":{"added":"2025-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Norway"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Højskolen i Sørøst, Norge","University of South-Eastern Norway","Western Norway University of Applied Sciences"],"keyword":["Children","Health interventions","Learning","Primary school","Public school","Teachers"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum","med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U","DI-UC"],"level3":{"OT":["Forsknings- og Innovationscenter for Idræt, Bevægelse og Læring"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"UC":["DI-UC-ALL","DI-UC-KP","DI-UC-UCL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU","DI-UC-KP","DI-UC-UCL"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"implementation outcomes and recommendations of two physical activity interventions results from the danish active school feasibility study","src":["kp","ku","ucl"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-UC-KP","DI-UC-UCL"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph22010067","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This research was funded by the Independent Research Fund Denmark (0132-00160B). The funder has no influence on the study design; collection, management, analysis, and interpretation of data; writing of the report; or the decision to submit the report for publication."],"id":"S03-090.54-804405","identifier":[{"id":"PMC11765277","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"39857521","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85216030851","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["kp-79c78326-9477-4589-83c4-110c7d8e3c3d","ku-3caf814f-909b-46be-9b5f-9f4666621669","ucl-e746ad6e-3c77-4ca9-bcde-e663036c074d"],"keyword":["children","health interventions","learning","primary school","public school","teachers"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities","Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-5A89C1D8DB0EFAB8754589676B451EF7"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/424186647/ijerph_22_00067.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/1660-4601/22/1/67","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph22010067","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Nutrition, Exercise and Sports, Movement and Neuroscience"],"nora_cod