rg University","AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Anthropometric measures and long-term mortality in non-ischaemic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction: Questioning the obesity paradox","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S04-094.88-803875","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"In this paper, we investigate the benefit of Condition-Based Maintenance (CBM) over Time-Based Maintenance (TBM) in a multi-component system. We consider a series system, where the components deteriorate according to a gamma process, with stochastic dependence between components modeled with a Clayton-Lévy copula function. Economic dependence between components is modeled as a joint setup cost for replacing components. The CBM and TBM policies are obtained by formulating the two cases as separate Markov decision processes and using dynamic programming for optimization. We compare varying system parameter configurations using the relative decrease in cost rate as a measure of how much the CBM policy outperforms the TBM policy. From the results of our numerical experiments the largest relative improvement when using CBM instead of TBM is 45%, which occurs in the single-component system. In the multi-component system, the cost rate improvement increases when the degree of stochastic dependence increases. Furthermore, when the number of components increases the improvement becomes less sensitive to the severity of failures. Finally, the results indicate that when the difference in the system components’ mean time to failure increases, so does the relative improvement in the cost rate.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Andersen, Jesper Fink","Nielsen, Bo Friis"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-789397"],"filters":{"added":"2025-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Condition based maintenance","Markov decision process","Multi component system"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a comparative study of time based maintenance and condition based maintenance for multi component systems","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ress.2024.110759","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-789397","identifier":[{"id":"85212848217","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-dad08252-7946-477b-90b2-508ade3cfb17"],"keyword":["Condition-based maintenance","Markov decision process","Multi-component system","Time-based maintenance"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-683D364B8455FB9C464F41F232AFDF62"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ress.2024.110759","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/386880376/1-s2.0-S0951832024008305-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science, Statistics and Data Analysis"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jesper Fink","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"b866063c-6bb9-4a46-8b75-8f054206f477","name":"Andersen, Jesper Fink","orcid":"0000-0002-3213-5853","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Bo Friis","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"364b98b5-e608-4186-a566-62fa91a37d59","name":"Nielsen, Bo Friis","orcid":"0000-0001-9146-9733","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.ress.2024.110759","issn":["09518320","18790836"],"issn_org":["09518320"],"journal":"Reliability Engineering and System Safety","paperid":"110759","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Reliability Engineering and System Safety","volume":"256"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85212848217"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-dad08252-7946-477b-90b2-508ade3cfb17"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"A comparative study of time-based maintenance and condition-based maintenance for multi-component systems","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-789397","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Industrial pectin production from citrus peels involves extended, multi-step acidic extraction at elevated temperatures. Pectin quality can be improved by reducing the extraction time or number of steps, but this comes at the expense of yield. A decrease in pectin yield revenue may be compensated for by developing new usage of the partially depectinized citrus residues. One option is to capitalize on the fucosylated xyloglucans present in the residues. Thus, the aim of this work was to maximize gentle extraction of fucosylated xyloglucan from partially depectinized citrus peel residues of orange and lemon and examine the effects of multi-step extraction on pectin quality. To maintain the structural integrity of the xyloglucan, an enzyme-assisted approach was used to release the xyloglucan from the fibrous pectin extraction residues, with a focus on enzymatic cellulose degradation. The performance of four commercial cellulase preparations were compared (Cellic CTec2, Cellic CTec3, ENZECO CE3, and ENZECO Glucanase PF) through a multivariate approach. All cellulase preparations, being multicomponent enzyme blends, induced partial degradation of xyloglucan simultaneously with the cellulose depolymerization; the lowest dosage of Cellic CTec2 (100 µL/g DM, 40 °C, pH 7.0) resulted in the highest yields of solubilized fucosylated xyloglucans of 53 % and 39 % from the depectinized lemon and orange residues, respectively. Following the enzymatic extraction, membrane filtration outperformed alcohol precipitation in separating the solubilized xyloglucan oligosaccharides (< 2 kDa) from the simultaneously released high molecular weight rhamnogalacturonan I-like structures (500–10 kDa). The data provide new options for improved valorization of industrial depectinized citrus residues.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Biel-Nielsen, Tessa Lund","Sejberg, Jimmy J.P.","Meyer, Anne S.","Holck, Jesper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-798351"],"filters":{"added":"2025-04","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Holck, Jesper"],"keyword":["Downstream processing","Enzymatic extraction","Fucosylated xyloglucan"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-CPK"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-PR-CPK","DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"enzyme assisted extraction and product profiling of fucosylated xyloglucan from industrial citrus pectin residues","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nbt.2025.03.003","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-798351","identifier":[{"id":"105000076227","type":"scopus"},{"id":"40081758","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["dtu-c4a3dcaf-5bf0-4a08-95e9-1307105e953c"],"keyword":["Downstream processing","Enzymatic extraction","Fucosylated xyloglucan","Pectin residues"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-C262DE2A57FB208E378DE70E887D28D5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nbt.2025.03.003","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/394251666/1-s2.0-S1871678425000299-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Biotechnology and Biomedicine","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Chemical and Biochemical Engineering","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Chemical and Biochemical Engineering, CERE – Center for Energy Ressources Engineering"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"cp kelco","l2":"DI-PR","l2n":"Private Research","l3":"DI-PR-CPK","l3nn":"CP Kelco","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CP Kelco","namevar":["CPKelco ApS"],"nora_code":"DI-PR-CPK","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Tessa L.","key":"bielnielsenpau","last":"Biel-Nielsen","locid":"214325ab-3e5a-4526-91fc-5a237d9d5b61","name":"Biel-Nielsen, Tessa Lund","name_org":"Biel-Nielsen, Tessa L.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jimmy J.P.","key":"sejbergpau","last":"Sejberg","name":"Sejberg, Jimmy J.P.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anne S.","key":"meyerpau","last":"Meyer","locid":"1e9f4759-72d8-4d52-94ec-ce192ffca184","name":"Meyer, Anne S.","orcid":"0000-0001-8910-9931","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jesper","key":"holckpau","last":"Holck","locid":"d444e5ac-34d3-487b-9327-7ae08138d861","name":"Holck, Jesper","orcid":"0000-0002-8072-1101","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40081758"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.nbt.2025.03.003","issn":["18716784","18764347"],"issn_org":["18716784"],"journal":"New Biotechnology","pages":"93-104","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"New Biotechnology","volume":"87"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105000076227"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Private Research","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Private Research","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-c4a3dcaf-5bf0-4a08-95e9-1307105e953c"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Enzyme-assisted extraction and product profiling of fucosylated xyloglucan from industrial citrus pectin residues","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://