.0-105013197447"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Other Hospitals","Region Zealand","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark","liva healthcare"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-8c06aa28-c0fb-4874-8186-1b978a26d247"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Protocol for the Digital, Individualized, and Collaborative Treatment of Type 2 Diabetes in General Practice Based on Decision Aid (DICTA)—A Randomized Controlled Trial","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-831258","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Background and purpose: The prognosis following loco-regional failure after primary radiotherapy (RT) for head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) is poor. The hypothesis that most failures occur as a consequence of tumor radioresistance, can be evaluated by proxy as the proportion of failures that occur in the high-dose region. Several studies have investigated possible reasons for treatment failure by an image-based pattern of failure analyses (POF), comparing the initial planning CT scan with a scan conducted upon failure. The aim of the present systematic review and meta-analysis was to evaluate the proportion of failures that occurred in the high-dose region of all analyzed failures. Materials and Methods: A systematic database search from 2000 to 2023, was performed for studies including results from image-based loco-regional POF, regardless of the method, after primary RT for HNSCC. Proportions of volumetrically in-field (opposed to marginal or outfield) failures, point of origin-based inside high-dose targets, or covered by curative doses for both the number of patients and the number of failure sites were analyzed in proportional meta-analyses. The review was registered at Prospero (CRD42023412545). Results: Out of 56 included studies, accumulated image-based POF results were available from 1,161 patients and 658 individual failure sites. The majority of patients had in-field failures in volumetric-based studies (84 % (95 % CI: 77;90)), inside failures in point of origin-based studies (82 % (95% CI:61;85)) or failures covered by 95 % of dose prescribed to CTV1 (84 % (95% CI:69;95)). A trend toward increasing proportions of non-high-dose failures in more recently treated patients was observed. Conclusion: Most loco-regional failures for patients treated with primary RT for HNSCC are related to the high-dose volume. Therefore, a focus on biomarkers predicting individual tumor radiosensitivity is warranted to enable individualized treatment intensification to increase loco-regional control.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Kristensen, Morten Horsholt","Nielsen, Signe Bergliot","Alsner, Jan","Holm, Anne","Hansen, Christian Rønn","Overgaard, Jens","Eriksen, Jesper Grau"],"cuid":["S02-095.65-804189"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Kristensen, Morten Horsholt"],"keyword":["Geographical miss","HNSCC","High dose failure","Image based pattern of failure","Image co registration","In field failure","Meta analysis","Pattern of failure","Radioresistance","Recurrence","Systematic review"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-SDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a systematic review and proportional meta analysis of image based pattern of loco regional failure analyses outcomes in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma","src":["au","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.radonc.2025.110838","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-095.65-804189","identifier":[{"id":"40089161","type":"pmid"},{"id":"105000619964","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-abb76d57-7204-4637-9f89-c48a7532d1ea","sdu-f93870ea-7775-439f-b744-f1df9e40e748"],"keyword":["Geographical miss","HNSCC","High-dose failure","Image co-registration","Image-based pattern of failure","In-field failure","Meta-analysis","Pattern of failure","Radioresistance","Recurrence","Systematic review"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-35D4DEE71B522EC16BD1541C9A8AC779"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/287370359/PIIS0167814025001331.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.radonc.2025.110838","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - The Department of Oncology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"central denmark region","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CDR","l3nl":"Central Denmark Region","l3nn":"Central Denmark Region","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Central Denmark Region","namevar":["Aarhus University Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CDR","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"region of southern denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-SDR","l3nl":"Region of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"Region of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Region of Southern Denmark","namevar":["Odense University Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-SDR","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"dk","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["University of Southern Denmark (SDU)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Morten Horsholt","key":"kristensenpau","last":"Kristensen","locid":"4ca8e886-c5bf-4e4e-a015-3ed07ddffbcd","name":"Kristensen, Morten Horsholt","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Signe Bergliot","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"19df8d18-bee6-42d8-9d29-93c30adf1377","name":"Nielsen, Signe Bergliot","orcid":"0000-0002-3004-3103","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jan","key":"alsnerpau","last":"Alsner","locid":"ce189551-3897-40a2-8c51-2d722b6cbf97","name":"Alsner, Jan","orcid":"0000-0002-5395-3193","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Anne Ivalu Sander","key":"holmpau","last":"Holm","locid":"f6da7f96-ee71-4d5c-8d04-2e5daeab064b","name":"Holm, Anne","name_org":"Holm, Anne Ivalu Sander","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3 4 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Christian Rønn","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","locid":"79d7883a-1c88-43fb-b429-23c9a9ee6996","name":"Hansen, Christian Rønn","orcid":"0000-0001-5716-6069","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jens","key":"overgaardpau","last":"Overgaard","locid":"1d7efc28-596b-4029-81e1-a61477fd32b0","name":"Overgaard, Jens","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jesper Grau","key":"eriksenpau","last":"Eriksen","locid":"8c1cfe12-4441-494c-a8b7-8abfbf7aa48c","name":"Eriksen, Jesper Grau","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40089161"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.radonc.2025.110838","issn":["01678140"],"journal":"Radiotherapy & Oncology","paperid":"110838","publisher":"Elsevier Ireland Ltd","status":"p","title":"Radiotherapy & Oncology","title_org":"Radiotherapy and Oncology","volume":"207"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105000619964"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-abb76d57-7204-4637-9f89-c48a7532d1ea","sdu-f93870ea-7775-439f-b744-f1df9e40e748"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"A systematic review and proportional meta-analysis of image-based pattern of loco-regional failure analyses outcomes in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-095.65-804189","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"ABSTRACT Introduction Chronic pain after total knee arthroplasty (TKA) affects approx. 15%?20% of patients undergoing surgery. It is largely unexplained why some patients develop chronic pain after TKA, but several factors, e.g., central pain mechanisms and psychosocial factors, have been suggested. This study assessed central pain mechanisms and psychosocial factors in patients with chronic pain after TKA, receiving neuromuscular exercise and pain neuroscience education (PNE) as treatment. Methods This is the secondary analysis of a randomised controlled trial. Sixty-nine patients with chronic pain at least one-year post-TKA, experiencing an average daily pain intensity of ≥?4 on a numerical rating scale, were recruited. Participants were randomised to receive either 12?weeks of group-based neuromuscular exercise and two sessions of group-based PNE or two sessions of group-based PNE alone. Outcomes measured included temporal summation of pain (TSP) and pressure pain thresholds at the index knee and forearm, conditioned pain modulation, PainDETECT scores, fear-avoidance beliefs and pain catastrophizing. Results The study found a significant between-group difference in change from baseline to 12?months for TSP at the index knee, favouring the neuromuscular exercise and PNE group (?1.45, 95% CI ?2.48 to ?0.42, p?=?0.006). This indicates less facilitated TSP in the neuromuscular exercise and PNE group after intervention. No significant between-group differences were observed for the remaining outcomes. Conclusion The results indicated that neuromuscular exercise combined with PNE significantly reduced TSP at the index knee, suggesting a reduction in central pain amplification. The clinical importance of this exploratory finding should be further investigated. Significance Statement This trial showed that neuromuscular exercises in combination with pain neuroscience education resulted on decreased pain sensitization, measured as temporal summation of pain, in patients with chronic pain after total knee arthroplasty. The clinical relevance of central amplification of pain remains unclear but point at improved pain processing, i.e., reduced pain amplification.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Larsen, Jesper Bie","Skou, Søren T","Laursen, Mogens Berg","Bruun, Niels Henrik","Madeleine, Pascal","Arendt-Nielsen, Lars"],"cuid":["S02-099.91-813491"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Larsen, Jesper Bie"],"keyword":["Aged","Arthroplasty Replacement Knee adverse effects","Catastrophization psychology","Chronic Pain psychology","Exercise Therapy methods","Female","Humans","Male","Middle Aged","Pain Management methods","Pain Measurement","Pain Postoperative psychology","Pain Threshold","Treatment Outcome"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-ZEA"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"pain mechanisms and psychosocial variables in patients with chronic pain after total knee arthroplasty secondary analysis from a randomised controlled trial","src":["aau","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/ejp.70064","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Funding: This work was supported by the Danish Rheumatism Association (R168-A5619), the Svend Andersen Foundation, and Lions Club Denmark. The funders had no role in designing or conducting the study, data analysis, interpretation of the data, or decision to submit the manuscript for publication. Centre for Neuroplasticity and Pain (CNAP) is supported by the Danish National Research Foundation (DNRF121). Centre for Mathematical Modelling of Knee Osteoarthritis (MathKOA) supported by the Novo Nordic Foundation (NNF21OC0065373). S.T.S. is supported by a programme grant from Region Zealand (Exercise First) and two grants from the European Union's Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme, one from the European Research Council (MOBILISE, grant agreement No. 801790) and the other under grant agreement No. 945377 (ESCAPE).","This work was supported by the Danish Rheumatism Association (R168-A5619), the Svend Andersen Foundation, and Lions Club Denmark. The funders had no role in designing or conducting the study, data analysis, interpretation of the data, or decision to submit the manuscript for publication. Centre for Neuroplasticity and Pain (CNAP) is supported by the Danish National Research Foundation (DNRF121). Centre for Mathematical Modelling of Knee Osteoarthritis (MathKOA) supported by the Novo Nordic Foundation (NNF21OC0065373). S.T.S. is supported by a programme grant from Region Zealand (Exercise First) and two grants from the European Union's Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme, one from the European Research Council (MOBILISE, grant agreement No. 801790) and the other under grant agreement No. 945377 (ESCAPE)."],"id":"S02-099.91-813491","identifier":[{"id":"105008756939","type":"scopus"},{"id":"40539733","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC12180387","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["aau-e88c4680-ef7b-4c60-bd5c-a6fcdd496326","sdu-0fb289e1-8ffc-4253-b5a3-a33a78b9d905"],"keyword":["Aged","Arthroplasty, Replacement, Knee/adverse effects","Catastrophization/psychology","Chronic Pain/psychology","Exercise Therapy/methods","Female","Humans","Male","Middle Aged","Pain Management/methods","Pain Measurement","Pain Threshold","Pain, Postoperative/psychology","Treatment Outcome"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-4DA5AF55C176BB7CB83604C054D4EE74"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://