678140"],"journal":"Radiotherapy & Oncology","paperid":"110950","publisher":"Elsevier Ireland Ltd","status":"p","title":"Radiotherapy & Oncology","title_org":"Radiotherapy and Oncology","volume":"209"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105007293153"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","North Denmark Region","Region Zealand","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-adedee9b-2c3e-4870-a032-db7034060a9a","au-c49b0bb9-bf6b-41c9-8754-dc5650c397d3","sdu-ef230b31-a073-49ce-9c99-e05221a64543"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","AU Aarhus University","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Recommendations for radiotherapy quality assurance in clinical trials","type":"Journal Review","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-097.83-822429","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Background and purpose: Radiotherapy improves outcomes for breast cancer. However, prior studies have correlated the risk of coronary artery disease (CAD) to the mean heart dose (MHD), mean dose to the left anterior descending artery (LAD_mean) and the left ventricle V5Gy (LV5). Other studies showed an increased risk of CAD for patients with pronounced coronary artery calcification (CAC) at the time of radiotherapy. Materials and methods: This cohort study included 3355 high-risk breast cancer patients treated in Western Denmark (2008–2016). We analysed CT scans, treatment plans, and dose distributions. CAC was measured using the Agatston score (AS). We examined the dose–response relationship between MHD, LV5 and LAD_mean and CAD, and the effect of CAC presence at radiotherapy. Secondary analysis assessed overall survival. Results: Of 3355 patients, 45 (1.2 %) developed CAD during follow-up. AS was a strong predictor of CAD risk with a hazard ratio of 9.51(CI95:5.16–17.53) for AS ≥ 100 versus AS < 100 and a 6.7 % difference in absolute cumulative CAD risk at ten years (7.7 % vs 1 %). For AS < 100 (97 % of patients) CAD risk increased with MHD, hazard ratio 1.25 (CI95:1.01–1.56) per Gy. ForAS ≥ 100, CAD risk was driven by CAC rather than radiation dose. CAC was associated with poorer overall survival. Median MHD for the whole cohort was 1.25 Gy (IQR:1.01–1.56). Conclusion: AS from planning CT-scans predicted CAD risk and overall survival in breast cancer patients receiving radiotherapy. The MHD remained the strongest predictor in patients with low CAC. For patients with high CAC, the high baseline risk from CAC was a stronger risk factor than the dose-related risk.","author_count":13,"contributors":["Refsgaard, Lasse","Milo, Marie Louise Holm","Buhl, Emma Skarsø","Jensen, Jesper Møller","Maae, Else","Berg, Martin","Jensen, Ingelise","Nielsen, Mette Holck","Lorenzen, Ebbe Laugaard","Thorsen, Lise Bech Jellesmark","Korreman, Stine Sofia","Offersen, Birgitte Vrou"],"cuid":["S03-100.00-792962"],"filters":{"added":"2025-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"corresponding_author":["Refsgaard, Lasse"],"keyword":["Agatston score","Aged","Breast Neoplasms radiotherapy","Breast cancer","Cardiac toxicity","Cohort Studies","Coronary Artery Disease etiology","Coronary artery calcification","Coronary artery disease","Denmark","Dose response relationship","Female","Humans","Mean heart dose","Middle Aged","Radiotherapy","Radiotherapy Dosage","Risk Factors","Survival Analysis","Tomography X Ray Computed","Vascular Calcification etiology"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the effect of coronary artery calcifications and radiotherapy on the risk of coronary artery disease in high risk breast cancer patients in the dbcg rt nation cohort","src":["aau","au","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.radonc.2024.110705","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["The work was supported by: DCCC Radiotherapy - The Danish National Research Center for Radiotherapy, Danish Cancer Society (grant no. R191-A11526), Danish Cancer Society (grant no. R204-A12389) and Department of Clinical Medicine, Faculty of Health, Aarhus University."],"id":"S03-100.00-792962","identifier":[{"id":"39725067","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85214306106","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-95f83d7a-f4d1-4583-96a8-4b8d90bd62e3","au-de70e2ce-4d1c-4d6f-be0d-237ddf319c5e","sdu-11b22898-e366-4ac1-95db-aaeff518e14f"],"keyword":["Agatston score","Aged","Breast Neoplasms/radiothera