3-2658-8933","rank":14,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40793750"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/sms.70121","issn":["09057188","16000838"],"issn_org":["09057188"],"issue":"8","journal":"Scandinavian Journal of Medicine & Science in Sports","paperid":"e70121","publisher":"Wiley","status":"p","title":"Scandinavian Journal of Medicine & Science in Sports","title_org":"Scandinavian Journal of Medicine and Science in Sports","volume":"35"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105013192835"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Miscellaneous","All Private Research","All Universities","All University Colleges","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","DI-UC","DI-UC-ALL","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","Other Companies","Other Research","Private Research","Universities","University Colleges","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark","VIA University College","mental motion aps"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-16c45af1-08b5-49d4-ae53-1e1270500f57","au-ed76529e-e060-445b-bae3-6deded25a24e","ku-1df1acda-04a7-4e5f-99c6-0a2cb5e83a57","sdu-12210e75-270a-4733-8d65-c4a36841ea03"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"How to Promote Wellbeing in Youth Sport : Recommendations From the 2024 Copenhagen Consensus Conference","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S04-098.69-822247","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Objectives We explored whether a spike in running distance during a single session or over 1 week, compared with the preceding period, was associated with increased risk of running-related overuse injury. Methods Adult runners were recruited for an 18-month cohort study. Three training-related exposures were defined based on a relative change in running distance, using data collected via Garmin devices: (1) session-specific running distance relative to the longest distance run in the past 30 days; (2) 1-week period relative to the preceding 3 weeks using the acute:chronic workload ratio (ACWR); (3) 1-week period using a week-to-week ratio. Runners were categorised into one of four time-varying states: (1) regression, or up to 10% increase (reference); (2)’small spike’ between >10% and 30% increase; (3)’moderate spike’ between >30% and 100% increase; and (4)’large spike’ >100% increase. Outcome was self-reported overuse running-related injury. A multistate Cox regression model was used to estimate adjusted hazard rate ratios (HRR). Results Among 5205 runners (mean age 45.8 years, SD=10.4; 22% female), a total of 1820 (35%) sustained a running-related injury during 588 071 sessions. Significantly increased rates were identified for small spikes (HRR=1.64 (95% CI: 1.31 to 2.05, p=0.01)), moderate spikes (HRR=1.52 (95% CI: 1.16 to 2.00, p<0.01)) and large spikes (HRR=2.28 (95% CI: 1.50 to 3.48, p<0.01)) in single-session running distance. A negative dose-response relationship was observed for the ACWR. No relationship was identified for the week-to-week ratio. Conclusion A significant increase in the rate of running-related overuse injury was found when the distance of a single running session exceeded 10% of the longest run undertaken in the last 30 days.","author_count":13,"contributors":["Frandsen, Jesper Schuster Brandt","Hulme, Adam","Parner, Erik Thorlund","Møller, Merete","Lindman, Ida","Abrahamson, Josefin","Simonsen, Nina Sjoerup","Jacobsen, Julie Sandell","Ramskov, Daniel","Skejø, Sebastian","Malisoux, Laurent","Bertelsen, Michael Lejbach","Nielsen, Rasmus Oestergaard"],"cuid":["S04-098.87-822772"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":4,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD","Oceania"],"corresponding_author":["Nielsen, Rasmus Oestergaard","Nielsen, Rasmus Østergaard"],"country":["Australia","Luxembourg","Norway","Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Luxembourg Institute of Health","NHMRC Centre for Clinical Research Excellence in Spinal Pain, Injury and Health, University of Queensland","Norwegian School of Sport Sciences","Region Västra Götaland","Sahlgrenska University Hospital","The University of Queensland","University of Gothenburg","University of Queensland","University of the Sunshine Coast"],"keyword":["Adult","Athletic Injurie","Athletic Injuries","Athletic Injuries epidemiology","Cohort Studies","Cumulative Trauma Disorders epidemiology","Female","Humans","Male","Middle Aged","Proportional Hazards Models","Risk Factors","Running","Running injuries","Sporting injuries","Sports medicine","Time Factors","health nutrition and quality of life"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum","med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U","DI-UC"],"level3":{"OT":["Forskningsenheden for Almen Praksis i Aarhus"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"],"UC":["DI-UC-ALL","DI-UC-UCN","DI-UC-VIA"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU","DI-UC-UCN","DI-UC-VIA"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"how much running is too much identifying high risk running sessions in a 000000005200 person cohort study","src":["au","sdu","ucn","via"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU","DI-UC-UCN","DI-UC-VIA"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1136/bjsports-2024-109380","version":"Published Version"},"funding_