","name":"Nielsen, Jens Cosedis","name_org":"Nielsen, Jens C","orcid":"0000-0001-9414-1653","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5 6","corresponding":null,"first":"Anna M.","key":"thøgersenpau","last":"Thøgersen","locid":"040c49e1-e9f1-4e74-8cd0-4a9ef9fb7006","name":"Thøgersen, Anna Margrethe","name_org":"Thøgersen, Anna M.","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jens","key":"haarbopau","last":"Haarbo","name":"Haarbo, Jens","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"7 8","corresponding":null,"first":"Lars","key":"videbækpau","last":"Videbæk","locid":"c0abdf18-4e26-411a-99c3-de3cbe029dee","name":"Videbæk, Lars","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Finn","key":"gustafssonpau","last":"Gustafsson","name":"Gustafsson, Finn","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Jesper Hastrup","key":"svendsenpau","last":"Svendsen","locid":"d80910cf-2f9c-457c-9e85-34b4b258bb81","name":"Svendsen, Jesper Hastrup","orcid":"0000-0001-8466-8515","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Steen","key":"pehrsonpau","last":"Pehrson","name":"Pehrson, Steen","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Lars","key":"køberpau","last":"Køber","locid":"e3f0c57c-605a-431a-9c63-2213c46621e6","name":"Køber, Lars","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38168556"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.ijcard.2023.131700","issn":["01675273","18741754"],"issn_org":["01675273"],"journal":"International Journal of Cardiology","paperid":"131700","publisher":"Elsevier Ireland Ltd","status":"p","title":"International Journal of Cardiology","volume":"399"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85182424840"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","North Denmark Region","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-450761af-0b9d-45fc-a3d5-5bb29446a492","au-31a61a0d-4ba4-4938-81bb-2222f3dc40c9","ku-2c0ac009-0bfe-445e-bde8-06e85496b056","sdu-2cf72995-419c-4f34-b778-4940e610d31d"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Association between QRS shortening and mortality after cardiac resynchronization therapy : Results from the DANISH study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S04-100.00-724116","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Objectives: To investigate the short- and long-term risks of atrioventricular block and other cardiac conduction disorders associated with being tested for Borrelia burgdorferi (Bb) antibodies or Bb seropositivity as measures of confounding by indication and Bb infection, respectively. Methods: We performed a nationwide population-based matched cohort study (Denmark, 1993–2021). We included 52 200 Bb-seropositive individuals (stratified as only Bb-IgM-seropositive [n = 26 103], only Bb-IgG-seropositive [n = 18 698], and Bb-IgM-and-IgG-seropositive [n = 7399]) and two age- and sex-matched comparison cohorts: 104 400 Bb-seronegative individuals and 261 000 population controls. We investigated the risk associated with being tested for serum Bb antibodies and being Bb seropositive. Outcomes were atrioventricular block and other conduction disorders. We calculated short-term odds ratios (aOR) (within 1 month), and long-term hazard ratios (aHR) (after 1 month) adjusted for age, sex, diabetes, chronic heart failure, and kidney disease with 95% CI. Results: Compared with population controls, individuals tested for Bb antibodies had increased short- and long-term risks of atrioventricular block (aOR 47.9, 95% CI: 30.0–76.7, aHR 1.3, 95% CI:1.2–1.3), and other conduction disorders (aOR 18.2, 95% CI: 10.1–32.8, aHR 1.2, 95% CI: 1.1–1.4). Compared with Bb-seronegative individuals, only Bb-IgM-and-IgG-seropositive individuals had increased short-term risk of atrioventricular block (aOR: 2.1, 95% CI: 1.5–3.1). Discussion: The results suggest that Bb antibody testing is included in the diagnostic work-up of conduction disorders. Finally, that Bb seropositivity is not associated with other conduction disorders than atrioventricular block or with increased long-term risk of conduction disorders.","author_count":16,"contributors":["Tetens, Malte M","Omland, Lars Haukali","Dessau, Ram","Ellermann-Eriksen, Svend","Andersen, Nanna S","Jørgensen, Charlotte Sværke","Østergaar