,"pub":{"doi":"10.1136/bmjopen-2023-081961","issn":["20446055"],"issue":"2","journal":"BMJ Open","paperid":"e081961","publisher":"BMJ Publishing Group","status":"p","title":"BMJ Open","volume":"14"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85186740220"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Non-Profit Organisations","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-NP","DI-NP-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Non-Profit Organisations","North Denmark Region","Other Hospitals","Region Zealand","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark","copenhagen university hospital - holbæk","danish hospital for rheumatic diseases"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-2152b6bf-8dd5-448a-91e3-f8d61a6e4d9a","au-4d65a017-aa9a-4052-9d5a-7bf4fadb9873","ku-54bc5d56-fc07-426c-bf57-672c23274c86","sdu-b701a909-e9f2-4068-b905-09c0e41b2cf5"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Protocol for a randomised controlled trial comparing warfarin with no oral anticoagulation in patients with atrial fibrillation on chronic dialysis: the Danish Warfarin-Dialysis (DANWARD) trial","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S04-099.51-724050","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Background: Hereditary anemias are a group of genetic diseases prevalent worldwide and pose a significant health burden on patients and societies. The clinical phenotype of hereditary anemias varies from compensated hemolysis to life-threatening anemia. They can be roughly categorized into three broad categories: hemoglobinopathies, membranopathies, and enzymopathies. Traditional therapeutic approaches like blood transfusions, iron chelation, and splenectomy are witnessing a paradigm shift with the advent of targeted treatments. However, access to these treatments remains limited due to lacking or imprecise diagnoses. The primary objective of the study is to establish accurate diagnoses for patients with hereditary anemias, enabling optimal management. As a secondary objective, the study aims to enhance our diagnostic capabilities. Results: The DAHEAN study is a nationwide cohort study that collects advanced phenotypic and genotypic data from patients suspected of having hereditary anemias from all pediatric and hematological departments in Denmark. The study deliberates monthly by a multidisciplinary anemia board involving experts from across Denmark. So far, fifty-seven patients have been thoroughly evaluated, and several have been given diagnoses not before seen in Denmark. Conclusions: The DAHEAN study and infrastructure harness recent advancements in diagnostic tools to offer precise diagnoses and improved management strategies for patients with hereditary anemias.","author_count":24,"contributors":["Glenthøj, Andreas","Rasmussen, Andreas Ørslev","Bendtsen, Selma Kofoed","Hasle, Henrik","Hoffmann, Marianne","Rieneck, Klaus","Dziegiel, Morten Hanefeld","Sjö, Lene Dissing","Frederiksen, Henrik","Hansen, Dennis Lund","Fassi, Daniel El","Rathe, Mathias","Jensen, Peter Diedrich Matthias","Winther-Larsen, Anne","Nielsen, Christian","Olsen, Marianne","Toft, Nina","Lorenzen, Mads Okkels Birk","Jensen, Lise Heilmann","Gudbrandsdottir, Sif","Helby, Jens","Rossing, Maria","van Wijk, Richard","Petersen, Jesper"],"cuid":["S04-100.00-755897"],"filters":{"added":"2024-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":4,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Glenthøj, Andreas"],"country":["Netherlands"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","UMC Utrecht Cancer Center","University Medical Center Utrecht","Utrecht University"],"keyword":["Anemia diagnosis","Child","Cohort Studies","Denmark","Enzymopathies","Female","Hemoglobinopathies","Hereditary anemia","Humans","Male","Membranopathies","Precision diagnostics","Quality Assurance Health Care","Whole genome sequencing"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-HO-ZEA"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"dahean a danish nationwide study ensuring quality assurance through real world data for suspected hereditary anemia patients","src":["aau","au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13023-024-03298-4","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This project is carried out within the framework of European Reference Network on Rare Haematological Diseases (ERN-EuroBloodNet)-Project ID No 101085717. ERN-EuroBloodNet is partly co-funded by the European Union within the framework of the Fourth EU Health Programme."],"id":"S04-100.00-755897","identifier":[{"id":"85200050588","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC11290079","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"39085840","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["aau-bc12a0af-2898-42f1-bd92-a619f2c19806","au-91e45ff