key":"petersenpau","last":"Petersen","locid":"54a9cf35-3f97-4623-9ae0-6b1f2ecfa1d2","name":"Petersen, Martin","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["00305448"],"issue":"4","journal":"Orientations","pages":"24-34","status":"p","title":"Orientations","volume":"55"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","Governmental Institutions"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["kum-6b2ddab8-1695-45b9-830d-f70bd69297d4"],"src":["KUM Ministry of Culture - Archives, Museums, and Royal Library Denmark"],"title":"Asian Collections at the National Museum of Denmark : From the Kunstkammer to Comics","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-779705","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Failing to comprehend risk communication might contribute to poor treatment adherence. Using hypertension as a case, we investigated how a risk communication tool for patients with an elevated risk of cardiovascular disease was perceived. METHODS: As part of a large project featuring a randomised controlled trial in a general practice setting in the Region of Southern Denmark, we conducted a semi-structured individual interview study. The study included patients with hypertension who had used an intervention comprising a visual and dynamic cardiovascular risk communication tool, along with receiving recurring emails providing advice on a healthy lifestyle. The analyses were based on Malterud's Systematic Text Condensation. RESULTS: This article focuses solely on the results of the interview study, which comprised a total of 9 conducted and analysed interviews. The IT setup had a major impact on adherence to the intervention. A positive impact was found when the IT setup was perceived as easy to use and accessible, while a negative impact was noted when it malfunctioned. The intervention increased patients' self-reported insight into risk of cardiovascular disease. Patients reported the intervention and their risk of cardiovascular disease to become less important to them when they had more severe comorbidities. The involved health professional was very important for treatment adherence when communicating risk visually. Patients expressed trust in their general practitioners, and the general practitioners' attitudes toward the intervention affected patients' perceptions of its usefulness. While the informants reported an increased awareness of their risk of cardiovascular disease, none of them felt more concerned. CONCLUSIONS: Patients reported an increase in their perceived insight into the risk of cardiovascular disease but not an increased concern. Our findings align with previous studies emphasizing the importance of patients' motivation as well as risk perception for adherence. General practitioners have an important role when implementing new tools for patients.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Jensen, Anders Elkær","Søndergaard, Jens","Kjær, Niels Kristian","Nielsen, Jesper Bo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-782908"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Jensen, Anders Elkær"],"keyword":["Cardiovascular Diseases epidemiology","Cardiovascular disease","Communication","Family medicine","General Practitioners","General practice","Humans","Hypertension","Primary sector","Qualitative","Risk communication","Visual communication"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"patients views on usefulness and effects of a risk communication tool for cardiovascular disease a qualitative analysis","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12875-024-02279-7","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-782908","identifier":[{"id":"38310258","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85184107380","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC10837932","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["sdu-2c2af331-22b6-4430-9d4c-8cd27de244f8"],"keyword":["Cardiovascular Diseases/epidemiology","Cardiovascular disease","Communication","Family medicine","General Practice","General Practitioners","Humans","Hypertension","Patient interview","Primary sector","Qualitative","Risk communication","Visual communication"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-31D701253883B2A6CB7EA31C7DF4C8D4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/278590518/s12875-024-02279-7.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12875-024-02279-7","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Public Health","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Public Health, Research Unit of General Pratice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders Elkær","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","locid":"c03a32e8-161e-45b7-82ff-bd93d460de3c","name":"Jensen, Anders Elkær","orcid":"0000-0002-9056-9326","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jens","key":"søndergaardpau","last":"Søndergaard","locid":"4829db0c-8486-4ad1-8e6b-d4f3dc56c386","name":"Søndergaard, Jens","orcid":"0000-0002-1629-1864","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Niels Kristian","key":"kjærpau","last":"Kjær","locid":"8fa36491-a268-47f4-88ce-05581af1f852","name":"Kjær, Niels Kristian","orcid":"0000-0002-1301-2663","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jesper Bo","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"75938cc2-43d6-4c8d-a506-6b1c0472a382","name":"Nielsen, Jesper Bo","orcid":"0000-0002-5764-5462","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38310258"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1186/s12875-024-02279-7","issn":["27314553"],"journal":"BMC Primary Care","paperid":"50","publisher":"BioMed Central","status":"p","title":"BMC Primary Care","volume":"25"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85184107380"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-2c2af331-22b6-4430-9d4c-8cd27de244f8"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Patients' views on usefulness and effects of a risk communication tool for cardiovascular disease : a qualitative analysis","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-782908","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Objectives: To investigate the associations between sensory impairments and the development of depressive symptoms across sex, age, and European regions, and to examine the mediating role of cognitive function, activities of daily living (ADL), and physical activity. Method: A cohort study including 56,847 Europeans aged 50+ participating in at least two waves of the Survey of Health, Ageing and Retirement in Europe (SHARE). Associations were analyzed using mixed effects logistic regression models considering several confounders. Results: Overall, 17.8% developed depressive symptoms. Compared to participants with good vision and hearing, those with vision impairment (VI) (odds ratio (OR) = 1.35, 95% confidence interval (CI) 1.27–1.44), hearing impairment (HI) OR = 1.32, 95% CI 1.21–1.43, and dual sensory impairment (DSI, i.e. VI and HI) (OR = 1.93, 95% CI 1.75–2.13) had increased odds of depressive symptoms. The associations were consistent across sex and European regions but became stronger with advancing age among men. Dose-response relationships were found for all associations. Mediation analyses revealed that preventing cognitive decline, ADL limitations, and physical inactivity would eliminate 15.0%, 11.5%, and 21.4% of the total effect for VI, HI, and DSI, respectively. Conclusion: Our findings emphasize the importance of preventing sensory impairments to avoid depressive symptoms.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Ahrenfeldt, Linda Juel","Möller, Sören","Nielsen, Dorthe Linding","Kjær, Niels Kristian","Søndergaard, Jens","Lykkegaard, Jesper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-783172"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Ahrenfeldt, Linda Juel"],"keyword":["Activities of Daily Living","Aged","Aged 80 and over","Cohort Studies","Depression","Depression epidemiology","Dual sensory impairment","Europe epidemiology","Exercise","Female","Hearing","Hearing Loss epidemiology","Humans","Male","Mediation","Middle Aged","SHARE","Sex Factors","Vision","Vision Disorders epidemiology"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"sensory impairments and depressive symptoms in europe a cross national cohort study","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/13607863.2024.2345790","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-783172","identifier":[{"id":"38695383","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85191793741","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-5efa4bb6-d5e0-440c-a8b8-9f2f611aac73"],"keyword":["Activities of Daily Living","Aged","Aged, 80 and over","Cohort Studies","Depression/epidemiology","Europe/epidemiology","Exercise","Female","Hearing Loss/epidemiology","Humans","Male","Middle Aged","SHARE","Sex Factors","Vision","Vision Disorders/epidemiology","depression","dual sensory impairment","hearing","mediation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-54C0F4A56D90BC10F7122A1510C199E0"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/13607863.2024.2345790","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/278605068/Sensory_impairments_and_depressive_symptoms_in_Europe_a_cross-national_cohort_study.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Public Health","SDU, The Faculty of Health