cial journal of the European Society for Medical Oncology","volume":"35"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85198075845"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","North Denmark Region","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-d15efe01-524a-435a-bed0-d75f4dd087d2","au-db3b7c01-8a62-4c09-b50f-8a213cb1505a"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Upfront resection versus no resection of the primary tumor in patients with synchronous metastatic colorectal cancer: the randomized phase III CAIRO4 study conducted by the Dutch Colorectal Cancer Group and the Danish Colorectal Cancer Group","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-090.97-755916","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: In older adults, obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) has been associated with several cardiovascular complications. Whether young patients diagnosed with OSA also are at higher risk of developing subsequent cardiovascular disease is uncertain. We aimed to estimate the risk of developing an incident cardiovascular event among young patients diagnosed with OSA. METHODS AND RESULTS: We linked nationwide Danish health registries to identify a cohort of patients aged ≤50â€‰years with OSA using data from 2010 through 2018. Cases without OSA from the general population were matched as controls (1:5). The main outcome was any cardiovascular event (including hypertension, diabetes, atrial fibrillation, ischemic heart disease, ischemic stroke, heart failure, and venous thromboembolism). All-cause mortality was a secondary outcome. The study included 20â€‰240 patients aged ≤50 years with OSA (19.6% female; mean±SD age 39.9±7.7â€‰years) and 80â€‰314 controls. After 5-year follow-up, 31.8% of the patients with OSA developed any cardiovascular event compared with 16.5% of the controls, with a corresponding relative risk (RR) of 1.96 (95% CI, 1.90-2.02). At 5-year follow-up, 27.3% of patients with OSA developed incident hypertension compared with 15.0% of the controls (RR, 1.84 [95% CI, 1.78-1.90]). Incident diabetes occurred in 6.8% of the patients with OSA and 1.4% of controls (RR, 5.05 [95% CI, 4.60-5.54]). CONCLUSIONS: Similar to older adults, young adults with OSA demonstrate increased risk of developing cardiovascular events. To prevent cardiovascular disease progression, accumulation of cardiovascular risk factors, and mortality, risk stratification and prevention strategies should be considered for these patients.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Albertsen, Ida Ehlers","Bille, Jesper","Piazza, Gregory","Lip, Gregory Y H","Nielsen, Peter Brønnum"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-735078"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Albertsen, Ida Ehlers"],"country":["United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Brigham and Women’s Hospital","Harvard University","Liverpool Centre for Cardiovascular Science","Liverpool Heart and Chest Hospital NHS Foundation Trust","Liverpool John Moores University","University of Liverpool"],"keyword":["Aged","Cardiovascular Diseases epidemiology","Cardiovascular disease","Diabetes","Diabetes mellitus","Female","Heart Disease Risk Factors","Humans","Hypertension","Hypertension complications","Male","Obstructive sleep apnea","Risk Factors","Sleep Apnea Obstructive complications","Young Adult"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"cardiovascular risk in young patients diagnosed with obstructive sleep apnea","src":["aau","au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1161/JAHA.123.033506","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-735078","identifier":[{"id":"PMC11262486","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"38563371","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85190900577","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-fe3d6bd5-895c-40ca-acab-cb496b0d8f90","au-728fb074-1b32-46ae-a198-2b9e4a87ed1a"],"keyword":["Aged","Cardiovascular Diseases/epidemiology","Diabetes Mellitus","Female","Heart Disease Risk Factors","Humans","Hypertension/complications","Male","Risk Factors","Sleep Apnea, Obstructive/complications","Young Adult","cardiovascular disease","diabetes","hypertension","obstructive sleep apnea"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Loca