{"abstract":"Digital holographic microscopy (DHM) is a rapidly developing imaging technique that allows for the reconstruction of three-dimensional images from holographic data. In this review paper, we present an overview of the state-of-the-art in on-axis DHM reconstruction methods. We classify these methods into three categories: direct, iterative, and machine learning based algorithms. In the direct category, we summarize methods that directly apply the holographic data to reconstruct the sample without any iterative optimization. These methods are fast and efficient, but they often suffer from noise and low contrast. The iterative category contains methods that use an iterative optimization process to improve the quality of the reconstructed image. This can lead to higher accuracy and noise reduction, but at the cost of increased computational complexity. Within the iterative category, we also discuss methods that assume sparsity in the reconstruction and other domains, which can further improve the convergence of the reconstruction algorithm. Finally, we describe machine learning methods for DHM reconstruction. These methods use deep neural networks trained on large datasets to learn the relationship between 2D holographic data and their reconstructed fields. Machine learning based methods can achieve similar results to the iterative methods at a fraction of the time, however, they require large amounts of computational resources and training data. We provide a summary of these methods in a table (Table B.1) and a map (Fig. 1), and unify the naming convention of all methods, allowing readers to easily compare and contrast the different approaches.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Madsen, Andreas Gejl","Panah, Mohammad Aryaee","Larsen, Peter emil","Nielsen, Frank","Glückstad, Jesper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-680050"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Madsen, Andreas Gejl"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Radiometer Medical"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"on axis digital holographic microscopy current trends and algorithms","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.optcom.2023.129458","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-680050","identifier":[{"id":"85151429585","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-121647cf-858a-42b7-9205-1f97724206c5"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-D8F1E468899E9FB6C2D378A51FA7D1CA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/225123113/Elsevier_Enhanced_Reader.pdfOn_axis_digital.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.optcom.2023.129458","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Engineering, Mads Clausen Institute (MCI)","SDU, The Faculty of Engineering, Mads Clausen Institute (MCI), SDU Centre for Photonics Engineering"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Radiometer Medical"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andreas Gejl","key":"madsenpau","last":"Madsen","locid":"d3ef9829-cd37-4f70-88e6-cc1f45be0dfe","name":"Madsen, Andreas Gejl","orcid":"0000-0002-6118-3176","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Mohammad Aryaee","key":"panahpau","last":"Panah","name":"Panah, Mohammad Aryaee","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Peter emil","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","name":"Larsen, Peter emil","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Frank","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Frank","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jesper","key":"glückstadpau","last":"Glückstad","locid":"33cb352b-2f1d-465b-ac7c-cc67b03d3850","name":"Glückstad, Jesper","orcid":"0000-0002-7442-1540","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.optcom.2023.129458","issn":["00304018","18730310"],"issn_org":["00304018"],"journal":"Optics Communications","paperid":"129458","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Optics Communications","volume":"537"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85151429585"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-121647cf-858a-42b7-9205-1f97724206c5"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"On-axis digital holographic microscopy : current trends and algorithms","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-680050","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Located at the interface of Europe and Asia and blessed with a favourable climate, Roman Bithynia was a prosperous province with a diverse economy and home to important cities, one of which was Dio’s hometown Nicaea at the eastern end of lake Ascanius. For generations the Cassii had ranked among the leading families of Nicaea. Yet Dio does not have a great deal to say about events in Bithynia and when he does, the action more often takes place in Nicomedia – the provincial capital – than in his native city. Even in the last books of the History, which deal with contemporary history, there is nothing to suggest that Dio drew on local knowledge or Bithynian informants to any significant extent; from his perspective as a member of the senatorial order, Rome was the best vantage point from which to observe and record current events. Dio’s focus is on the emperor(s), and when Bithynia appears in his History, it is almost invariably due to the presence of an emperor or a pretender to the imperial throne. While Dio’s narrative of the battle of Nicaea (AD 193) reveals his first-hand knowledge of the region’s topography, it contains little local colour or local history.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Bekker-Nielsen, Tønnes"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-680201"],"filters":{"added":"2023-07","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Bekker-Nielsen, Tønnes"],"keyword":["Bithynien","Cassius Dio","Historiografi","Romerriget"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"cassius dio s bithynian background","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85164924591","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-680201","identifier":[{"id":"85164924591","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-3c2a6a3b-a4e9-4fc7-a0f6-d350430ad2ef"],"keyword":["Bithynien","Cassius Dio","Romerriget","historiografi"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Local-1-1-35D5468D506C505237CA9A558E049132"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, Faculty of Humanities, Department of Culture and Language","SDU, Faculty of Humanities, Department of History, Classical Studies"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Tønnes","key":"bekkernielsenpau","last":"Bekker-Nielsen","locid":"0b1d0a8f-6ba2-466c-8302-ff66ddefb4c9","name":"Bekker-Nielsen, Tønnes","orcid":"0000-0003-4628-5411","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Jesper Majbom","key":"madsenpedh","last":"Madsen","name":"Madsen, Jesper Majbom","rank":2,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Andrew G.","key":"scottpedh","last":"Scott","name":"Scott, Andrew G.","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1163/9789004524187_003","eisbn":["9789004524187"],"isbn":["9789004524170"],"issn":["18723357"],"pages":"21-47","place":"Leiden","publisher":"Brill","series":"Brill's Companions to Classical Studies","status":"p","title":"Brill¹s Companion to Cassius Dio"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-3c2a6a3b-a4e9-4fc7-a0f6-d350430ad2ef"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Cassius Dio’s Bithynian Background","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-680201","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Rationale: Dopaminergic dysfunction is implicated in disorders of impulsivity and inattention. The rodent continuous performance test (rCPT) has been used to quantify changes in attention and impulsivity. Objective: To examine the roles of dopamine receptors in attention and impulsivity behaviours measured in the rCPT variable stimulus duration (vSD) and the variable intertrial interval schedules (vITI) using DA receptor antagonists. Methods: Two cohorts of 35 and 36 female C57BL/6JRj mice were examined separately in the rCPT, vSD, and vITI schedules, respectively. Both cohorts received antagonists of the following receptors: D1/5 (SCH23390, SCH: 0.01, 0.02, 0.04 mg/kg) and D2/3 (raclopride, RAC 0.03, 0.10, 0.30 mg/kg) in consecutive balanced Latin square designs with flanking reference measurements. The antagonists were subsequently examined for effects on locomotor activity. Results: SCH showed similar effects in both schedules, and the effects were reference-dependent in the vITI schedule. SCH reduced responding, but improved response accuracy, impulsivity, discriminability, and locomotor activity. RAC showed mixed effects on responsivity, but improved accuracy and discriminability. The discriminability improvement was driven by an increase in hit rate in the vITI schedule and a reduction in false alarm rate in the vSD schedule. RAC also decreased locomotor activity. Conclusion: Both D1/5 and D2/3 receptor antagonism reduced responding, but the outcome on discriminability differed, stemming from individual effects on hit and false alarm rate, and the weight of omissions within the calculation. The effects of SCH and RAC suggest that endogenous DA increases responding and impulsivity, but reduces accuracy and shows mixed effects on discriminability.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Klem, L.","Nielsen, M. M.","Gestsdóttir, S. B.","Frandsen, S. L.","Prichardt, S.","Andreasen T., Jesper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-684622"],"filters":{"added":"2023-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Antagonist","Arousal","Attention","Dopamine","Impulsivity","Rodent continuous performance test"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"assessing attention and impulsivity in the variable stimulus duration and variable intertrial interval rodent continuous performance test schedules using dopamine receptor antagonists in female c000000000057bl 000000000006jrj mice","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00213-023-06387-7","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-684622","identifier":[{"id":"37378887","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85162017602","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC10349733","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-e758e7e2-7129-450e-96db-386a50873fad"],"keyword":["Antagonist","Arousal","Attention","Dopamine","Impulsivity","Rodent continuous performance test"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-4-8-B7BE3528F2076541E19B3DCF4734881B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/360027441/s00213_023_06387_7.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00213-023-06387-7","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Drug Design and Pharmacology, Translational and Clinical Pharmacology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"