,"DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Non-Profit Organisations","Region of Southern Denmark","Roskilde University","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-d3c07942-6a51-4970-8cef-c3905a48fab5","ruc-100b0271-51e0-438d-8925-e2d9e5565316","sdu-5a1e5507-9a18-418b-93b2-8c6e138025c8"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","RUC Roskilde University","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Transportation Noise and Risk of Tinnitus : A Nationwide Cohort Study from Denmark","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-099.35-028010","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Background: Cardiac resynchronization therapy (CRT) improves symptoms, health-related quality of life and long-term survival in patients with systolic heart failure (HF) and shortens QRS duration. However, up to one third of patients attain no measurable clinical benefit from CRT. An important determinant of clinical response is optimal choice in left ventricular (LV) pacing site. Observational data have shown that achieving an LV lead position at a site of late electrical activation is associated with better clinical and echocardiographic outcomes compared to standard placement, but mapping-guided LV lead placement towards the site of latest electrical activation has never been investigated in a randomized controlled trial (RCT). The purpose of this study was to evaluate the effect of targeted positioning of the LV lead towards the latest electrically activated area. We hypothesize that this strategy is superior to standard LV lead placement. Methods: The DANISH-CRT trial is a national, double-blinded RCT (ClinicalTrials.gov NCT03280862). A total of 1,000 patients referred for a de novo CRT implantation or an upgrade to CRT from right ventricular pacing will be randomized 1:1 to receive conventional LV lead positioning preferably in a nonapical posterolateral branch of the coronary sinus (CS) (control group) or targeted positioning of the LV lead to the CS branch with the latest local electrical LV activation (intervention group). In the intervention group, late activation will be determined using electrical mapping of the CS. The primary endpoint is a composite of death and nonplanned HF hospitalization. Patients are followed for a minimum of 2 years and until 264 primary endpoints occurred. Analyses will be conducted according to the intention-to-treat principle. Enrollment for this trial began in March 2018, and per April 2023, a total of 823 patients have been included. Enrollment is expected to be complete by mid-2024. Conclusions: The DANISH-CRT trial will clarify whether mapping-guided positioning of the LV lead according to the latest local electrical activation in the CS is beneficial for patients in terms of reducing the composite endpoint of death or nonplanned hospitalization for heart failure. Results from this trial are expected to impact future guidelines on CRT. ClinicalTrials.gov identifier: NCT03280862.","author_count":28,"contributors":["Kronborg, Mads Brix","Frausing, Maria Hee Jung Park","Svendsen, Jesper Hastrup","Johansen, Jens Brock","Riahi, Sam","Haarbo, Jens","Poulsen, Steen Hvitfeldt","Eiskjær, Hans","Køber, Lars","Øvrehus, Kristian","Sommer, Anders","Schou, Morten","Nørgaard, Bjarne Linde","Risum, Niels","Poulsen, Mikael Kjær","Søgaard, Peter","Sandgaard, Niels","Kofoed, Klaus F.","Hansen, Thomas Fritz","Graff, Claus","Pedersen, Susanne S","Skals, Regitze Kuhr","Nielsen, Jens Cosedis"],"cuid":["S04-099.85-697738"],"filters":{"added":"2023-07","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":4,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Kronborg, Mads Brix"],"country":["Czechia"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Institute for Clinical and Experimental Medicine"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U","DI-UC"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"UC":["DI-UC-ALL","DI-UC-KP"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU","DI-UC-KP"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"does targeted positioning of the left ventricular pacing lead towards the latest local electrical activation in cardiac resynchronization therapy reduce the incidence of death or hospitalization for heart failure","src":["aau","au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ahj.2023.05.011","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S04-099.85-697738","identifier":[{"id":"85161310255","type":"scopus"},{"id":"37220821","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["aau-7aabc235-9226-482b-a1bb-aca57ffa0280","au-16e6f135-ab26-442e-868f-68e02be687b4","ku-694b3fb3-e84c-41b3-b971-22cb595ebb3a","sdu-b6ff9ba3-885d-441d-b84b-89c02276f704"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-7-AFA6FE7549356E4F1F42994E7918E85A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/550316898/1-s2.0-S0002870323001291-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/384474586/1-s2.0-S0002870323001291-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/369551392/1_s2.0_S0002870323001291_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/243897399/Does_targeted_positioning_of_the_left_ventricular_pacing_lead_towards_the_latest_local_electrical_activation_in_cardiac_resynchronization_therapy_reduce_the_incidence_of_death_or_hospitalization_for_heart_failure.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ahj.2023.05.011","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - The Department of Cardiological Medicine B"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n"