{"abstract":"In a crowded metro network, it is not unusual to observe that passengers actively choose not to board but wait for the next train for a seat, even if there is vacant standing room on the arriving train. We analyse such behaviour using a logit-based choice model based on revealed preference data collected from the smart card records and the operational timetables. The choice model considers waiting time, fluctuating crowding levels, and passengers’ expected seat availability at each station on their trip. The revealed preference data are collected based on an existing time component framework, which can estimate passengers’ itineraries by dividing passengers’ travel time into time components (i.e. access, egress, boarding delay, and transfer-walking times) and analysing their uncertainty. We improve the time component framework by developing methods for estimating distributions corresponding to each time component. Using Chengdu Metro as a case, we find that the extra waiting time resulting from active boarding delay and standing time is valued 50.5% more positively and 25.3% more negatively than the in-vehicle sitting time, respectively. By comparing our findings with studies focused on passive boarding delays caused by fully loaded trains, we suggest that extra waiting time due to active and passive boarding delays should be explicitly distinguished in practice. The estimation results of the distributions for the time components indicate that access and egress walking times follow different distributions at given stations, as opposed to the assumption in most prior studies.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Chen, Xin","Jiang, Yu","Ingvardson, Jesper Bláfoss","Luo, Xia","Nielsen, Otto Anker"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-700919"],"filters":{"added":"2023-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East"],"corresponding_author":["Jiang, Yu"],"country":["China"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Southwest Jiaotong University"],"keyword":["Crowding","Public transport","Revealed preference"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"i can board but i d rather wait active boarding delay choice behaviour analysis using smart card data in metro systems","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tra.2023.103747","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This study is supported by the Chinese Scholarship Council and supported in part by the Chunhui Cooperative Research Project of the Ministry of Education of P. R. China (No. CH2019lt)."],"id":"S01-000.00-700919","identifier":[{"id":"85163422146","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-70e8580c-b86f-45de-962a-df408a939d6c"],"keyword":["Active boarding delay","Crowding","Itinerary inference","Public transport","Revealed preference","Smart card records"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-09171A64E3E711BA197E3E467202BC35"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/340605994/1-s2.0-S0965856423001672-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tra.2023.103747","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Technology, Management and Economics","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Technology, Management and Economics, Transportation Science"