{"abstract":"Cable bacteria are centimeter-long filamentous bacteria that conduct electrons via internal wires, thus coupling sulfide oxidation in deeper, anoxic sediment with oxygen reduction in surface sediment. This activity induces geochemical changes in the sediment, and other bacterial groups appear to benefit from the electrical connection to oxygen. Here, we report that diverse bacteria swim in a tight flock around the anoxic part of oxygen-respiring cable bacteria and disperse immediately when the connection to oxygen is disrupted (by cutting the cable bacteria with a laser). Raman microscopy shows that flocking bacteria are more oxidized when closer to the cable bacteria, but physical contact seems to be rare and brief, which suggests potential transfer of electrons via unidentified soluble intermediates. Metagenomic analysis indicates that most of the flocking bacteria appear to be aerobes, including organotrophs, sulfide oxidizers, and possibly iron oxidizers, which might transfer electrons to cable bacteria for respiration. The association and close interaction with such diverse partners might explain how oxygen via cable bacteria can affect microbial communities and processes far into anoxic environments.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Bjerg, Jesper Jensen","Lustermans, Jamie","Marshall, Ian P.G.","Mueller, Anna J.","Brokjær, Signe","Thorup, Casper","Tataru, Paula","Schmid, Markus","Wagner, Michael","Nielsen, Lars Peter","Schramm, Andreas"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-734820"],"filters":{"added":"2023-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Bjerg, Jesper J.","Bjerg, Jesper Jensen","Schramm, Andreas"],"country":["Austria","Belgium"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Antwerp","University of Vienna"],"keyword":["Bacteria genetics","Deltaproteobacteria","Geologic Sediments microbiology","Oxidation Reduction","Oxygen","Sulfides"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"cable bacteria with electric connection to oxygen attract flocks of diverse bacteria","src":["aau","au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-37272-8","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-734820","identifier":[{"id":"36959175","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC10036481","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85150935498","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-062c32b6-0bf3-4057-a588-03660dde4b7d","au-826e8229-aff6-4475-98d1-f44040a61ad2"],"keyword":["Bacteria/genetics","Deltaproteobacteria","Geologic Sediments/microbiology","Oxidation-Reduction","Oxygen","Sulfides"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-39F50C6E92EB20D69425D90B110D6246"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-37272-8","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/706506345/s41467-023-37272-8.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Biology, Department of Biology - Center for Electromicrobiology","Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Biology, Department of Biology - Microbiology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"be","key":"university of antwerp","loc":["Belgium","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Antwerp","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"at","key":"university of vienna","loc":["Austria","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Vienna","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science, Department of Chemistry and Bioscience","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science, Department of Chemistry and Bioscience, Center for Microbial Communities","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science, Department of Chemistry and Bioscience, Section for Bioscience and Engineering"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jesper J.","key":"bjergpau","last":"Bjerg","locid":"682ae3d8-d830-4101-b5a3-494ea2e4e0be","name":"Bjerg, Jesper Jensen","name_org":"Bjerg, Jesper J.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jamie J.M.","key":"lustermanspau","last":"Lustermans","locid":"c00ba933-55b0-4ee6-b201-572b6d5a8039","name":"Lustermans, Jamie","name_org":"Lustermans, Jamie J.M.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Ian P.G.","key":"marshallpau","last":"Marshall","locid":"25504f0f-4132-4611-8697-0019cedc5d5d","name":"Marshall, Ian P.G.","orcid":"0000-0001-9264-4687","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Anna J.","key":"muellerpau","last":"Mueller","name":"Mueller, Anna J.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Signe","key":"brokjærpau","last":"Brokjær","locid":"2c041bf0-72b0-4425-a1f1-b2a393a07e79","name":"Brokjær, Signe","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Casper A.","key":"thoruppau","last":"Thorup","locid":"d17273fd-b218-47b1-847d-fef2afa67ddf","name":"Thorup, Casper","name_org":"Thorup, Casper A.","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Paula","key":"tatarupau","last":"Tataru","locid":"4969ba5c-0416-4aac-aa18-2c348ef6819c","name":"Tataru, Paula","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Markus","key":"schmidpau","last":"Schmid","name":"Schmid, Markus","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"wagnerpau","last":"Wagner","locid":"6d340334-6137-4749-8a94-1fc3240cfa00","name":"Wagner, Michael","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lars Peter","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"7b1f70e1-663b-4a12-b10c-a12dfc320c81","name":"Nielsen, Lars Peter","orcid":"0000-0002-7269-4860","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Andreas","key":"schrammpau","last":"Schramm","locid":"25080de3-b60e-46b4-a606-d99f1ec089e5","name":"Schramm, Andreas","orcid":"0000-0002-7614-9616","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["36959175"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41467-023-37272-8","issn":["20411723"],"issue":"1","journal":"Nature Communications","paperid":"1614","publisher":"Nature Research","status":"p","title":"Nature Communications","volume":"14"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85150935498"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-062c32b6-0bf3-4057-a588-03660dde4b7d","au-826e8229-aff6-4475-98d1-f44040a61ad2"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Cable bacteria with electric connection to oxygen attract flocks of diverse bacteria","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-734820","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Background: Outcome after colorectal liver metastases (CRLM) resection has improved over time, despite increased resection rates. Hence, it's crucial to identify all patients possible to treat with curative intent. The objectives of this study were to map recurrence pattern, treatment strategy and survival depending on treatment and follow-up strategy. Methods: In the COLOFOL-trial, patients with radically resected stage II-III colorectal cancer were randomized to high-frequency (6, 12, 18, 24 and 36 months; HF) or low-frequency (12 and 36 months; LF) follow-up. In this study, all CRLM within 5 years were identified and medical files scrutinized. Overall survival (OS) was analysed in uni- and multivariable analyses. Primary endpoint was 5-year OS. Results: Of 2442 patients, 235 (9.6%) developed metachronous CRLM of which 123 (52.3%) underwent treatment with curative intent, resulting in 5-year OS of 58%. Five-year OS for patients with CRLM was 43% after HF versus 24% after LF. The survival benefit was confirmed for HF 8 years from resection of the primary tumour, HR 0.63 (CI 0.46–0.85). Conclusion: A high proportion of metachronous CRLM was possible to treat with curative intent, yielding high survival rates. More intense follow-up after colorectal cancer resection might be of value in high-risk patients.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Scherman, Peter","Hansdotter, Pernilla","Holmberg, Erik","Viborg Mortensen, Frank","Petersen, Sune H.","Rizell, Magnus","Naredi, Peter","Syk, Ingvar","Nielsen, Jesper Dan"],"cuid":["S03-098.37-681210"],"filters":{"added":"2023-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corpau":["COLOFOL Study Group"],"corresponding_author":["Scherman, Peter"],"country":["Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Helsingborg Hospital","Lund University","Sahlgrenska University Hospital","Skåne University Hospital","University of Gothenburg"],"keyword":["Colorectal Neoplasms pathology","Follow Up Studies","Hepatectomy adverse effects","Humans","Liver Neoplasms surgery","Neoplasm Recurrence Local","Outcome Assessment Health Care","Retrospective Studies"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"high resection rates of colorectal liver metastases after standardized follow up and multimodal management an outcome study within the colofol trial","src":["aau","au","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.hpb.2023.03.003","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S03-098.37-681210","identifier":[{"id":"85150836501","type":"scopus"},{"id":"36967324","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["aau-2a13b7e5-8c2e-425b-ae3e-17c83b91c959","au-f5eb7a69-d164-4f0f-b0e6-910318e2c25b","sdu-8b2b57ad-5186-48d6-b48c-87576398255b"],"keyword":["Colorectal Neoplasms/pathology","Follow-Up Studies","Hepatectomy/adverse effects","Humans","Liver Neoplasms/surgery","Neoplasm Recurrence, Local","Outcome Assessment, Health Care","Retrospective Studies"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-123F4DFBD1A02C035DC0862306CE2030"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/237711142/1_s2.0_S1365182X23000758_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/539004144/1-s2.0-S1365182X23000758-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.hpb.2