Southern Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-c7aefa44-e11d-4afb-b8c6-9fa90768e062","au-20aa6b9c-4eba-4c13-96dd-324748706aee","ku-1a891cbf-09aa-4440-8213-2f7621a4094b","sdu-ba2d2fbe-2e85-470f-9e65-b8584e4122f7"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Diabetes and Implantable Cardioverter Defibrillator in Nonischemic Systolic Heart Failure: An Extended Follow-Up Analysis of DANISH","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S04-100.00-703832","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Inducing antiretroviral therapy (ART)-free virological control is a critical step toward a human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) cure. In this phase 2a, placebo-controlled, double-blinded trial, 43 people (85% males) with HIV-1 on ART were randomized to (1) placebo/placebo, (2) lefitolimod (TLR9 agonist)/placebo, (3) placebo/broadly neutralizing anti-HIV-1 antibodies (bNAbs) or (4) lefitolimod/bNAb. ART interruption (ATI) started at week 3. Lefitolimod was administered once weekly for the first 8 weeks, and bNAbs were administered twice, 1 d before and 3 weeks after ATI. The primary endpoint was time to loss of virologic control after ATI. The median delay in time to loss of virologic control compared to the placebo/placebo group was 0.5 weeks (P = 0.49), 12.5 weeks (P = 0.003) and 9.5 weeks (P = 0.004) in the lefitolimod/placebo, placebo/bNAb and lefitolimod/bNAb groups, respectively. Among secondary endpoints, viral doubling time was slower for bNAb groups compared to non-bNAb groups, and the interventions were overall safe. We observed no added benefit of lefitolimod. Despite subtherapeutic plasma bNAb levels, 36% (4/11) in the placebo/bNAb group compared to 0% (0/10) in the placebo/placebo group maintained virologic control after the 25-week ATI. Although immunotherapy with lefitolimod did not lead to ART-free HIV-1 control, bNAbs may be important components in future HIV-1 curative strategies. ClinicalTrials.gov identifier: NCT03837756 .","author_count":33,"contributors":["Gunst, Jesper D.","Højen, Jesper F.","Pahus, Marie H.","Rosás-Umbert, Miriam","Stiksrud, Birgitte","McMahon, James H.","Denton, Paul W.","Nielsen, Henrik","Johansen, Isik S.","Benfield, Thomas","Leth, Steffen","Gerstoft, Jan","Østergaard, Lars","Schleimann, Mariane H","Olesen, Rikke","Støvring, Henrik","Vibholm, Line","Weis, Nina","Dyrhol-Riise, Anne M.","Pedersen, Karen B. H.","Lau, Jillian S. Y.","Copertino, Dennis C.","Linden, Noemi","Huynh, Tan T.","Ramos, Victor","Jones, R. Brad","Lewin, Sharon R.","Tolstrup, Martin","Rasmussen, Thomas A.","Nussenzweig, Michel C.","Caskey, Marina","Reikvam, Dag Henrik","Søgaard, Ole S."],"cuid":["S04-100.00-715684"],"filters":{"added":"2023-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":4,"continent":["America, North","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD","Oceania"],"corresponding_author":["Søgaard, Ole S.","Søgaard, Ole Schmeltz"],"country":["Australia","Norway","United States"],"glorg":["Alfred Health","Alfred Hospital","All Global Affiliations","Cornell University","Oslo University Hospital","Rockefeller University","Royal Melbourne Hospital","The Alfred Hospital","The Peter Doherty Institute for Infection and Immunity","University of Melbourne","University of Nebraska Omaha","University of Nebraska at Omaha","University of Oslo","Weill Cornell Graduate School of Medical Sciences","Weill Cornell Medical College","Weill Cornell Medicine"],"keyword":["Adjuvants Immunologic","Antibodies Neutralizing","Broadly neutralizing antibodies","Female","HIV 1","HIV Infections","Humans","Male"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"impact of a tlr000000000009 agonist and broadly neutralizing antibodies on hiv 000000000001 persistence the randomized phase 000000000002a titan trial","src":["aau","au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41591-023-02547-6","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["We thank all study participants who devoted time to our research as well as every clinical research unit involved in the study. We acknowledge The Rockefeller University for providing 3BNC117 and 10-1074 and Mologen AG (now part of Gilead Sciences, Inc.) for providing lefitolimod. The funders were not involved in the study design/operations, data collection/analysis/interpretation or preparation of the manuscript. The study was funded through a Gilead HIV Cure grant to O.S.S. Study drugs were donated free of charge by The Rockefeller University (3BNC117 and 10–1074) and Mologen AG/Gilead Sciences, Inc. (lefitolimod) for use in this trial. J.D.G. is supported by the Lundbeck Foundation (R381–2021–1405). P.W.D. is supported by the National Institute of General Medical Sciences of the National Institutes of Health under award P20 GM103427. Partial support for virologic analysis was provided by the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases of the National Institutes of Health under award R01AI47845 (RBJ), supported by the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases, the National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases, the National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke, the National Institute on Drug Abuse and the National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute. S.R.L. is supported by funding from the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases of the National Institutes of Health under award UM1AI126611-01 and the National Health and Medical Research Council (NHMRC) of Australia (1135851 and 1149990). S.R.L. is an NHMRC Practitioner Fellow. O.S.S. is also supported by the Danish Council for Independent Research (grant 9060-00023B) and the Lundbeck Foundation (R313-2019-790). None of the specific sources of funding had any role in the conceptualization, design, data collection, analysis, decision to publish or preparation of the manuscript.","We thank all study participants who devoted time to our research as well as every clinical research unit involved in the study. We acknowledge The Rockefeller University for providing 3BNC117 and 10-1074 and Mologen AG (now part of Gilead Sciences, Inc.) for providing lefitolimod. The funders were not involved in the study design/operations, data collection/analysis/interpretation or preparation of the manuscript. The study was funded through a Gilead HIV Cure grant to O.S.S. Study drugs were donated free of charge by The Rockefeller University (3BNC117 and 10–1074) and Mologen AG/Gilead Sciences, Inc. (lefitolimod) for use in this trial. J.D.G. is supported by the Lundbeck Foundation (R381–2021–1405). P.W.D. is supported by the National Institute of General Medical Sciences of the National Institutes of Health under award P20 GM103427. Partial support for virologic analysis was provided by the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases of the National Institutes of Health under award R01AI47845 (RBJ), supported by the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases, the National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases, the National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke, the National Institute on Drug Abuse and the National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute. S.R.L. is supported by funding from the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases of the National Institutes of Health under award UM1AI126611-01 and the National Health and Medical Research Council (NHMRC) of Australia (1135851 and 1149990). S.R.L. is an NHMRC Practitioner Fellow. O.S.S. is also supported by the Danish Council for Independent Research (grant 9060-00023B) and the Lundbeck Foundation (R313-2019-790). None of the specific sources of funding had any role in the conceptualization, design, data collection, analysis, decision to publish or preparation of the manuscript. We thank all study participants who devoted time to our research as well as every clinical research unit involved in the study. We acknowledge The Rockefeller University for providing 3BNC117 and 10-1074 and Mologen AG (now part of Gilead Sciences, Inc.) for providing lefitolimod. The funders were not involved in the study design/operations, data collection/analysis/interpretation or preparation of the manuscript. The study was funded through a Gilead HIV Cure grant to O.S.S. Study drugs were donated free of charge by The Rockefeller University (3BNC117 and 10–1074) and Mologen AG/Gilead Sciences, Inc. (lefitolimod) for use in this trial. J.D.G. is supported by the Lundbeck Foundation (R381–2021–1405). P.W.D. is supported by the National Institute of General Medical Sciences of the National Institutes of Health under award P20 GM103427. Partial support for virologic analysis was provided by the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases of the National Institutes of Health under award R01AI47845 (RBJ), supported by the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases, the National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases, the National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke, the National Institute on Drug Abuse and the National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute. S.R.L. is supported by funding from the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases of the National Institutes of Health under award UM1AI126611-01 and the National Health and Medical Research Council (NHMRC) of Australia (1135851 and 1149990). S.R.L. is an NHMRC Practitioner Fellow. O.S.S. is also supported by the Danish Council for Independent Research (grant 9060-00023B) and the Lundbeck Foundation (R313-2019-790). None of the specific sources of funding had any role in the conceptualization, design, data collection, analysis, decision to publish or preparation of the manuscript."],"id":"S04-100.00-715684","identifier":[{"id":"PMC10579101","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85170556818","type":"scopus"},{"id":"37696935","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["aau-6ad91524-b672-49bf-9014-dbbc60094ffe","au-36275b18-109b-4217-a6fc-b06a25bf11c8","ku-9b764ba8-c4b8-4bab-8b84-751fea0ffc89","sdu-60122784-2e20-4387-bc8e-643b608821f5"],"keyword":["Adjuvants, Immunolo