{"abstract":"Public sector innovation has politically come more into focus as a tool to meet future challenges. However, there is a need to increase our knowledge on innovation activity in the public sector. In this chapter, we provide insights on innovation in Danish public workplaces by exploring recent survey data. We present evidence on the overall activity levels and types of innovation. We focus on the extent of collaborative innovation between workplaces and firms, citizens, research institutions, etc. Results show that 80% of all types of innovations involve some kind of collaboration, a level similar to that of other Nordic countries. External collaboration on innovation enhances knowledge; competences for problem solving, in turn increase productivity.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Stamhus, Jørgen","Nielsen, René Nesgaard"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-070491"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"collaboration as a cornerstone in public sector innovation the case of denmark","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"http://www.scopus.com/inward/record.url?scp=85104687062&partnerID=8YFLogxK","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-070491","identifier":[{"id":"85104687062","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-468a6f65-a295-4f4e-b2d5-91a836598268"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-C3EB0270288A3A5717972DFFD9937083"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Aalborg University Business School","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Aalborg University Business School, The IKE Research Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jørgen","key":"stamhuspau","last":"Stamhus","locid":"5ab50b64-e39d-421b-a6b4-cc7bd8e09fec","name":"Stamhus, Jørgen","orcid":"0000-0002-2319-929X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"René Nesgaard","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"74df64b0-7daa-471f-ad04-20b1253a0909","name":"Nielsen, René Nesgaard","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Jesper Lindgaard","key":"christensenpedh","last":"Christensen","name":"Christensen, Jesper Lindgaard","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Birgitte","key":"gregersenpedh","last":"Gregersen","name":"Gregersen, Birgitte","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Jacob Rubæk","key":"holmpedh","last":"Holm","name":"Holm, Jacob Rubæk","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Edward","key":"lorenzpedh","last":"Lorenz","name":"Lorenz, Edward","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.4324/9781003037750-10","edition":"1.","eisbn":["9781000368727"],"isbn":["9780367480479"],"pages":"130-145","place":"London","publisher":"Routledge","series":"Routledge Studies in Innovation, Organizations and Technology","status":"p","subtitle":"The Danish Innovation System in Transition","title":"Globalisation, new and emerging technologies, and sustainable development"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85104687062"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-468a6f65-a295-4f4e-b2d5-91a836598268"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Collaboration as a cornerstone in public sector innovation : The case of Denmark","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-070491","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Within the information systems research, there is a long tradition for longitudinal research, and it plays a significant role in the research literature. In this chapter, we will overview the reasons provided by researchers for when a longitudinal study is appropriate. Longitudinal studies have a particular focus on time and change. Time and change address a concern for understanding the details of human actors’ behaviour and perceptions both as individuals and in social arrangements. This addresses ‘how’ to conduct a longitudinal study and why a deeper level of understanding is beneficial. In this chapter, we will map longitudinal research in information systems from the last two decades. This mapping shows critical distinctions that can be used in designing longitudinal research. The most important difference in longitudinal studies is between variance studies and process studies. Variance studies set the research design before the data collection, treat the change over time as a black box, favour a positivist stance and ask what-questions to see how the input causes the output over time. Process studies have a research design that emerges gradually as the data collection and analysis moves forward, favours an interpretive stance and asks what happens within the process.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Nielsen, Peter Axel"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-078939"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Information systems research","Literature review","Longitudinal case study","Longitudinal research"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"longitudinal research in information systems","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-078939","ids":["aau-63798655-42ed-4b00-ba6b-e1dbfbd4dc0b"],"keyword":["Information systems research","Literature review","Longitudinal Research","Longitudinal case study","Process study","Variance study"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-2B776FD3FA9EEAAB34ABFA4A22F3CE02"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Computer Science","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Computer Science, Human-Centered Computing"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Peter Axel","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"d5302c36-a512-42d6-8ab5-32d3f7da115e","name":"Nielsen, Peter Axel","orcid":"0000-0002-0282-7445","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"E","key":"karapanospedh","last":"Karapanos","name":"Karapanos, E","rank":2,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"J","key":"gerkenpedh","last":"Gerken","name":"Gerken, J","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Jesper","key":"kjeldskovpedh","last":"Kjeldskov","name":"Kjeldskov, Jesper","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Mikael","key":"skovpedh","last":"Skov","name":"Skov, Mikael","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-030-67322-2_3","eisbn":["9783030673222"],"isbn":["9783030673215","9783030673246"],"issn":["15715035"],"pages":"41-56","publisher":"Springer","series":"Human-Computer Interaction","status":"p","title":"Advances in Longitudinal HCI Research"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-63798655-42ed-4b00-ba6b-e1dbfbd4dc0b"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Longitudinal research in information systems","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-078939","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Entrepreneurship is considered an important driver of any innovation system as entrepreneurs provide new goods and services to the market and spur innovation among incumbent businesses. The design of entrepreneurship policies has been debated extensively. In formulating policies, one of the major questions that emerges is whether policies should promote the entry of more entrepreneurs into the market, and if yes, which type of entry should be promoted, and how can it be implemented? On the one hand, excess entry can lead to a significant waste of resources because the majority of new entrepreneurs fail within a short period, and very few achieve the societal goal of high growth and job creation. On the other hand, focusing on promoting only attractive entrepreneurs, from society’s point of view, might be an impossible exercise. Recently a different rationale for entrepreneurship policy has been suggested, arguing that entrepreneurship is largely about experimentation, where ideas are carried from firm to firm by individuals, despite failures along the way. This chapter discusses these policy directions, reviews the recent literature and points to the future direction of entrepreneurship policy.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Nielsen, Kristian","Dahl, Michael S.","Timmermans, Bram","Christensen, Jesper Lindgaard"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-094515"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Norway"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Norwegian School of Economics"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"entrepreneurship experimentation and innovation future policy for innovative and growth oriented entrepreneurs in denmark","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"http://www.scopus.com/inward/record.url?scp=85104801234&partnerID=8YFLogxK","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-094515","identifier":[{"id":"85104801234","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-9953e0c5-c5c9-4ee5-ac70-2895c3f9e70b"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-24D0023FEE369FC3008AE4D1E5C5365D"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Aalborg University Business School","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Aalborg University Business School, IMPact Analyses of investments in Knowledge and Technology","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Aalborg University Business School, The IKE Research Group","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Politics and Society, ForskningsCenter for Evaluering"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"no","key":"norwegian school of economics","loc":["Norway","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Norwegian School of Economics","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Kristian","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"8212fdc4-91fa-4027-9f80-f9039303522d","name":"Nielsen, Kristian","orcid":"0000-0002-2790-4064","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Michael S.","key":"dahlpau","last":"Dahl","locid":"86f3b9e4-2c48-4e47-aa28-cba7e34742bb","name":"Dahl, Michael S.","orcid":"0000-0002-3970-1483","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Bram","key":"timmermanspau","last":"Timmermans","locid":"b2f8aede-3fec-437d-82a5-cfdb6315f6fc","name":"Timmermans, Bram","orcid":"0000-0003-1087-6017","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jesper Lindgaard","key":"christensenpau","last":"Christensen","locid":"117d598e-4112-43ea-af07-221e55d688e8","name":"Christensen, Jesper Lindgaard","orcid":"0000-0002-8779-5170","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.4324/9781003037750-5","eisbn":["9781003037750"],"isbn":["9780367480479"],"pages":"52-69","publisher":"Routledge","series":"Routledge Studies in Innovation, Organizations and Technology","status":"p","subtitle":"The Danish Innovation System in Transition","title":"Globalisation, New and Emerging Technologies, and Sustainable Development"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85104801234"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-9953e0c5-c5c9-4ee5-ac70-2895c3f9e70b"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Entrepreneurship, experimentation and innovation : Future policy for innovative and growth-oriented entrepreneurs in Denmark","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-094515","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Background: The distribution and nature of symptoms among SARS-CoV-2 infected individuals need to be clarified. Methods: Between May and August 2020, 11 138 healthcare and administrative personnel from Central Denmark Region were tested for SARS-CoV-2 antibodies and subsequently completed a questionnaire. Symptom prevalence and overall duration for symptoms persisting for more than 30 days were calculated. Logistic regression models were used to estimate adjusted odds ratios (ORs) with 95% CIs. Results: In total, 447 (4%) of the participants were SARS-CoV-2-seropositive. Loss of sense of smell and taste was reported by 50% of seropositives compared with 3% of seronegatives. Additionally, seropositives more frequently reported fever, dyspnoea, muscle or joint ache, fatigue, cough, headache and sore throat, and they were more likely to report symptoms persisting for more than 30 days. In adjusted models, they had a higher risk of reporting symptoms, with the strongest association observed for loss of sense of taste and smell (OR = 35.6; 95% CI: 28.6–44.3). Conclusion: In this large study, SARS-CoV-2-seropositive participants reported COVID-19-associated symptoms more frequently than those who were seronegative, especially loss of sense of taste and smell. Overall, their symptoms were also more likely to persist for more than 30 days.","author_count":13,"contributors":["Kaspersen, Kathrine Agergård","Greve, Thomas","Nielsen, Kent Jacob","Jespersen, Sanne","Mikkelsen, Susan","Vestergaard, Jesper Medom","Redder, Jacob Dvinge","Tolstrup, Martin","Thomsen, Marianne Kragh","Møller, Holger Jon","Østergaard, Lars Jørgen","Kolstad, Henrik A.","Erikstrup, Christian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-154974"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Kaspersen, Kathrine Agergård"],"keyword":["Covid 19","Long term symptoms","Prevalence","SARS CoV 2","Serosurvey","Symptoms"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"symptoms reported by sars cov 000000000002 seropositive and seronegative healthcare and administrative employees in denmark from may to august 000000002020","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijid.2021.06.017","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-154974","identifier":[{"id":"PMC8193970","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"34126235","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85110067910","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-2cd1d151-ff41-457a-b835-823a4a20d06b"],"keyword":["COVID-19","Long-term symptoms","Prevalence","SARS-CoV-2","Serosurvey","Symptoms"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B7B682BB2BAA3C197BD5E1C310F198FE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijid.2021.06.017","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Clinical Immunology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Kathrine Agergård","key":"kaspersenpau","last":"Kaspersen","locid":"8a7df28d-6c67-4cb5-93a7-ee2172fc6db1","name":"Kaspersen, Kathrine Agergård","orcid":"0000-0002-7973-3623","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"grevepau","last":"Greve","locid":"9278c035-c92b-4ac8-bcf5-e40935ba4f96","name":"Greve, Thomas","rank"