","key":"ullénpau","last":"Ullén","name":"Ullén, Susann","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Johan","key":"undénpau","last":"Undén","name":"Undén, Johan","rank":14,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Erik","key":"westhallpau","last":"Westhall","name":"Westhall, Erik","rank":15,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"9","first":"Matt P.","key":"wisepau","last":"Wise","name":"Wise, Matt P.","rank":16,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4 10","first":"Henrik","key":"zetterbergpau","last":"Zetterberg","name":"Zetterberg, Henrik","rank":17,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Niklas","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Niklas","rank":18,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Tobias","key":"cronbergpau","last":"Cronberg","name":"Cronberg, Tobias","rank":19,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34417831"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s00134-021-06481-4","issn":["03424642","09351701"],"issn_org":["03424642"],"journal":"Intensive Care Medicine","pages":"984-994","status":"p","title":"Intensive Care Medicine","volume":"47"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85113165680"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-f77a035f-cfc8-4260-93e4-84db1c42f2a6"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Serum markers of brain injury can predict good neurological outcome after out-of-hospital cardiac arrest","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-541183","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"The present study investigated the effects of prior levels of general life stress on young adults’ cognitive and emotional reactions to media coverage of terrorism. University students (n = 94) completed an online baseline survey, after which they were randomly assigned to a terrorism or non-terrorism media exposure condition. Approximately 1–2 weeks later, participants attended an experimental session in which they were shown a real life news clip about a recent terrorist event (terrorism media exposure) or a non-violent societal risk (non-terrorism media exposure). Participants filled out a brief online survey immediately before and after the intervention. Socio-demographic factors and perceived life stress were assessed at baseline. Perceived susceptibility to terrorism, terrorism-related worry, and support for security measures were measured at pre- and post-intervention. Data were analyzed using a series of two-way analyses of covariance (ANCOVA) controlling for pretest scores and relevant socio-demographic factors. Significant interaction effects were found between media exposure and perceived life stress on support for the following security measures: video surveillance, mandatory bag checks at universities, and security patrols at universities during the day. More specifically, participants with lower levels of stress scored similarly on these measures in the terrorism and non-terrorism media exposure groups, whereas among those with higher stress levels, scores were significantly greater in the terrorism compared to non-terrorism media exposure group. Thus, young adults with elevated levels of perceived life stress were more responsive to media coverage of terrorism compared to those with lesser life stress when it comes to a desire for regulatory action. This indicates that higher levels of general life stress may sensitize young people towards the impact of threatening information.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Skøt, Lotte","Nielsen, Jesper Bo","Leppin, Anja"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-596869"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Skøt, Lotte"],"keyword":["Media","Risk perception","Terrorism"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"risk perception and support for security measures interactive effects of media exposure to terrorism and prior life stress","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85083593800","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-596869","identifier":[{"id":"85083593800","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-527816bc-d20d-4aea-93ae-dc9af9e788ae"],"keyword":["Perceived life stress","demand for security measures","media","risk perception","terrorism"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-3821B0B24B2B3A63C43D54D0955A78F5"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research Unit OPEN (Odense)","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Public Health, Health Promotion"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lotte","key":"skøtpau","last":"Skøt","locid":"4902df5a-0988-4535-9480-434af6b26352","name":"Skøt, Lotte","orcid":"0000-0003-1762-8876","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jesper Bo","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"75938cc2-43d6-4c8d-a506-6b1c0472a382","name":"Nielsen, Jesper Bo","orcid":"0000-0002-5764-5462","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anja","key":"leppinpau","last":"Leppin","locid":"6bef1637-e9f4-44e0-902e-6c92974c8711","name":"Leppin, Anja","orcid":"0000-0001-6203-6162","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1080/13669877.2020.1750460","issn":["13669877","14664461"],"issn_org":["13669877"],"issue":"2","journal":"Journal of Risk Research","pages":"228-246","publisher":"Routledge","status":"p","title":"Journal of Risk Research","volume":"24"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85083593800"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-527816bc-d20d-4aea-93ae-dc9af9e788ae"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Risk perception and support for security measures : interactive effects of media exposure to terrorism and prior life stress?","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-596869","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Background: Understanding behavioral factors associated with low health literacy (HL) is relevant for health care providers to better support their patients’ health and adherence to preventive treatment. In this study, we aim to study associations between low HL and socio-demographic characteristics, medication-related perceptions and experience, as well as general psychological factors among patients aged 50–80 years. Methods: We used a cross-sectional survey design based on a representative group of 6,871 Danish citizens aged 50–80 years returning a web-based questionnaire with socio-demographic data added from a national registry. Chi-square tests were conducted to analyze associations between low HL and daily use of medication and self-rated health. Chi-square tests and binary logistic regression were conducted for analyzing data from respondents using prescribed medicines daily (N = 4,091). Results: Respondents with low HL were more often on daily medications (19 % [777/4,091] vs. 16 % [436/2,775]; P < 0.001) and were more likely to have poorer self-rated health (P < 0.001). Among patients on daily medications, low HL was significantly higher among men and those with lower educational attainment and lower family income. Low HL was independently and positively associated with perceptions that taking prescribed medicines daily is difficult and time-consuming, with forgetting to take prescribed medicines, and with lower satisfaction with life and poor self-assessed health. Conclusions: Our study provides information that patients aged 50–80 years with low HL are challenged on their adherence to treatment plans which is not only related to traditional sociodemographic factors but also on perceptions related to taking medication per se.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Thapa, Subash","Nielsen, Jesper Bo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-600523"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Thapa, Subash"],"keyword":["Chronic disease management","Cross Sectional Studies","Denmark epidemiology","Health literacy","Humans","Male","Medication Adherence","Self assessed health","Surveys and Questionnaires","Treatment adherence"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"association between health literacy general psychological factors and adherence to medical treatment among danes aged 000000000050 000000000080 years","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12877-021-02339-y","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-600523","identifier":[{"id":"PMC8236136","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"34174815","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85109624205","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-5f90f5da-510d-4df0-abbb-1bd4144a7fdd"],"keyword":["Chronic disease management","Cross-Sectional Studies","Denmark/epidemiology","Health Literacy","Humans","Male","Medication Adherence","Medication behavior","Medication perception","Patient self-management","Self-assessed health","Surveys and Questionnaires","Treatment adherence"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-FB18A32476E11030F823EC06A35026FE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/183842121/Thapa_2021_Association_between_health_literacy.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12877-021-02339-y","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Public Health, Research Unit of General Pratice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Subash","key":"thapapau","last":"Thapa","locid":"a271bef3-0a7e-4c8d-b6b7-28a6a21e9480","name":"Thapa, Subash","orcid":"0000-0002-1182-8511","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jesper B.","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"75938cc2-43d6-4c8d-a506-6b1c0472a382","name":"Nielsen, Jesper Bo","name_org":"Nielsen, Jesper B.","orcid":"0000-0002-5764-5462","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34174815"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1186/s12877-021-02339-y","issn":["14712318"],"journal":"BMC Geriatrics","paperid":"386","publisher":"BioMed Central","status":"p","title":"BMC Geriatrics","volume":"21"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85109624205"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-5f90f5da-510d-4df0-abbb-1bd4144a7fdd"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Association between health literacy, general psychological factors, and adherence to medical treatment among Danes aged 50–80 years","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-600523","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Seafaring is a risky occupation with high prevalence of risk factors for non-communicable diseases. Food intake and eating habits are important cornerstones regarding health and health promotion. The aim of this study was to provide an overview of dietary intake and dietary intake assessment methods of seafarers and suggestions for applicable assessment tools. METHODS: We systematically searched PubMed and NLM Gateway (for MEDLINE), Web of Science, and SCOPUS up to February 2020 using standard keywords including nutrition OR diet OR meal AND maritime settings. Two independent reviewers extracted the data. The quality of included studies was assessed using the Joanna Briggs Institute Critical Appraisal checklist. RESULTS: From 4449 studies initially identified, 26 articles were included in the final review. Qualitative data (e.g. on unhealthy eating) had been gathered using in-depth individual or group interviews, participant observations, and phone-based chats. Composition of menu analysis, 24 h dietary recall, food diaries/ diet records, dietary habits questionnaire, food stores and food waste of the ship were used to assess the quantitative outcomes (e.g. dietary intakes). Access to meat, processed meat and egg, frozen and canned food items, sugary drinks, alcohol, greasy and salty food was high. In contrast, consumption of fruit, vegetables, dairy products, and cereals was lower than recommended. CONCLUSIONS: Eating habits and dietary intakes in maritime settings are unhealthy. Subjective dietary assessment methods combining menu analysis with new technologies (e.g. mobile-based) might be an applicable method in this hard to reach setting which is the vessels.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Baygi, Fereshteh","Mohammadi-Nasrabadi, Fatemeh","Zyriax, Birgit-Christiane","Jensen, Olaf Chresten","Bygvraa, Despena Andrioti","Oldenburg, Marcus","Nielsen, Jesper Bo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-607858"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, Middle East","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Baygi, Fereshteh"],"country":["Germany","Iran","Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Shahid Beheshti University of Medical Sciences","University Medical Center Hamburg-Eppendorf","University of Gothenburg"],"keyword":["Food","Maritime settings","Meal","Nutrition","Systematic review"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"global overview of dietary outcomes and dietary intake assessment methods in maritime settings a systematic review","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12889-021-11593-z","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-607858","identifier":[{"id":"PMC8379789","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85113238959","type":"scopus"},{"id":"34419000","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["sdu-78e36c20-dc70-47ad-90d0-1485255a4459"],"keyword":["Food","Maritime settings","Meal","Nutrition","Systematic review"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-1BC0C510A2C47E9789C9083D198CA9D7"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12889-021-11593-z","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/190642745/Baygi_2021_Global_overview_of_dietary_outcomes.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Public Health, Research Unit of General Pratice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"ir","key":"shahid beheshti university of medical sciences","loc":["Iran","Asia, Middle East"],"name":"Shahid Beheshti University of Medical Sciences","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"university medical center hamburg eppendorf","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University Medical Center Hamburg-Eppendorf","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"se","key":"university of gothenburg","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Gothenburg","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Fereshteh","key":"baygipau","last":"Baygi","locid":"82ac54a3-f35a-4ee6-85dc-362c9e160ee9","name":"Baygi, Fereshteh","orcid":"0000-0002-9260-2281","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Fatemeh","key":"mohammadinasrabadipau","last":"Mohammadi-Nasrabadi","name":"Mohammadi-Nasrabadi, Fatemeh","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Birgit-Christiane","key":"zyriaxpau","last":"Zyriax","name":"Zyriax, Birgit-Christiane","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Olaf Chresten","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","locid":"07d58668-54e6-4acc-a0e0-84d9a2d59bc1","name":"Jensen, Olaf Chresten","orcid":"0000-0001-7672-9891","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Despena Andrioti","key":"bygvraapau","last":"Bygvraa","name":"Bygvraa, Despena Andrioti","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Marcus","key":"oldenburgpau","last":"Oldenburg","name":"Oldenburg, Marcus","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jesper Bo","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"75938cc2-43d6-4c8d-a506-6b1c0472a382","name":"Nielsen, Jesper Bo","orcid":"0000-0002-5764-5462","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34419000"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1186/s12889-021-11593-z","issn":["14712458"],"journal":"BMC Public Health","paperid":"1579","publisher":"BioMed Central","status":"p","title":"BMC Public Health","volume":"21"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85113238959"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-78e36c20-dc70-47ad-90d0-1485255a4459"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Global overview of dietary outcomes and dietary intake assessment methods in maritime settings : a systematic review","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-607858","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Aim: In the Danish faecal occult blood test based bowel cancer screening programme, the first round was rolled out over 4 years. After roll-out, the planned faecal test recall procedure for individuals with either no or low risk adenomas at colonoscopy is 8 and 2 years, respectively. Here, we aimed to investigate the post colonoscopy colorectal cancer incidence in these two groups. Methods: All Danish screening individuals from 2014 to 2015 with a positive faecal test and either no or low risk adenomas at colonoscopy were included and followed for 3 years post screening for the event of colorectal cancer through national registries. Results: Out of 533,023 submitted faecal tests and 36,673 positive tests, 17,627 had no or low risk adenomas. We identified 60 (0.34%) individuals diagnosed with colorectal cancer within 3 years, 18 (0.29%) in the low risk adenoma group, and 42 (0.37%) in the no adenomas group (p = 0.44). Advancing age (HR = 1.079, p < 0.001) and higher faecal test value (HR = 1.001, p = 0.002) increased hazard of colorectal cancer occurrence, whereas male sex (HR = 1.3, p = 0.308) and having low risk adenomas (HR = 0.729, p = 0.264) did not. Conclusion: We found no difference in post colonoscopy colorectal cancer occurrence between individuals with either no or low risk adenomas. Instead, advancing age and increased faecal test value was associated with a higher risk of post colonoscopy colorectal cancer.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Nielsen, Jesper Clausen","Ploug, Magnus","Baatrup, Gunnar","Kroijer, Rasmus"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-624865"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Kroijer, Rasmus"],"keyword":["Colonoscopy","Colorectal cancer","Interval cancer","Screening"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-SDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"risk of post colonoscopy colorectal cancer following screening colonoscopy with low risk or no adenomas a population based study","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/191814504/codi.15886.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-624865","identifier":[{"id":"85114156661","type":"scopus"},{"id":"34427981","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["sdu-b4563dca-3f5f-4e4f-91af-f410cd26b8e4"],"keyword":["colonoscopy","colorectal cancer","interval cancer","screening"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-4-8-E1A991E16A7F31D80678E72C8ABD268B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/191814504/codi.15886.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"region of southern denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-SDR","l3nl":"Region of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"Region of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Region of Southern Denmark","namevar":["Sydvestjysk Sygehus Esbjerg"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-SDR","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Regional Health Research, IRS - Esbjerg and Grindsted Hospital, Research Unit of Medical gastroenterology-hepatology (Esbjerg)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jesper Clausen","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Jesper Clausen","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Magnus","key":"plougpau","last":"Ploug","locid":"bd5fdb65-db6f-4e39-a601-592461784caa","name":"Ploug, Magnus","orcid":"0000-0002-4229-3994","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Gunnar","key":"baatruppau","last":"Baatrup","locid":"3e62f6ae-0738-4c20-ac2f-3543bb12fa6b","name":"Baatrup, Gunnar","orcid":"0000-0003-0300-5766","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Rasmus","key":"kroijerpau","last":"Kroijer","locid":"c26556ac-04a1-4ee4-b5aa-6e230c1679b2","name":"Kroijer, Rasmus","orcid":"0000-0003-4358-7916","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34427981"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/codi.15886","issn":["14628910","14631318"],"issn_org":["14631318"],"issue":"11","journal":"Colorectal Disease","pages":"2932-2936","publisher":"Wiley","status":"p","title":"Colorectal Disease","volume":"23"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85114156661","2-s2.0-85118673286"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-b4563dca-3f5f-4e4f-91af-f410cd26b8e4"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Risk of post colonoscopy colorectal cancer following screening colonoscopy with low-risk or no adenomas : A population-based study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-624865","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Lipid-filled adipocytes are incompatible with droplet-based single-cell methods, such as 10x Genomics-based technology, thus restricting droplet-based single-cell analyses of adipose tissues to the stromal vascular fraction. To overcome this limitation and obtain cellular and molecular insight into adipose tissue composition and plasticity, single-nucleus sequencing-based technologies can be applied. Here, we provide an optimized protocol for nuclei isolation from mouse adipose tissues suitable for single-nucleus RNA sequencing. This allows for transcriptomic profiling of the entire adipose tissue at single-cell resolution. For complete details on the use of this protocol, please refer to Sárvári et al., 2021.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Van Hauwaert, Elvira Laila","Gammelmark, Ellen","Sárvári, Anitta Kinga","Larsen, Lena","Nielsen, Ronni","Madsen, Jesper Grud Skat","Mandrup, Susanne"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-637508"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Mandrup, Susanne","Van Hauwaert, Elvira Laila"],"keyword":["Cell isolation","Gene expression","Genomics","Metabolism","RNA seq","Single cell"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"isolation of nuclei from mouse white adipose tissues for single nucleus genomics","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xpro.2021.100612","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["The work was supported by grants from the Danish National Research Foundation (DNRF grant No. 141 to the Center for Functional Genomics and Tissue Plasticity [ATLAS]) and grants from the Novo Nordisk Foundation through a grant to the Danish Diabetes Academy. The authors thank Tenna Pavia Mortensen for expert technical assistance and colleagues from the Functional Genomics & Metabolism Research Unit for fruitful discussions. Methodology, E.L.V.H. E.G. and L.L.; investigation, E.L.V.H. E.G. A.K.S. L.L. and R.N.; validation, E.L.V.H. E.G. L.L.; writing ? original draft, E.L.V.H. and S.M.; writing ? review & editing, S.M. J.G.S.M. E.L.V.H. E.G. and A.K.S.; visualization, E.L.V.H. and E.G.; supervision, S.M. and J.G.S.M.; project administration, S.M. and J.G.S.M.; funding acquisition, S.M. and E.G. The authors declare no competing interests."],"id":"S01-000.00-637508","identifier":[{"id":"85108000529","type":"scopus"},{"id":"34189477","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC8220393","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["sdu-e07fcc4e-622f-4fda-a4e1-32000d7c2b6d"],"keyword":["Cell isolation","Gene Expression","Genomics","Metabolism","RNA-seq","Single Cell"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-4B1DACD5C6FA11A3B7CA2E3180473C94"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xpro.2021.100612","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/183817052/1_s2.0_S2666166721003191_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology","SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology, ATLAS - Center for Functional Genomics and Tissue Plasticity"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Elvira Laila","key":"vanhauwaertpau","last":"Van Hauwaert","locid":"ca4ec6e6-c3d5-4158-b0b9-bd7a125cf8de","name":"Van Hauwaert, Elvira Laila","orcid":"0000-0002-6248-0025","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ellen","key":"gammelmarkpau","last":"Gammelmark","locid":"ff07fdc8-20b8-401a-85b5-2dc69788d6ee","name":"Gammelmark, Ellen","orcid":"0000-0003-1486-5219","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anitta Kinga","key":"sárváripau","last":"Sárvári","locid":"067c0118-3b2f-4943-991d-54a19815bd23","name":"Sárvári, Anitta Kinga","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lena","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","name":"Larsen, Lena","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Ronni","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"3fd12718-04a0-4664-882c-8213d3b5291b","name":"Nielsen, Ronni","orcid":"0000-0001-6331-7660","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jesper Grud Skat","key":"madsenpau","last":"Madsen","locid":"f3263ff0-108b-4afd-a88b-27fd259db777","name":"Madsen, Jesper Grud Skat","orcid":"0000-0002-0518-0800","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Susanne","key":"mandruppau","last":"Mandrup","locid":"6e8c29d6-da8a-4ef0-ab1a-03efe2e3d9e5","name":"Mandrup, Susanne","orcid":"0000-0002-0961-5787","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34189477"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.xpro.2021.100612","issn":["26661667"],"issue":"3","journal":"STAR Protocols","paperid":"100612","publisher":"Cell Press","status":"p","title":"STAR Protocols","volume":"2"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85108000529"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-e07fcc4e-622f-4fda-a4e1-32000d7c2b6d"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Isolation of nuclei from mouse white adipose tissues for single-nucleus genomics","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-637508","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Most type 2 diabetes patients are treated in general practice and there is a need of developing and implementing efficient lifestyle interventions. eHealth interventions have shown to be effective in promoting a healthy lifestyle. The purpose of this study was to test the feasibility, including the identification of factors of importance, when offering digital lifestyle coaching to type 2 diabetes patients in general practice. We conducted a qualitative feasibility study with focus group interviews in four general practices. We identified two overall themes and four subthemes: (1) the distribution of roles and lifestyle interventions in general practice (subthemes: external and internal distribution of roles) and (2) the pros and cons for digital lifestyle interventions in general practice (subthemes: access to real life data and change in daily routines). We conclude that for digital lifestyle coaching to be feasible in a general practice setting, it was of great importance that the general practitioners and practice nurses knew the role and content of the intervention. In general, there was a positive attitude in the general practice setting towards referring type 2 diabetes patients to digital lifestyle intervention if it was easy to refer the patients and if easily understandable and accessible feedback was implemented into the electronic health record. It was important that the digital lifestyle intervention was flexible and offered healthcare providers in general practice an opportunity to follow the type 2 diabetes patient closely.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Jakobsen, Pernille Ravn","Christensen, Jeanette Reffstrup","Nielsen, Jesper Bo","Søndergaard, Jens","Jarbøl, Dorte Ejg","Olsen, Michael Hecht","Nielsen, Jens Steen","Kristensen, Jette Kolding","Brandt, Carl J."],"cuid":["S02-088.89-009071"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":