{"abstract":"Threshold secret sharing allows a dealer to split a secret into n shares such that any authorized subset of cardinality at least t of those shares efficiently reveals the secret, while at the same time any unauthorized subset of cardinality less than t contains no information about the secret. Leakage-resilience additionally requires that the secret remains hidden even if one is given a bounded amount of additional leakage from every share. In this work, we study leakage-resilient secret sharing schemes and prove a lower bound on the share size and the required amount of randomness of any information-theoretically secure scheme. We prove that for any information-theoretically secure leakage-resilient secret sharing scheme either the amount of randomness across all shares or the share size has to be linear in n. More concretely, for a secret sharing scheme with p-bit long shares, l-bit leakage per share, where t shares uniquely define the remaining t shares, it has to hold that (Formula presented) We use this lower bound to gain further insights into a question that was recently posed by Benhamouda et al. (CRYPTO’18), who ask to what extend existing regular secret sharing schemes already provide protection against leakage. The authors proved that Shamir’s secret sharing is 1-bit leakage-resilient for reconstruction thresholds (Formula presented) and conjectured that it is also 1-bit leakage-resilient for any other threshold that is a constant fraction of the total number of shares. We do not disprove their conjecture, but show that it is the best one could possibly hope for. Concretely, we show that for large enough n and any constant (Formula presented) it holds that Shamir’s secret sharing scheme is not leakage-resilient for. In contrast to the setting with information-theoretic security, we show that our lower bound does not hold in the computational setting. That is, we show how to construct a leakage-resilient secret sharing scheme in the random oracle model that is secure against computationally bounded adversaries and violates the lower bound stated above.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Nielsen, Jesper Buus","Simkin, Mark"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-174707"],"event":{"acronym":"EUROCRYPT","end":"2020-05-14","place":"Zagreb, hr","start":"2020-05-10","title":"39th Annual International Conference on the Theory and Applications of Cryptographic Techniques, EUROCRYPT 2020","year":"2020"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Nielsen, Jesper Buus"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"lower bounds for leakage resilient secret sharing","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85089715755","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-174707","identifier":[{"id":"85089715755","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-4fa7ed32-ab08-4576-9680-4c2fdf4a5b00"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-1AE794C7D37DACBF9A82BF63F27CE05C"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jesper Buus","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"f428c4b3-80b3-403a-ada6-9ecb0fad97b2","name":"Nielsen, Jesper Buus","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mark","key":"simkinpau","last":"Simkin","locid":"0ce0b3ea-cb5a-4bce-bfe5-9bac481d652c","name":"Simkin, Mark","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Anne","key":"canteautpedh","last":"Canteaut","name":"Canteaut, Anne","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Yuval","key":"ishaipedh","last":"Ishai","name":"Ishai, Yuval","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-030-45721-1_20","isbn":["9783030457204"],"issn":["03029743"],"pages":"556-577","place":"Cham","publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","title":"Advances in Cryptology – EUROCRYPT 2020 - 39th Annual International Conference on the Theory and Applications of Cryptographic Techniques, Proceedings"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85089715755"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-4fa7ed32-ab08-4576-9680-4c2fdf4a5b00"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Lower bounds for leakage-resilient secret sharing","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-174707","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Residential wood combustion (RWC) is an important contributor to air quality in numerous regions worldwide. This study is the first extensive evaluation of the influence of RWC on ambient air quality in several Nordic cities. We have analysed the emissions and concentrations of PM classCombining double low line\"inline-formula\" 2.5 in cities within four Nordic countries: in the metropolitan areas of Copenhagen, Oslo, and Helsinki and in the city of Umeä. We have evaluated the emissions for the relevant urban source categories and modelled atmospheric dispersion on regional and urban scales. The emission inventories for RWC were based on local surveys, the amount of wood combusted, combustion technologies and other relevant factors. The accuracy of the predicted concentrations was evaluated based on urban concentration measurements. The predicted annual average concentrations ranged spatially from 4 to 7&thinsp; classCombining double low line\"inline-formula\" μg m-3 (2011), from 6 to 10&thinsp; classCombining double low line\"inline-formula\" μg m-3 (2013), from 4 to more than 13&thinsp; classCombining double low line\"inline-formula\" μg m-3 (2013) and from 9 to more than 13&thinsp; classCombining double low line\"inline-formula\" μg m-3 (2014), in Umeä, Helsinki, Oslo and Copenhagen, respectively. The higher concentrations in Copenhagen were mainly caused by the relatively high regionally and continentally transported background contributions. The annual average fractions of PM classCombining double low line\"inline-formula\" 2.5 concentrations attributed to RWC within the considered urban regions ranged spatially from 0&thinsp;% to 15&thinsp;%, from 0&thinsp;% to 20&thinsp;%, from 8&thinsp;% to 22&thinsp;% and from 0&thinsp;% to 60&thinsp;% in Helsinki, Copenhagen, Umeä and Oslo, respectively. In particular, the contributions of RWC in central Oslo were larger than 40&thinsp;% as annual averages. In Oslo, wood combustion was used mainly for the heating of larger blocks of flats. In contrast, in Helsinki, RWC was solely used in smaller detached houses. In Copenhagen and Helsinki, the highest fractions occurred outside the city centre in the suburban areas. In Umeä, the highest fractions occurred both in the city centre and its surroundings.","author_count":28,"contributors":["Kukkonen, Jaakko","López-Aparicio, Susana","Segersson, David","Geels, Camilla","Kangas, Leena","Kauhaniemi, Mari","Maragkidou, Androniki","Jensen, Anne","Assmuth, Timo","Karppinen, Ari","Sofiev, Mikhail","Hellén, Heidi","Riikonen, Kari","Nikmo, Juha","Kousa, Anu","Niemi, Jarkko V.","Karvosenoja, Niko","Sousa Santos, Gabriela","Sundvor, Ingrid","Im, Ulas","Christensen, Jesper Heile","Nielsen, Ole-Kenneth","Plejdrup, Marlene Schmidt","Nøjgaard, Jacob Klenø","Omstedt, Gunnar","Andersson, Camilla","Forsberg, Bertil","Brandt, Jørgen"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-196127"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Kukkonen, Jaakko"],"country":["Finland","Norway","Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Finnish Environment Institute","Finnish Meteorological Institute","Swedish Meteorological and Hydrological Institute","The Climate and Environmental Research Institute (NILU)","Umeå University"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the influence of residential wood combustion on the concentrations of pm000000000002 000000000005 in four nordic cities","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.5194/acp-20-4333-2020","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-196127","identifie