{"abstract":"The knowledge of speech presence probability (SPP) plays an essential role in noise estimation and speech enhancement. Single channel SPP estimation and centralized multi-channel SPP estimation have been well studied. However, how to estimate SPP in wireless acoustic sensor networks (WASNs) remains a great challenge and few efforts can be found in this topic, particularly for WASN applications with multiple speakers. Accordingly, this paper is devoted to the problem of SPP estimation in WASNs and it presents a distributed model-based SPP estimation method for multi-speaker detection, which does not need any fusion center. A distributed k-means clustering method is first used to cluster the nodes into subnetworks, which detect different speakers. For each node in the subnetwork, the speech and noise power spectral densities are estimated locally by using a model-based method, then a distributed SPP estimator is developed and applied in every subnetwork. A distributed consensus method is used to obtain the distributed clustering and the distributed SPP estimation. Simulation results show that the proposed distributed clustering method can assign nodes into subnetworks based on their noisy observations. Moreover, the proposed distributed SPP estimator achieves robust speech detection performance under different noise conditions.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Zhao, Yingke","Nielsen, Jesper Kjær","Chen, Jingdong","Christensen, Mads Græsbøll"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-056649"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East"],"country":["China"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Northwestern Polytechnical University Xian"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"model based distributed node clustering and multi speaker speech presence probability estimation in wireless acoustic sensor networks","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/395244578/Manuscript.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-056649","identifier":[{"id":"85087657734","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-16825df3-d942-41b2-945b-11c0d40bdfad"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-81088A1DE85E15387F0CC869DA198C5A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/395244578/Manuscript.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, Audio Analysis Lab (CREATE)","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, Section for Media Technology - Campus Aalborg"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"cn","key":"northwestern polytechnical university xian","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Northwestern Polytechnical University Xian","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Yingke","key":"zhaopau","last":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yingke","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jesper Kjær","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"3d21c73c-3aa1-4590-870f-8be578634702","name":"Nielsen, Jesper Kjær","orcid":"0000-0002-2586-7925","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jingdong","key":"chenpau","last":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jingdong","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mads Græsbøll","key":"christensenpau","last":"Christensen","locid":"cf9e922d-e5f8-4964-9b84-581667f97b0b","name":"Christensen, Mads Græsbøll","orcid":"0000-0003-3586-7969","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1121/10.0001449","issn":["00014966","01630962"],"issn_org":["00014966"],"issue":"6","journal":"Journal of the Acoustical Society of America","pages":"4189-4201","publisher":"Acoustical Society of America","status":"p","title":"Journal of the Acoustical Society of America","title_org":"The Journal of the Acoustical Society of America","volume":"147"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85087657734"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-16825df3-d942-41b2-945b-11c0d40bdfad"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Model-Based Distributed Node Clustering and Multi-Speaker Speech Presence Probability Estimation in Wireless Acoustic Sensor Networks","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-056649","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Weather radar has become an invaluable tool for monitoring rainfall and studying its link to hydrological response. However, when it comes to accurately measuring small-scale rainfall extremes responsible for urban flooding, many challenges remain. The most important of them is that radar tends to underestimate rainfall compared to gauges. The hope is that by measuring at higher resolutions and making use of dual-polarization radar, these mismatches can be reduced. Each country has developed its own strategy for addressing this issue. However, since there is no common benchmark, improvements are hard to quantify objectively. This study sheds new light on current performances by conducting a multinational assessment of radar's ability to capture heavy rain events at scales of 5 min up to 2 h. The work is performed within the context of the joint experiment framework of project MUFFIN (Multiscale Urban Flood Forecasting), which aims at better understanding the link between rainfall and urban pluvial flooding across scales. In total, six different radar products in Denmark, the Netherlands, Finland and Sweden were considered. The top 50 events in a 10-year database of radar data were used to quantify the overall agreement between radar and gauges as well as the bias affecting the peaks. Results show that the overall agreement in heavy rain is fair (correlation coefficient 0.7–0.9), with apparent multiplicative biases on the order of 1.2–1.8 (17  %–44  % underestimation). However, after taking into account the different sampling volumes of radar and gauges, actual biases could be as low as 10 %. Differences in sampling volumes between radar and gauges play an important role in explaining the bias but are hard to quantify precisely due to the many post-processing steps applied to radar. Despite being adjusted for bias by gauges, five out of six radar products still exhibited a clear conditional bias, with intensities of about 1 %–2 % per mmh−1. As a result, peak rainfall intensities were severely underestimated (factor 1.8–3.0 or 44 %–67 %). The most likely reason for this is the use of a fixed Z–R relationship when estimating rainfall rates (R) from reflectivity (Z), which fails to account for natural variations in raindrop size distribution with intensity. Based on our findings, the easiest way to mitigate the bias in times of heavy rain is to perform frequent (e.g., hourly) bias adjustments with the help of rain gauges, as demonstrated by the Dutch C-band product. An even more promising strategy that does not require any gauge adjustments is to estimate rainfall rates using a combination of reflectivity (Z) and differential phase shift (Kdp), as done in the Finnish OSAPOL product. Both approaches lead to approximately similar performances, with an average bias (at 10 min resolution) of about 30 % and a peak intensity bias of about 45 %.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Schleiss, Marc","Olsson, Jonas","Bjerg, Peter","Niemi, Tero","Kokkonen, Teemu","Thorndahl, Søren Liedtke","Nielsen, Rasmus","Nielsen, Jesper Ellerbæk","Bozhinova, Denica","Pulkkinen, Seppo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-071526"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Schleiss, Marc"],"country":["Finland","Netherlands","Sweden","United States"],"glorg":["Aalto University","All Global Affiliations","Colorado State University","Delft University of Technology","Finnish Meteorological Institute","Hydrology Research Unit, Swedish Meteorological and Hydrological Institute SMHI, Norrkoping, Sweden","Research & Development (Hydrology), Swedish Meteorological and Hydrological Institute, Norrköping, Sweden"],"keyword":["Accuracy","Denmark","Finland","Heavy rainfall","Netherlands","Sweden","Weather radar"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the accuracy of weather radar in heavy rain a comparative study for denmark the netherlands finland and sweden","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-24-3157-2020","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-071526","identifier":[{"id":"85088293528","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-4a0d01d9-780c-48bc-b1fd-fa6854a1f99f"],"keyword":["Accuracy","Denmark","Finland","Heavy rainfall","Netherlands","Sweden","Weather radar"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-2CAEE96AB66059F6FA7831F81207A80A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/334586181/The_accuracy_of_weather_radar_in_heavy_rain_a_comparative_study_for_Denmark_the_Netherlands_Finland_and_Sweden.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-24-3157-2020","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"nl","key":"delft university of technology","loc":["Netherlands","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Delft University of Technology","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"se","key":"research development swedish meteorological and hydrological institute norrköping sweden","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Research & Development (Hydrology), Swedish Meteorological and Hydrological Institute, Norrköping, Sweden","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"se","key":"hydrology research unit swedish meteorological and hydrological institute smhi norrkoping sweden","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Hydrology Research Unit, Swedish Meteorological and Hydrological Institute SMHI, Norrk