{"abstract":"Aims: The aim of this systematic review was to explore health care professionals' attitudes towards deprescribing in older people with limited life expectancy. Methods: A systematic literature search was conducted from inception to December 2017 using MEDLINE, EMBASE and CINAHL. Studies were included if they specifically concerned older people (≥65 years) with limited life expectancy, including those residing in any type of aged care facility, or were based on representative patient profiles. Results were analyzed inspired by the Joanna Briggs Institute's method for synthesis of qualitative data. Studies were characterized using a checklist for reporting of qualitative research. Results: Eight studies were included. Six studies explored health care professionals' views on deprescribing in general, and two studies focused specifically on psychotropic agents. All eight studies explored the views of physicians, mostly general practitioners, while three studies also considered other health care professionals. Four themes related to health care professionals' attitudes towards deprescribing were identified: (i) patient and relative involvement; (ii) the importance of teamwork; (iii) health care professionals' self-assurance and skills; and (iv) the impact of organizational factors. Within each of these themes, 3–4 subthemes were identified and analysed. Conclusions: Our results suggest that health care professionals' decisions to engage in deprescribing activities with older people with limited life expectancy depend on multiple factors which are highly interdependent. Consequently, there is an urgent need for more research on how to approach deprescribing in clinical practice within this population.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Lundby, Carina","Graabæk, Trine","Ryg, Jesper","Søndergaard, Jens","Pottegård, Anton","Nielsen, Dorthe Susanne"],"cuid":["S02-082.78-049543"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Aged","Aged 80 and over","Attitude of Health Personnel","Clinical Competence","Clinical Decision Making","Deprescriptions","Drug safety","Elderly","General Practitioners statistics numerical data","Humans","Life Expectancy","Practice Patterns Physicians statistics numerical data","Prescribing","Qualitative Research"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U","DI-UC"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-SDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"],"UC":["DI-UC-ALL","DI-UC-UCL"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-SDU","DI-UC-UCL"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"health care professionals attitudes towards deprescribing in older patients with limited life expectancy a systematic review","src":["sdu","ucl"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU","DI-UC-UCL"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Andet","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/bcp.13861","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-082.78-049543","identifier":[{"id":"30630219","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85062358841","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC6475695","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["sdu-d203b526-f21a-4d75-9cee-73d1b1f25949","ucl-46686ec5-bd36-4e30-8452-38e422517b5c"],"keyword":["Aged","Aged, 80 and over","Attitude of Health Personnel","Clinical Competence","Clinical Decision-Making","Deprescriptions","General Practitioners/statistics & numerical data","Humans","Life Expectancy","Practice Patterns, Physicians'/statistics & numerical data","Qualitative Research","drug safety","elderly","prescribing"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-5AFB0245A2A881548DA03502E5076EBB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Andet","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/bcp.13861","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/145601462/Health_care_professionals_attitudes_towards_deprescribing_in_older_patients_with_limited_life_expectancy.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Public Health, Clinical Pharmacology and Pharmacy"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"region of southern denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-SDR","l3nl":"Region of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"Region of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Region of Southern Denmark","namevar":["Odense University Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-SDR","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"ucl university college","l2":"DI-UC","l2n":"University Colleges","l3":"DI-UC-UCL","l3nl":"UCL University College","l3nn":"UCL University College","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"UCL University College","namevar":["University College Lillebælt"],"nora_code":"DI-UC-UCL","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Carina","key":"lundbypau","last":"Lundby","locid":"ef58a4be-0ffe-42f3-80ec-7f6cecf96102","name":"Lundby, Carina","orcid":"0000-0002-2121-6252","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Trine","key":"graabækpau","last":"Graabæk","locid":"67e97b89-6918-4950-9557-f5e3939a3c57","name":"Graabæk, Trine","orcid":"0000-0001-6848-3107","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jesper","key":"rygpau","last":"Ryg","locid":"c63490d8-ba13-4875-bf40-82c4aebbab6f","name":"Ryg, Jesper","orcid":"0000-0002-8641-3062","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jens","key":"søndergaardpau","last":"Søndergaard","locid":"4829db0c-8486-4ad1-8e6b-d4f3dc56c386","name":"Søndergaard, Jens","orcid":"0000-0002-1629-1864","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"A","key":"pottegårdpau","last":"Pottegård","locid":"addfc2c1-4fc9-44cd-8087-bce3bc986652","name":"Pottegård, Anton","name_org":"Pottegård, A","orcid":"0000-0001-9314-5679","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Dorthe Susanne","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"52791e3c-f692-493c-9085-fd6de2c829ac","name":"Nielsen, Dorthe Susanne","orcid":"0000-0002-3954-7551","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["30630219"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/bcp.13861","issn":["02643774","03065251"],"issn_org":["03065251"],"issue":"5","journal":"British Journal of Clinical Pharmacology","pages":"868-892","publisher":"Wiley","status":"p","title":"British Journal of Clinical Pharmacology","volume":"85"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85062358841"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","All University Colleges","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","DI-UC","DI-UC-ALL","Hospitals","Region of Southern Denmark","UCL University College","Universities","University Colleges","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-d203b526-f21a-4d75-9cee-73d1b1f25949","ucl-46686ec5-bd36-4e30-8452-38e422517b5c"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark","UCL University College"],"title":"Health care professionals' attitudes towards deprescribing in older patients with limited life expectancy: A systematic review","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-082.78-049543","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: An effective way of preventing sudden cardiac death is the use of an implantable cardioverter defibrillator (ICD). In spite of the potential mortality benefits of receiving an ICD device, psychological problems experienced by patients after receiving an ICD may negatively impact their health-related quality of life, and lead to increased readmission to hospital and healthcare needs, loss of productivity and employment earnings, and increased morbidity and mortality. Evidence from other heart conditions suggests that cardiac rehabilitation should consist of both exercise training and psychoeducational interventions; such rehabilitation may benefit patients with an ICD. Prior systematic reviews of cardiac rehabilitation have excluded participants with an ICD. A systematic review was therefore conducted to assess the evidence for the use of exercise-based intervention programmes following implantation of an ICD. OBJECTIVES: To assess the benefits and harms of exercise-based cardiac rehabilitation programmes (exercise-based interventions alone or in combination with psychoeducational components) compared with control (group of no intervention, treatment as usual or another rehabilitation programme with no physical exercise element) in adults with an ICD. SEARCH METHODS: We searched CENTRAL, MEDLINE, Embase and four other databases on 30 August 2018 and three trials registers on 14 November 2017. We also undertook reference checking, citation searching and contacted study authors for missing data. SELECTION CRITERIA: We included randomised controlled trials (RCTs) if they investigated exercise-based cardiac rehabilitation interventions compared with no intervention, treatment as usual or another rehabilitation programme. The trial participants were adults (aged 18 years or older), who had been treated with an ICD regardless of type or indication. DATA COLLECTION AND ANALYSIS: Two review authors independently extracted data and assessed risk of bias. The primary outcomes were all-cause mortality, serious adverse events and health-related quality of life. The secondary outcomes were exercise capacity, antitachycardia pacing, shock, non-serious adverse events, employment or loss of employment and costs and cost-effectiveness. Risk of systematic errors (bias) was assessed by evaluation of predefined bias risk domains. Clinical and statistical heterogeneity were assessed. Meta-analyses were undertaken using both fixed-effect and random-effects models. We used the GRADE approach to assess the quality of evidence. MAIN RESULTS: We identified eight trials published from 2004 to 2017 randomising a total of 1730 participants, with mean intervention duration of 12 weeks. All eight trials were judged to be at overall high risk of bias and effect estimates are reported at the end of the intervention with a follow-up range of eight to 24 weeks.Seven trials reported all-cause mortality, but deaths only occurred in one trial with no evidence of a difference between exercise-based cardiac rehabilitation and control (risk ratio (RR) 1.96, 95% confidence interval (CI) 0.18 to 21.26; participants = 196; trials = 1; quality of evidence: low). There was also no evidence of a difference in serious adverse events between exercise-based cardiac rehabilitation and control (RR 1.05, 95% CI 0.77 to 1.44; participants = 356; trials = 2; quality of evidence: low). Due to the variation in reporting of health-related quality of life outcomes, it was not possible to pool data. However, the five trials reporting health-related quality of life at the end of the intervention, each showed little or no evidence of a difference between exercise-based cardiac rehabilitation and control.For secondary outcomes, there was evidence of a higher pooled exercise capacity (peak VO 2) at the end of the intervention (mean difference (MD) 0.91 mL/kg/min, 95% CI 0.60 to 1.21; participants = 1485; trials = 7; quality of evidence: very low) favouring exercise-based cardiac rehabilitation, albeit there was evidence of substantial statistical heterogeneity (I 2 = 78%). There was no evidence of a difference in the risk of requiring antitachycardia pacing (RR 1.26, 95% CI 0.84 to 1.90; participants = 356; trials = 2; quality of evidence: moderate), appropriate shock (RR 0.56, 95% CI 0.20 to 1.58; participants = 428; studies = 3; quality of evidence: low) or inappropriate shock (RR 0.60, 95% CI 0.10 to 3.51; participants = 160; studies = 1; quality of evidence: moderate). AUTHORS' CONCLUSIONS: Due to a lack of evidence, we were unable to definitively assess the impact of exercise-based cardiac rehabilitation on all-cause mortality, serious adverse events and health-related quality of life in adults with an ICD. However, our findings do provide very low-quality evidence that patients following exercise-based cardiac rehabilitation experience a higher exercise capacity compared with the no exercise control. Further high-quality randomised trials are needed in order to assess the impact of exercise-based cardiac rehabilitation in this population on all-cause mortality, serious adverse events, health-related quality of life, antitachycardia pacing and shock.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Nielsen, Kim M.","Zwisler, Ann Dorthe","Taylor, Rod S.","Svendsen, Jesper Hastrup","Lindschou, Jane","Anderson, Lindsey","Jakobsen, Janus C.","Berg, Selina Kikkenborg"],"cuid":["S02-087.09-666849"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Nielsen, Kim M."],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Exeter"],"keyword":["Aged","Cardiac Rehabilitation adverse effects","Cause of Death","Defibrillators Implantable adverse effects","Exercise Therapy","Exercise Tolerance","Female","Humans","Male","Middle Aged","Oxygen Consumption","Quality of Life","Randomized Controlled Trials as Topic"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"exercise based cardiac rehabilitation for adult patients with an implantable cardioverter defibrillator","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/245617888/Nielsen_et_al_2019_Cochrane_Database_of_Systematic_Reviews_1_.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Six trials reported their sources of funding (Berg 2014; Dougherty 2015; Piccini 2013; Piotrowicz 2015; Smolis-Bak 2015; Smolis-Bak 2017). Berg 2014 was funded by several foundations, none of which had any conflicts of interest in the trial. Dougherty 2015 was funded by the National Institutes of Health, National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute. Piccini 2013 was founded by several grants, e.g. from companies that produce ICDs. Piotrowicz 2015 was funded by the National Science Centre, Poland. Smolis-Bak 2015 and Smolis-Bak 2017 were funded by the State Committee for Scientific Research. The rest of the included trials did not report on their source of funding (Belardinelli 2006; Frizelle 2004). Lichtman JH, Bigger JT Jr, Blumenthal JA, Frasure-Smith N, Kaufman PG, Lespérance F, et al. Depression and coronary heart disease: recommendations for screening, referral, and treatment: a science advisory from the American Heart Association Prevention Committee of the Council on Cardiovascular Nursing, Council on Clinical Cardiology, Council on Epidemiology and Prevention, and Interdisciplinary Council on Quality of Care and Outcomes Research: endorsed by the American Psychiatric Association. Circulation 2008;118(17):1768-75. Trial acronym: COPE-ICD, CopenHeart and DanRehab. These trials are funded by the Danish government and by external funds. The funders have no influence on the research. • This project was supported by the National Institute for Health Research (NIHR) via Cochrane Infrastructure, Cochrane Programme Grant or Cochrane Incentive funding to the Cochrane Heart Group. The views and opinions expressed therein are those of the authors and do not necessarily reflect those of the Systematic Reviews Programme, NIHR, National Health Service (NHS) or the Department of Health, UK. • The Cochrane Heart Group US Satellite is supported by intramural support from the Northwestern University Feinberg School of Medicine and the Northwestern University Clinical and Translational Science (NUCATS) Institute (UL1TR000150), USA."],"id":"S02-087.09-666849","identifier":[{"id":"PMC6371011","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"PMC6953352","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85061379808","type":"scopus"},{"id":"30746679","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-59f1c266-3188-4f46-b010-b8e8eccaab87","sdu-d26312d5-7905-499a-81b4-8dda53d35316"],"keyword":["Aged","Cardiac Rehabilitation/adverse effects","Cause of Death","Defibrillators, Implantable/adverse effects","Exercise Therapy","Exercise Tolerance","Female","Humans","Male","Middle Aged","Oxygen Consumption","Quality of Life","Randomized Controlled Trials as Topic"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-68CB4549079822079666C60187FB152C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/174073998/CD011828.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/245617888/Nielsen_et_al_2019_Cochrane_Database_of_Systematic_Reviews_1_.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of exeter","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Exeter","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Internal medicine: Cardiology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Regional Health Research, IRS - Holbæk Hospital, Research Unit of Medicine (Holbæk)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kim M.","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Kim M.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ann Dorthe","key":"zwislerpau","last":"Zwisler","name":"Zwisler, Ann Dorthe","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Rod S.","key":"taylorpau","last":"Taylor","name":"Taylor, Rod S.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 1","first":"Jesper H.","key":"svendsenpau","last":"Svendsen","locid":"d80910cf-2f9c-457c-9e85-34b4b258bb81","name":"Svendsen, Jesper Hastrup","name_org":"Svendsen, Jesper H.","orcid":"0000-0001-8466-8515","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Jane","key":"lindschoupau","last":"Lindschou","name":"Lindschou, Jane","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Lindsey","key":"andersonpau","last":"Anderson","name":"Anderson, Lindsey","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4 1","corresponding":null,"first":"Janus C.","key":"jakobsenpau","last":"Jakobsen","locid":"06691aa2-020b-4869-a6d9-56ac223008cc","name":"Jakobsen, Janus Christian","name_org":"Jakobsen, Janus C.","orcid":"0000-0002-3642-2120","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 1","first":"Selina K.","key":"bergpau","last":"Berg","locid":"92d15fb6-c29f-4d38-88a4-53d28ecc9a13","name":"Berg, Selina Kikkenborg","name_org":"Berg, Selina K.","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["30746679"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1002/14651858.cd011828.pub2","issn":["13616137","1469493X"],"issn_org":["1469493X"],"issue":"2","journal":"Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews","paperid":"CD011828","publisher":"Wiley","status":"p","title":"Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews","volume":"2019"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85061379808"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-59f1c266-3188-4f46-b010-b8e8eccaab87","sdu-d26312d5-7905-499a-81b4-8dda53d35316"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Exercise-based cardiac rehabilitation for adult patients with an implantable cardioverter defibrillator","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-087.09-666849","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Artiklen belyser relationer mellem unge talentfulde atleter og deres trænere. Ved at læse på tværs af interviewmateriale med unge idrætstalenter finder vi tre former for nøglerelationer trænere kan have i talentudviklingen, henholdsvis som gatekeepere, ressourcedistributører og partnere. Analyserne viser, at trænere indtager en nøgleposition i talentudvikling og talentudviklingsmiljøer, hvor de har en central betydning for tilblivelsen af unge talenter. Det diskuteres om den nøgleposition i talentudviklingen også indebærer et etisk ansvar for dem, der ikke kommer gennem gaten, får ressourcer distribueret eller opnår et partnerskab.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Nielsen, Jens Christian","Skrubbeltrang, Lotte Stausgaard","Olesen, Jesper Stilling"],"cuid":["S02-093.18-007335"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Elitesport","Etik","Gatekeeping","Talent","Talentudvikling","Trænerrelationer","talenttilblivelse"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum","med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"træneren som gatekeeper ressourcedistributør og partner for idrætstalenter magt og etik i talentudviklingens relationer","src":["aau","au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 3.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/307059301/tr_neren_som_gatekeeper_ressourcedistribut_r_og_partner_for_idr_tstalenter.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-093.18-007335","ids":["aau-97393070-7d94-4002-9383-56dac386af1f","au-8c087763-2a9b-4280-8d22-20f68c3fbc96"],"keyword":["Elitesport","Gatekeeping","Talent","Talentudvikling","Trænerrelationer","atlet-træner-relationer","etik","forsvarlighed i talentudvikling","ressourcedistribueri