{"abstract":"Speech intelligibility is often severely degraded among hearing impaired individuals in situations such as the cocktail party scenario. The performance of the current hearing aid technology has been observed to be limited in these scenarios. In this paper, we propose a binaural speech enhancement framework that takes into consideration the speech production model. The enhancement framework proposed here is based on the Kalman filter that allows us to take the speech production dynamics into account during the enhancement process. The usage of a Kalman filter requires the estimation of clean speech and noise short term predictor (STP) parameters, and the clean speech pitch parameters. In this work, a binaural codebook-based method is proposed for estimating the STP parameters, and a directional pitch estimator based on the harmonic model and maximum likelihood principle is used to estimate the pitch parameters. The proposed method for estimating the STP and pitch parameters jointly uses the information from left and right ears, leading to a more robust estimation of the filter parameters. Objective measures such as PESQ and STOI have been used to evaluate the enhancement framework in different acoustic scenarios representative of the cocktail party scenario. We have also conducted subjective listening tests on a set of nine normal hearing subjects, to evaluate the performance in terms of intelligibility and quality improvement. The listening tests show that the proposed algorithm, even with access to only a single channel noisy observation, significantly improves the overall speech quality, and the speech intelligibility by up to 15%.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Kavalekalam, Mathew Shaji","Nielsen, Jesper Kjær","Boldt, Jesper Bünsow","Christensen, Mads Græsbøll"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-106410"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Autoregressive model","Kalman filter","Pitch estimation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"model based speech enhancement for intelligibility improvement in binaural hearing aids","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/306356859/Model_based_speech_enhancement_for_Intelligibility_improvement_in_binaural_hearing_aids.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-106410","identifier":[{"id":"85054391475","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-c241c8e3-266f-435f-8b39-f95fa0952679"],"keyword":["Kalman filter","autoregressive model","binaural enhancement","pitch estimation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-77CF1311FE19A978D097A075CC5FC15B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/306356859/Model_based_speech_enhancement_for_Intelligibility_improvement_in_binaural_hearing_aids.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, Audio Analysis Lab (CREATE)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mathew Shaji","key":"kavalekalampau","last":"Kavalekalam","locid":"6082dcd3-6e02-459e-8b51-2ac06555e654","name":"Kavalekalam, Mathew Shaji","orcid":"0000-0003-3124-3008","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jesper Kjær","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"3d21c73c-3aa1-4590-870f-8be578634702","name":"Nielsen, Jesper Kjær","orcid":"0000-0002-2586-7925","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Jesper Bünsow","key":"boldtpau","last":"Boldt","name":"Boldt, Jesper Bünsow","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mads Græsbøll","key":"christensenpau","last":"Christensen","locid":"cf9e922d-e5f8-4964-9b84-581667f97b0b","name":"Christensen, Mads Græsbøll","orcid":"0000-0003-3586-7969","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/taslp.2018.2872128","issn":["23299290"],"issue":"1","journal":"IEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing","pages":"99-113","paperid":"8472176","publisher":"IEEE Advancing Technology for Humanity","status":"p","title":"IEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing","volume":"27"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85054391475"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-c241c8e3-266f-435f-8b39-f95fa0952679"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Model-based Speech Enhancement for Intelligibility Improvement in Binaural Hearing Aids","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-106410","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Rainfall-runoff-generating mechanisms in urban green areas are scarcely understood, and limited knowledge and data on rainfall-runoff processes are available. Therefore, a large-scale experimental field station was established to investigate the inherent hydrological processes of a grass-covered 4,300  m2 urban catchment consisting of sandy loam soil. A facility to collect surface runoff from the area was designed. Runoff, soil moisture properties, and rainfall were measured simultaneously by a flow meter, in-ground soil sensors, and rain gauges, respectively. Measured soil volumetric water content was above 0.34  m3 H2O m−3 soil during fall and winter and ranging between 0.13 and 0.34  m3 H2O m−3 soil during late spring and summer. Measured runoff recorded from September 2016 until July 2018 strongly indicates that subsurface throughflow was the dominant runoff type. There was good correlation between the dynamics of soil water content and runoff. Accumulated rainfall and runoff was linearly correlated for soil volumetric water contents above 0.34  m3 H2O m−3 soil. The relationship between runoff and rainfall shows a runoff coefficient of 0.18 for the 4,300  m2 area.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Nielsen, Kristoffer","Møldrup, Per","Thorndahl, Søren Liedtke","Nielsen, Jesper Ellerbæk","Uggerby, Mads","Rasmussen, Michael R."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-121065"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Infiltration","Permeable","Pervious surface","Rainfall runoff from green areas","Subsurface throughflow","Urban drainage"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"field scale monitoring of urban green area rainfall runoff processes","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1061/(ASCE)HE.1943-5584.0001795","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-121065","identifier":[{"id":"85066807292","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-f44859f8-f68a-4436-aa23-79c88ab4bfd3"],"keyword":["Infiltration","Permeable","Pervious surface","Rainfall-runoff from green areas","Subsurface throughflow","Urban drainage"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-06CDAB04BC7184C29753C38BE3F9CD07"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/307053953/Field_Scale_Monitoring_of_Urban_Green_Area_Rainfall_Runoff_Processes.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1061/(ASCE)HE.1943-5584.0001795","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science, Department of Civil Engineering","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science, Department of Civil Engineering, Division of Water and Environment (CIVIL)","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science, Department of Civil Engineering, Urban Water Research Group (CIVIL)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University"],"nora_code":"DI-U