{"abstract":"Edu-larp can be described as implementing live-action role-playing games in formal or informal educational contexts, “used to impart pre-determined pedagogical or didactic content” (Balzer & Kurz 2015). The aim of the Edu-larp Conference 2019 was to present and discuss recent international research as well as share best practice examples or innovative formats of edu-larp.The first edu-larp conference was held in 2014 in Sweden, organized by Carolina Dahlberg. From single workshops at the Nordic Larp Conferences Knudepunkt dealing with edu-larp, Carolina took to actually making a separate conference, taking place before the main event. In consecutive years, the edu-larp conference has been running every year since and will again in Finland 2020. Edu-larp conferences have also been hosted in the United States in a similar setup as the ones in the Nordic countries. They have taken place the day before the Living Games Conference, the US larpers’ version of Knudepunkt, and were hosted in 2016 (Austin, Texas) and 2018 (Boston, Massachussetts). The Edu-larp Conference 2019 was held at Ungdomshuset in Odense (Denmark) on 7 February 2019 from 9-17. The organizers (Muriel Algayres, Charles Bo Nielsen, and Katrin Geneuss) had sent out an open call for papers via social media and all proposals were accepted. There were eight presentations in total, spanning research and development projects, as well as reports on specific ways of facilitating edu-larps and of implementing future projects. Furthermore, three workshops were held (described below) and the program included an optional trip to Efterskolen Epos, a boarding school that uses edu-larp as one of its teaching tools. This article contains summaries of presentations, workshops, and the trip to Efterskolen Epos.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Geneuss, Katrin","Bruun, Jesper","Bo Nielsen, Charles"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-474645"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Ludwig Maximilian University of Munich"],"keyword":["Conference report","Education","Faculty of Science","Roleplay"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"overview of edu larp conference 000000002019","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"doi","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://nordiclarp.org/2019/12/27/edu-larp-conference-2019/","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-474645","ids":["ku-87c6af4b-41f2-4801-929d-26e7cd6d130a"],"keyword":["Conference report","Faculty of Science","education","edularp","roleplay"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-437582ABF7A77C34146B129A7CA4ADC8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://nordiclarp.org/2019/12/27/edu-larp-conference-2019/","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"ludwig maximilian university of munich","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Ludwig Maximilian University of Munich","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Science Education","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Science Education, Assessment, Teaching and Learning in Science Education","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Science Education, Center for Digital Education"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Katrin","key":"geneusspau","last":"Geneuss","name":"Geneuss, Katrin","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Jesper","key":"bruunpau","last":"Bruun","locid":"d2218073-20b7-41ac-aa88-f7c4bc9fff74","name":"Bruun, Jesper","orcid":"0000-0003-4739-7351","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Charles","key":"bonielsenpau","last":"Bo Nielsen","name":"Bo Nielsen, Charles","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"X"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Undefined"]},"sid":["ku-87c6af4b-41f2-4801-929d-26e7cd6d130a"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Overview of Edu-Larp Conference 2019","type":"Other-Internet Publication","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-474645","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Optimal surface methods are a class of graph cut methods posing surface estimation as an n-ary ordered labeling problem. They are used in medical imaging to find interacting and layered surfaces optimally and in low order polynomial time. Representing continuous surfaces with discrete sets of labels, however, leads to discretization errors and, if graph representations are made dense, excessive memory usage. Limiting memory usage and computation time of graph cut methods are important and graphs that locally adapt to the problem has been proposed as a solution. Min-marginal energies computed using dynamic graph cuts offer a way to estimate solution uncertainty and these uncertainties have been used to decide where graphs should be adapted. Adaptive graphs, however, introduce extra parameters, complexity, and heuristics.We propose a way to use min-marginal energies to estimate continuous solution labels that does not introduce extra parameters and show empirically on synthetic and medical imaging datasets that it leads to improved accuracy. The increase in accuracy was consistent and in many cases comparable to accuracy otherwise obtained with graphs up to 8 times denser, but with proportionally less memory usage and improvements in computation time.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Petersen, Jens","Arias-Lorza, Andres M","Selvan, Raghavendra","Bos, Daniel","van der Lugt, Aad","Pedersen, Jesper H","Nielsen, Mads","de Bruijne, Marleen"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-498012"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"increasing accuracy of optimal surfaces using min marginal energies","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-498012","iden