,"name":"Tekie, H.","rank":24,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"T.","key":"thorsteinssonpau","last":"Thorsteinsson","name":"Thorsteinsson, T.","rank":25,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"H.","key":"vennemopau","last":"Vennemo","name":"Vennemo, H.","rank":26,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jørgen","key":"brandtpau","last":"Brandt","locid":"5f570c62-a5a5-4760-b7c7-8755b6e37ebf","name":"Brandt, Jørgen","orcid":"0000-0002-7580-9547","rank":27,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-d3767908-9222-4ad0-ac3e-38e2100bd7b3"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"The first high resolution integrated assessment of air pollution and related health impacts across the Nordic region","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-250101","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Non-malleable codes for the split-state model allow to encode a message into two parts, such that arbitrary independent tampering on each part, and subsequent decoding of the corresponding modified codeword, yields either the same as the original message, or a completely unrelated value. Continuously non-malleable codes further allow to tolerate an unbounded (polynomial) number of tampering attempts, until a decoding error happens. The drawback is that, after an error happens, the system must self-destruct and stop working, otherwise generic attacks become possible. In this paper we propose a solution to this limitation, by leveraging a split-state refreshing procedure. Namely, whenever a decoding error happens, the two parts of an encoding can be locally refreshed (i.e., without any interaction), which allows to avoid the self-destruct mechanism. An additional feature of our security model is that it captures directly security against continual leakage attacks. We give an abstract framework for building such codes in the common reference string model, and provide a concrete instantiation based on the external Diffie-Hellman assumption. Finally, we explore applications in which our notion turns out to be essential. The first application is a signature scheme tolerating an arbitrary polynomial number of split-state tampering attempts, without requiring a self-destruct capability, and in a model where refreshing of the memory happens only after an invalid output is produced. This circumvents an impossibility result from a recent work by Fuijisaki and Xagawa (Asiacrypt 2016). The second application is a compiler for tamper-resilient RAM programs. In comparison to other tamper-resilient compilers, ours has several advantages, among which the fact that, for the first time, it does not rely on the self-destruct feature.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Faonio, Antonio","Nielsen, Jesper Buus","Simkin, Mark","Venturi, Daniele"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-267394"],"event":{"acronym":"ACNS","end":"2018-07-04","place":"Leuven, be","start":"2018-07-02","title":"The 16th International Conference on Applied Cryptography and Network Security","year":"2018"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Italy","Spain"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","IMDEA Software Institute","University of Rome La Sapienza"],"keyword":["Non malleable codes","Tamper resilient cryptography"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"continuously non malleable codes with split state refresh","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85049100561","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-267394","identifier":[{"id":"85049100561","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-f1fd60b5-5f21-484c-9eba-70ab8cfd606e"],"keyword":["Non-malleable codes","Tamper-resilient cryptography"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-A9FE6E84D082C732C73E74CC9D613216"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"es","key":"imdea software institute","loc":["Spain","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"IMDEA Software Institute","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"it","key":"university of rome la sapienza","loc":["Italy","Europe, EU","OECD"],"na