425","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Jesper V.","key":"olsenpau","last":"Olsen","locid":"16f5cd56-244f-4fe8-bebd-13ee7bd17822","name":"Olsen, Jesper V.","orcid":"0000-0002-4747-4938","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Blagoy","key":"blagoevpau","last":"Blagoev","locid":"0c38f8ce-49bc-47b9-90b6-4eb233ccfc5f","name":"Blagoev, Blagoy","orcid":"0000-0002-3596-0066","rank":14,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["29967540"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41594-018-0084-y","issn":["15459985","15459993"],"issn_org":["15459993"],"issue":"7","journal":"Nature Structural and Molecular Biology","pages":"631-640","publisher":"Nature Research","status":"p","title":"Nature Structural and Molecular Biology","volume":"25"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85049563460"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-8b6c3aa1-5ba2-4dc0-87f4-8a17c284de68","sdu-e2fc9188-461b-4cd2-9f3b-e68d0d85adc6"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"UbiSite approach for comprehensive mapping of lysine and N-terminal ubiquitination sites","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-099.57-044226","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Purpose: The present study evaluated the effects of regular participation in small-sided team handball training on body composition, osteogenic response, physical performance, and cardiovascular risk factors, as well as well-being and motivation, in young untrained women.  Methods: Twenty-eight untrained 20- to 30-year-old women were randomized to a handball training group (HG; n = 14, height 170 ± 5 cm, weight 73 ± 11 kg, VO2peak 37.7 ± 4.1 mL/min/kg) that trained 1.7 ± 0.3 time per week over 12 weeks (70 min 4v4 handball sessions) or an inactive control group (CG; n = 14, 169 ± 5 cm, 71 ± 12 kg, 38.1 ± 3.7 mL/min/kg). Physiological and psychological and motivational training adaptations were assessed pre- and post-intervention by Dual-Energy X-ray Absorptiometry (DXA) scans, blood sampling, physical tests, and questionnaires.  Results: The average heart rate (HR) over all training sessions was equal to 85% ± 6% HRmax. Between-group intervention effects were observed in favor of HG for muscle mass (2.1%, p = 0.024), proximal femur bone mineral density (0.8%, p = 0.041), Yo-Yo IE1 intermittent endurance test level 1 (IE1) performance (35%, p < 0.001), and incremental treadmill test performance (11.5%, p = 0.003), but not total fat mass (p = 0.176), mean arterial blood pressure (p = 0.328), resting heart rate (p = 0.219), or blood lipids (p = 0.298-0.854). In CG, no changes were observed in any of the measured physiological variables after the training period. Compared to CG, HG had an increase in intrinsic motivation (p < 0.001) and in the well-being subscale \"energy\" (p = 0.010).  Conclusion: Participation in regular recreational team handball training organized as small-sided games has marked beneficial effects on physical performance, musculoskeletal fitness, well-being, and motivation in untrained young women.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Hornstrup, Therese","Wikman, Johan Michael","Fristrup, Bjørn","Póvoas, Susana","Helge, Eva Wulff","Nielsen, Signe H","Helge, Jørn Wulff","Andersen, Jesper Løvind","Nybo, Lars","Krustrup, Peter"],"cuid":["S02-099.69-040885"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Krustrup, Peter"],"country":["Portugal","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University Institute of Maia","University of Exeter","niversity Institute of Maia"],"keyword":["Bone mineral density BMD","Intensity","Intermittent","Motivation","Muscle mass","Physical performance","Recreational handball","Well being"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"fitness and health benefits of team handball training for young untrained women a cross disciplinary rct on physiological adaptations and motivational aspects","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jshs.2017.09.007","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["We would like to thank all the participants for their committed participation. We would also like to thank Jens Jung Nielsen, Jon Egelund, Mikkel Alleslev Larsen, Frederik Terkildsen Løwenstein, Christian Ritz, and Nadia Quardon for their excellent practical and technical assistance. A special thank goes to TrygFonden , Nordea-fonden ( Nordea-fonden 02-2011-4360 ) and the Danish Handball Federation (Dansk Håndbold Forbund, DHF), as well as the European Handball Federation (EHF) for their collaboration and financial support. The authors declare that this study was funded in part by the Danish and European Handball Federation."],"id":"S02-099.69-040885","identifier":[{"id":"85035808014","type":"scopus"},{"id":"30356492","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC6180563","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-247883e4-e5ad-4eed-a383-a2067d63679a","sdu-123ae4e7-e351-4408-8e1c-f5eb7f6fa8a8"],"keyword":["Bone mineral density (BMD)","Intensity","Intermittent","Motivation","Muscle mass","Physical performance","Recreational handball","Well-being"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-D1CA219F5ABA34C50EE5E920A6F85DEE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/141579304/1_s2.0_S2095254617301187_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/196348562/Hornstrup_et_al_Journal_of_Sport_and_Health_Science_2018_Vol_7_2_139_148.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jshs.2017.09.007","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Nutrition, Exercise and Sports, The August Krogh Section for Human Physiology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"pt","key":"niversity institute of maia","loc":["Portugal","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"niversity Institute of Maia","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"pt","key":"university institute of maia","loc":["Portugal","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University Institute of Maia","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","