{"abstract":"OBJECTIVE: To investigate whether children and adolescents exhibiting an impaired glucose metabolism are more obese at treatment entry and less likely to reduce their degree of obesity during treatment. METHODS: The present study is a longitudinal observational study, including children and adolescents from the Children's Obesity Clinic, Holbaek, Denmark. Anthropometrics, pubertal development, socioeconomic status (SES), and fasting concentrations of plasma glucose, serum insulin, serum C-peptide, and whole blood glycosylated hemoglobin (HbA1c) were collected at treatment entry and at follow-up. Proxies of Homeostasis Model Assessment 2-insulin sensitivity (HOMA2-IS) and Homeostasis Model Assessment 2-β-cell function (HOMA2-B) were calculated with the Homeostasis Model Assessment 2 program. RESULTS: In total, 569 (333 boys) patients, median 11.5 years of age (range 6-22 years), and median body mass index (BMI) z-score 2.94 (range 1.34-5.54) were included. The mean BMI z-score reduction was 0.31 (±0.46) after 13 months (range 6-18) of treatment. At treatment entry, patients with impaired estimates of glucose metabolism were more obese than normoglycemic patients. Baseline concentration of C-peptide was associated with a lower weight loss during treatment in girls (P = .02). Reduction in the insulin concentrations was associated with reduction in BMI z-score in both sexes (P < .0001, P = .0005). During treatment, values of glucose, HbA1c, HOMA2-IS, and HOMA2-B did not change or impact the treatment outcome, regardless of age, sex, SES, or degree of obesity at treatment entry. CONCLUSION: The capability to reduce weight during multidisciplinary treatment in children and adolescents with overweight/obesity is not influenced by an impaired glucose metabolism at study entry or during the course of treatment.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Kloppenborg, Julie T.","Gamborg, Michael","Fonvig, Cilius Esmann","Nielsen, Tenna Ruest Haarmark","Pedersen, Oluf","Johannesen, Jesper","Hansen, Torben","Holm, Jens-Christian"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-040166"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Adolescent","Blood Glucose","Body Mass Index","C Peptide blood","Child","Children","Female","Glucose Intolerance blood","Glycated Hemoglobin A metabolism","Humans","Impaired glucose metabolism","Insulin blood","Journal Article","Longitudinal Studies","Male","Obesity","Pediatric Obesity blood","Prediabetes","Prediabetic State blood","Weight Reduction Programs statistics numerical data","Weight loss","Young Adult"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-ZEA"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the effect of impaired glucose metabolism on weight loss in multidisciplinary childhood obesity treatment","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85034639354","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-040166","identifier":[{"id":"85034639354","type":"scopus"},{"id":"29159854","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-0ed2f446-e7c3-4545-8b49-ae74d7a3195a","sdu-f2bd12e1-9b71-46e3-bb8b-c94471b83679"],"keyword":["Adolescent","Blood Glucose","Body Mass Index","C-Peptide/blood","Child","Female","Glucose Intolerance/blood","Glycated Hemoglobin A/metabolism","Humans","Insulin/blood","Journal Article","Longitudinal Studies","Male","Pediatric Obesity/blood","Prediabetic State/blood","Weight Loss","Weight Reduction Programs/statistics & numerical data","Young Adult","children","impaired glucose metabolism","obesity","prediabetes"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-AF3