{"abstract":"OBJECTIVE: To test serum tau as a predictor of neurological outcome after cardiac arrest. METHODS: We measured the neuronal protein tau in serum at 24, 48, and 72 hours after cardiac arrest in 689 patients in the prospective international Target Temperature Management trial. The main outcome was poor neurological outcome, defined as Cerebral Performance Categories 3-5 at 6 months. RESULTS: Increased tau was associated with poor outcome at 6 months after cardiac arrest (median = 38.5, interquartile range [IQR] = 5.7-245ng/l in poor vs median = 1.5, IQR = 0.7-2.4ng/l in good outcome, for tau at 72 hours, p < 0.0001). Tau improved prediction of poor outcome compared to using clinical information (p < 0.0001). Tau cutoffs had low false-positive rates (FPRs) for good outcome while retaining high sensitivity for poor outcome. For example, tau at 72 hours had FPR = 2% (95% CI = 1-4%) with sensitivity = 66% (95% CI = 61-70%). Tau had higher accuracy than serum neuron-specific enolase (NSE; the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve was 0.91 for tau vs 0.86 for NSE at 72 hours, p = 0.00024). During follow-up (up to 956 days), tau was significantly associated with overall survival. The accuracy in predicting outcome by serum tau was equally high for patients randomized to 33 °C and 36 °C targeted temperature after cardiac arrest. INTERPRETATION: Serum tau is a promising novel biomarker for prediction of neurological outcome in patients with cardiac arrest. It may be significantly better than serum NSE, which is recommended in guidelines and currently used in clinical practice in several countries to predict outcome after cardiac arrest. Ann Neurol 2017;82:665-675.","author_count":18,"contributors":["Mattsson, Niklas","Zetterberg, Henrik","Nielsen, Niklas","Blennow, Kaj","Dankiewicz, Josef","Friberg, Hans","Lilja, Gisela","Insel, Philip S","Rylander, Christian","Stammet, Pascal","Aneman, Anders","Hassager, Christian","Kjaergaard, Jesper","Kuiper, Michael","Pellis, Tommaso","Wetterslev, Jørn","Wise, Matthew","Cronberg, Tobias"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-487740"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Journal Article"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"serum tau and neurological outcome in cardiac arrest","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/ana.25067","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-487740","identifier":[{"id":"PMC5725735","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85034417608","type":"scopus"},{"id":"28981963","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-9dc22c53-d593-41d8-9663-059373f2b5c7"],"keyword":["Journal Article"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-A7EDCB8B7651580458B77FC97ACE0ED1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/ana.25067","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/185993680/Mattsson_et_al_2017_Annals_of_Neurology.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2017","yearSub":"2017"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Internal medicine: Cardiology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Niklas","key":"mattssonpau","last":"Mattsson","name":"Mattsson, Niklas","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Henrik","key":"zetterbergpau","last":"Zetterberg","name":"Zetterberg, Henrik","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Niklas","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Niklas","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Kaj","key":"blennowpau","last":"Blennow","name":"Blennow, Kaj","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Josef","key":"dankiewiczpau","last":"Dankiewicz","name":"Dankiewicz, Josef","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Hans","key":"fribergpau","last":"Friberg","name":"Friberg, Hans","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Gisela","key":"liljapau","last":"Lilja","name":"Lilja, Gisela","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Philip S","key":"inselpau","last":"Insel","name":"Insel, Philip S","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Christian","key":"rylanderpau","last":"Rylander","name":"Rylander, Christian","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Pascal","key":"stammetpau","last":"Stammet","name":"Stammet, Pascal","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Anders","key":"anemanpau","last":"Aneman","name":"Aneman, Anders","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Christian","key":"hassagerpau","last":"Hassager","locid":"49196e5d-0f61-44b2-a6b9-cd6c5f8f3bc2","name":"Hassager, Christian","orcid":"0000-0002-1199-0981","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Jesper","key":"kjaergaardpau","last":"Kjaergaard","name":"Kjaergaard, Jesper","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Michael","key":"kuiperpau","last":"Kuiper","name":"Kuiper, Michael","rank":14,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Tommaso","key":"pellispau","last":"Pellis","name":"Pellis, Tommaso","rank":15,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Jørn","key":"wetterslevpau","last":"Wetterslev","name":"Wetterslev, Jørn","rank":16,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Matthew","key":"wisepau","last":"Wise","name":"Wise, Matthew","rank":17,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Tobias","key":"cronbergpau","last":"Cronberg","name":"Cronberg, Tobias","rank":18,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["28981963"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1002/ana.25067","issn":["03645134","15318249"],"issn_org":["03645134"],"issue":"5","journal":"Annals of Neurology","pages":"665-675","status":"p","title":"Annals of Neurology","volume":"82"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85034417608"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-9dc22c53-d593-41d8-9663-059373f2b5c7"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Serum tau and neurological outcome in cardiac arrest","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-487740","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"Objective: The ability to acquire new motor skills is essential both during childhood and later in life. Recent studies have demonstrated that an acute bout of exercise can improve motor memory consolidation in adults. The objective of the present study was to investigate whether acute exercise protocols following motor skill practice in a school setting can also improve long-term retention of motor memory in preadolescent children. Methods: Seventy-seven pre-adolescent children (age 10.5 ± 0.75 (SD)) participated in the study. Prior to the main experiment age, BMI, fitness status and general physical activity level was assessed in all children and they were then randomly allocated to three groups. All children practiced a visuomotor tracking task followed by 20 min of rest (CON), high intensity intermittent floorball (FLB) or running (RUN) with comparable exercise intensity and duration for exercise groups. Delayed retention of motor memory was assessed 1 h, 24 h and 7 days after motor skill acquisition. Results: During skill acquisition, motor performance improved significantly to the immediate retention test with no differences between groups. One hour following skill acquisition, motor performance decreased significantly for RUN. Twenty-four hours following skill acquisition there was a tendency towards improved performance for FLB but no significant effects. Seven days after motor practice however, both FLB and RUN performed better when compared to their immediate retention test indicating significant offline gains. This effect was not observed for CON. In contrast, 7 days after motor practice, retention of motor memory was significantly better for FLB and RUN compared to CON. No differences were observed when comparing FLB and RUN. Conclusions: Acute intense intermittent exercise performed immediately after motor skill acquisition facilitates long-term motor memory in pre-adolescent children, presumably by promoting memory consolidation. The results also demonstrate that the effects can be accomplished in a school setting. The positive effect of both a team game (i.e., FLB) and running indicates that the observed memory improvements are determined to a larger extent by physiological factors rather than the types of movements performed during the exercise protocol.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Lundbye-Jensen, Jesper","Skriver, Kasper Christen","Nielsen, Jens Bo","Roig, Marc"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-496351"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["Canada"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","McGill University"],"keyword":["Children","Consolidation","Exercise","Faculty of Science","Learning","Motor memory","Retention"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"acute exercise improves motor memory consolidation in preadolescent children","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fnhum.2017.00182","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-496351","identifier":[{"id":"28473761","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC5397400","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85018310739","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-ac4d248f-6b60-4155-a977-a2225a3e7d3b"],"keyword":["Children","Consolidation","Exercise","Faculty of Science","Learning","Motor memory","Retention"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-11C1367EC4B9B209D9BBFAD2544623E2"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/176914807/Lundbye_Jensen_et_al_Frontiers_in_Human_Neuroscience_2017_Vol_11_182.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://