b":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Garbled circuits (GC) are one of the main tools for secure two-party computation. One of the most promising techniques for efficiently achieving active-security in the context of GCs is the so called cut-and-choose approach, and the main measure of efficiency in cut-andchoose based protocols is the number of garbled circuits which need to be constructed, exchanged and evaluated. In this paper we investigate the following, natural question: how many garbled circuits are needed to achieve active security? and we show that in the amortized setting (for large enough circuits and number of executions), it is possible to achieve active security while using only a constant number of garbled circuits.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Nielsen, Jesper Buus","Orlandi, Claudio"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-215036"],"event":{"end":"2016-11-03","place":"Beijing, cn","start":"2016-10-31","title":"Fourteenth IACR Theory of Cryptography Conference","year":"2016"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"cross and clean amortized garbled circuits with constant overhead","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84994476446","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-215036","identifier":[{"id":"84994476446","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-96781683-5b73-408d-9cab-8ba27a445940"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-C7FEF378CA4614199610206EBF4B4D18"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jesper Buus","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"f428c4b3-80b3-403a-ada6-9ecb0fad97b2","name":"Nielsen, Jesper Buus","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Claudio","key":"orlandipau","last":"Orlandi","locid":"393c3898-d32f-431e-aea6-ee1e79c535ef","name":"Orlandi, Claudio","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Martin","key":"hirtpedh","last":"Hirt","name":"Hirt, Martin","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Adam","key":"smithpedh","last":"Smith","name":"Smith, Adam","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-662-53641-4_22","eisbn":["9783662536414"],"isbn":["9783662536407"],"issn":["03029743"],"pages":"582-603","place":"Berlin","publisher":"Springer VS","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","title":"Theory of Cryptography - 14th International Conference, TCC 2016-B, Proceedings","volume":"9985"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84994476446"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-96781683-5b73-408d-9cab-8ba27a445940"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Cross and Clean: Amortized Garbled Circuits with Constant Overhead","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-215036","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"We present a new constant round additively homomorphic commitment scheme with (amortized) computational and communication complexity linear in the size of the string committed to. Our scheme is based on the non-homomorphic commitment scheme of Cascudo et al. presented at PKC 2015. However, we manage to add the additive homomorphic property, while at the same time reducing the constants. In fact, when opening a large enough batch of commitments we achieve an amortized communication complexity converging to the length of the message committed to, i.e., we achieve close to rate 1 as the commitment protocol by Garay et al. from Eurocrypt 2014.A main technical improvement over the scheme mentioned above, and other schemes based on using error correcting codes for UC commitment, we develop a new technique which allows to based the extraction property on erasure decoding as opposed to error correction. This allows to use a code with significantly smaller minimal distance and allows to use codes without efficient decoding. Our scheme only relies on standard assumptions. Specifically we require a pseudorandom number generator, a linear error correcting code and an ideal oblivious transfer functionality. Based on this we prove our scheme secure in the Universal Composability (UC) framework against a static and malicious adversary corrupting any number of parties. On a practical note, our scheme improves significantly on the nonhomomorphic scheme of Cascudo et al. Based on their observations in regards to efficiency of using linear error correcting codes for commitments we conjecture that our commitment scheme might in practice be more efficient than all existing constructions of UC commitment, even non-homomorphic constructions and even constructions in the random oracle model. In particular, the amortized price of computing one of our commitments is less than that of evaluating a hash function once.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Trifiletti, Roberto","Nielsen, Jesper Buus","Frederiksen, Tore Kasper","Jakobsen, Thomas Pelle"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-216325"],"event":{"end":"2016-01-13","place":"Tel Aviv, il","start":"2016-01-10","title":"Theory of Cryptography Conference","year":"2016"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Commitments","Minimal assumptions","UC"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GTS","DI-U"],"level3":{"GTS":["DI-GTS-ALL","DI-GTS-ALX"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-GTS-ALX","DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"on the complexity of additively homomorphic uc commitments","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84952690695","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-216325","identifier":[{"id":"84952690695","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-98bd26e6-af3c-484b-a3f1-e1dc154c7aba"],"keyword":["Commitments","Erasure Codes","Homomorphic","Linear Error Correcting Codes","Minimal Assumptions","UC"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-0EBFC4E6ED66276904A5B2A6633D53B0"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"alexandra institute","l2":"DI-GTS","l2n":"GTS Institutes","l3":"DI-GTS-ALX","l3nn":"Alexandra Institute","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Alexandra Institute","namevar":["Alexandra Instituttet A/S"],"nora_code":"DI-GTS-ALX","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Roberto","key":"trifilettipau","last":"Trifiletti","locid":"cb5c1c20-f54d-444d-a37f-c621dcc1c27b","name":"Trifiletti, Roberto","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jesper Buus","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"f428c4b3-80b3-403a-ada6-9ecb0fad97b2","name":"Nielsen, Jesper Buus","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Tore Kasper","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"b96bd502-b384-44c2-9a99-2386d87d22b8","name":"Frederiksen, Tore Kasper","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas P.","key":"jakobsenpau","last":"Jakobsen","locid":"03cea827-2da0-4861-92c1-4162551c38c3","name":"Jakobsen, Thomas Pelle","name_org":"Jakobsen, Thomas P.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Eyal","key":"kushilevitzpedh","last":"Kushilevitz","name":"Kushilevitz, Eyal","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Tal","key":"malkinpedh","last":"Malkin","name":"Malkin, Tal","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-662-49096-9","eisbn":["9783662490969"],"isbn":["9783662490952"],"issn":["03029743"],"pages":"542-565","place":"Springer Berlin Heidelberg","publisher":"Springer VS","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","title":"Theory of Cryptography - 13th International Conference, TCC 2016-A, Proceedings","volume":"9562"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84952690695"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All GTS Institutes","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-GTS","DI-GTS-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","GTS Institutes","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-98bd26e6-af3c-484b-a3f1-e1dc154c7aba"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"On the Complexity of Additively Homomorphic UC Commitments","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https:/