{"abstract":"We investigated whether different definitions of healthcare-associated infection influenced the prevalence, characteristics, and mortality of patients with Staphylococcus aureus bacteremia. With different definitions, the proportion of patients classified as having healthcare-associated S. aureus bacteremia varied substantially and the distribution of patient characteristics was influenced, whereas 30-day mortality remained robust. Infect. Control Hosp. Epidemiol. 2015;00(0):1-4.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Smit, Jesper","Søgaard, Mette","Schønheyder, Henrik Carl","Nielsen, Henrik","Thomsen, Reimar Wernich"],"cuid":["S02-097.59-005335"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"classification of healthcare associated staphylococcus aureus bacteremia influence of different definitions on prevalence patient characteristics and outcome","src":["aau","au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84966359130","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-097.59-005335","identifier":[{"id":"26503397","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84966359130","type":"scopus"},{"id":"000369150400014","type":"wos"}],"ids":["aau-6e059c9f-8b8b-4437-8f23-77bcb12e7a47","au-73a6a671-1cdb-475d-8692-dd2495248d01"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-18E9F8312B57913E9F8C897E52085C66"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital, Department of Clinical Microbiology","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital, Infektionsmedicin [Klinik Medicin]","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital, Klinik Diagnostik","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital, Klinik Medicin"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Aalborg Sygehus"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jesper","key":"smitpau","last":"Smit","locid":"29aa2e66-1f20-4693-9114-5f988949c879","name":"Smit, Jesper","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Mette","key":"søgaardpau","last":"Søgaard","locid":"5550d2b5-320f-4f20-82aa-3bd98f0fe3dd","name":"Søgaard, Mette","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Henrik Carl","key":"schønheyderpau","last":"Schønheyder","locid":"d35b264a-6efc-467d-ac9d-e558542ef8a8","name":"Schønheyder, Henrik Carl","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Henrik Ib","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"f0dd5aa7-1ea7-4567-9595-b60afb486088","name":"Nielsen, Henrik","name_org":"Nielsen, Henrik Ib","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Reimar Wernich","key":"thomsenpau","last":"Thomsen","locid":"36e7af2c-e02f-48d4-b124-0f1bd95b1748","name":"Thomsen, Reimar Wernich","orcid":"0000-0001-9135-3474","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["26503397"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1017/ice.2015.259","issn":["0899823X","15596834"],"issn_org":["0899823X"],"issue":"2","journal":"Infection Control & Hospital Epidemiology","pages":"208-211","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","status":"p","title":"Infection Control & Hospital Epidemiology","volume":"37"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84966359130"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-6e059c9f-8b8b-4437-8f23-77bcb12e7a47","au-73a6a671-1cdb-475d-8692-dd2495248d01"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Classification of Healthcare-Associated Staphylococcus aureus Bacteremia : Influence of Different Definitions on Prevalence, Patient Characteristics, and Outcome","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-097.59-005335","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Among police officers in Denmark, we studied (i) how many consecutive night shifts participants preferred at baseline; (ii) preferences regarding three intervention conditions (two, four, and seven consecutive night shifts followed by the same number of days off/day shifts: '2 + 2', '4 + 4', '7 + 7') at follow-up; (iii) characteristics of participants preferring each of these intervention conditions. Questionnaire data from a crossover intervention study were used (baseline: n = 73; follow-up: n = 68). At baseline, 49% preferred four consecutive night shifts. At follow-up, 57% preferred '4 + 4', 26% preferred '2 + 2' and 26% preferred '7 + 7'. Participants, who preferred longer spells of night work experienced that night work was less demanding, found it easier to sleep at different times of the day, and were more frequently evening types compared with participants who preferred shorter spells of night work. The participants' preferences are likely to be influenced by their previous shift work experience. Practitioner Summary: We investigated police officers' preferences regarding the number of consecutive night shifts. The majority preferred four consecutive night shifts. Those who preferred the longer spells of night work found night work less demanding, found it easier to sleep at different times of the day, and were more frequently evening types.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Nabe-Nielsen, Kirsten","Jensen, Marie Aarrebo","Hansen, Åse Marie","Kristiansen, Jesper","Garde, Anne Helene"],"cuid":["S02-099.90-768971"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"level":"sci","mra":["med","soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-WORK"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-WORK","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"what is the preferred number of consecutive night shifts results from a crossover intervention study among police officers in denmark","src":["ku","nfa"],"src-code":["DI-GV-WORK","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84961203947","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-099.90-768971","identifier":[{"id":"84961203947","type":"scopus"},{"id":"26947135","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-798621b5-640f-42f2-93ba-0f33c087d7d7","nfa-522860e7-3fde-4c35-9340-d7beb0fca68c"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-565E5B404761A0C2DD0710F6B5464DF6"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Public Health, Section of Social Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"nfa national research centre for the working environment","l2":"DI-GV","l2n":"Governmental Institutions","l3":"DI-GV-WORK","l3nn":"NFA National Research Centre for the Working Environment","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"NFA National Research Centre for the Working Environment","namevar":["Det Nationale Forskningscenter for Arbejdsmiljø"],"nora_code":"DI-GV-WORK","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Kirsten","key":"nabenielsenpau","last":"Nabe-Nielsen","locid":"266b247f-d127-46ae-a6ab-aa16c3f010b9","name":"Nabe-Nielsen, Kirsten","orcid":"0000-0002-4298-1420","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Marie Aarrebo","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","locid":"56d55b14-b9d2-4886-8819-0a395b08846a","name":"Jensen, Marie Aarrebo","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","first":"Åse Marie","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","locid":"5b151ef4-abb8-4673-9522-abc399e62fea","name":"Hansen, Åse Marie","orcid":"0000-0002-4075-3918","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Jesper","key":"kristiansenpau","last":"Kristiansen","locid":"2b90a97c-15d6-468c-a8d6-1362e731d2bc","name":"Kristiansen, Jesper","orcid":"0000-0002-1637-8900","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Anne Helene","key":"gardepau","last":"Garde","locid":"8e894baf-467c-45d3-8abe-41a805b30b22","name":"Garde, Anne Helene","orcid":"0000-0002-1553-2291","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["26947135"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1080/00140139.2015.1136698","issn":["00140139","13665847"],"issn_org":["00140139"],"issue":"10","journal":"Ergonomics","pages":"1392-1402","publisher":"Taylor and Francis Ltd.","status":"p","title":"Ergonomics","volume":"59"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84961203947"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","NFA National Research Centre for the Working Environment","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-798621b5-640f-42f2-93ba-0f33c087d7d7","nfa-522860e7-3fde-4c35-9340-d7beb0fca68c"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","NFA National Research Centre for the Working Environment"],"title":"What is the preferred number of consecutive night shifts? : results from a crossover intervention study among police officers in Denmark","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-099.90-768971","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Objective-Arterial injury stimulates remodeling responses that, when excessive, lead to stenosis. These responses are influenced by integrin signaling in vascular smooth muscle cells (VSMCs). Microfibrillar-associated protein 4 (MFAP4) is an integrin ligand localized to extracellular matrix fibers in the vascular wall. The role of MFAP4 in vascular biology is unknown. We aimed to test the hypothesis that MFAP4 would enhance integrin-dependent VSMC activation. Approach and Results-We produced Mfap4-deficient (Mfap4-/-) mice and performed carotid artery ligation to explore the role of MFAP4 in vascular biology in vivo. Furthermore, we investigated the effects of MFAP4 in neointimal formation ex vivo and in primary VSMC and monocyte cultures in vitro. When challenged with carotid artery ligation, Mfap4-/- mice exhibited delayed neointimal formation, accompanied by early reduction in the number of proliferating medial and neointimal cells, as well as infiltrating leukocytes. Delayed neointimal formation was associated with decreased cross-sectional area of ligated Mfap4-/- carotid arteries resulting in lumen narrowing 28 days after ligation. MFAP4 blockade prohibited the formation of neointimal hyperplasia ex vivo. Moreover, we demonstrated that MFAP4 is a ligand for integrin αVβ3 and mediates VSMC phosphorylation of focal adhesion kinase, migration, and proliferation in vitro. MFAP4-dependent VSMC activation was reversible by treatment with MFAP4-blocking antibodies and inhibitors of focal adhesion kinase and downstream kinases. In addition, we showed that MFAP4 promotes monocyte chemotaxis in integrin αVβ3-dependent manner. Conclusions-MFAP4 regulates integrin αVβ3-induced VSMC proliferation and migration, as well as monocyte chemotaxis, and accelerates neointimal hyperplasia after vascular injury.","author_count":26,"contributors":["Schlosser, Anders","Pilecki, Bartosz","Hemstra, Line E.","Kejling, Karin","Kristmannsdottir, Gudlaug B.","Wulf-Johansson, Helle","Moeller, Jesper B.","Füchtbauer, Ernst-Martin","Nielsen, Ole","Kirketerp-Møller, Katrine","Dubey, Lalit K.","Hansen, Pernille B L","Stubbe, Jane","Wrede, Christoph","Hegermann, Jan","Ochs, Matthias","Rathkolb, Birgit","Schrewe, Anja","Bekeredjian, Raffi","Wolf, Eckhard","Gailus-Durner, Valérie","Fuchs, Helmut","De Angelis, Martin Hrabě","Lindholt, Jes S.","Holmskov, Uffe","Sorensen, Grith L."],"cuid":["S02-099.93-024672"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Sorensen, Grith L."],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Excellence Cluster ORIGINS","German Mouse Clinic","Hannover Medical School","Heidelberg University ","Helmholtz Association of German Research Centres","Ludwig Maximilian University","Technical University of Munich"],"keyword":["Animals","Apoptosis","Carotid Artery Diseases genetics","Carotid Stenosis","Carrier Proteins genetics","Cell Movement drug effects","Cell Proliferation drug effects","Cells Cultured","Chemotaxis Leukocyte","Disease Models Animal","Extracellular Matrix Proteins","Genotype","Glycoproteins deficiency","Humans","Hyperplasia","Ligands","MFAP4 protein","Male","Mice Inbred BALB C","Mice Inbred C57BL","Mice Knockout","Monocytes metabolism","Mouse","Muscle","Muscle Smooth Vascular drug effects","Myocytes Smooth Muscle drug effects","Neointima","Phenotype","Phosphorylation","Protein Kinase Inhibitors pharmacology","Signal Transduction","Smooth","Time Factors","Vascular","Vascular remodeling","integrin alphaVbeta3"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-SDR"],"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"