{"abstract":"The skin is a strong and flexible organ with barrier properties essential for maintaining homeostasis and thereby human life. Characterizing this barrier is the ability to prevent some chemicals from crossing the barrier while allowing others, including medicinal products, to pass at varying rates. During recent decades, the latter has received increased attention as a route for intentionally delivering drugs to patients. This has stimulated research in methods for sampling, measuring and predicting percutaneous penetration. Previous chapters have described how different endogenous, genetic and exogenous factors may affect barrier characteristics. The present chapter introduces the theory for barrier penetration (Fick's law), and describes and discusses different methods for measuring the kinetics of percutaneous penetration of chemicals, including in vitro methods (static and flow-through diffusion cells) as well as in vivo methods (microdialysis and microperfusion). Then follows a discussion with examples of how different characteristics of the skin (age, site and integrity) and of the penetrants (size, solubility, ionization, logPow and vehicles) affect the kinetics of percutaneous penetration. Finally, a short discussion of the advantages and challenges of each method is provided, which will hopefully allow the reader to improve decision making and treatment planning, as well as the evaluation of experimental studies of percutaneous penetration of chemicals.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Nielsen, Jesper Bo","Benfeldt, Eva","Holmgaard, Rikke"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-627253"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Absorption Physicochemical","Diffusion","Diffusion Chambers Culture","Humans","Microdialysis","Models Theoretical","Permeability","Skin Absorption","Skin Physiological Phenomena","Skin blood supply"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"OT":["Regional Research Unit"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"penetration through the skin barrier","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84957643979","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-627253","identifier":[{"id":"84957643979","type":"scopus"},{"id":"26844902","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["sdu-bc8146b2-3886-40fc-8fe3-2780517b4243"],"keyword":["Absorption, Physicochemical","Diffusion","Diffusion Chambers, Culture","Humans","Microdialysis","Models, Theoretical","Permeability","Skin Absorption","Skin Physiological Phenomena","Skin/blood supply"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-5D6D5AD252FD2F537D77471F5B03D954"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Public Health","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Public Health, Research Unit of General Pratice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Regional Research Unit"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jesper Bo","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"75938cc2-43d6-4c8d-a506-6b1c0472a382","name":"Nielsen, Jesper Bo","orcid":"0000-0002-5764-5462","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Eva","key":"benfeldtpau","last":"Benfeldt","name":"Benfeldt, Eva","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Rikke","key":"holmgaardpau","last":"Holmgaard","name":"Holmgaard, Rikke","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["26844902"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1159/000441549","eisbn":["9783318055863"],"isbn":["9783318055856"],"issn":["14215721"],"pages":"103-111","publisher":"Karger","series":"Current Problems in Dermatology","status":"p","title":"Skin Barrier Function","volume":"49"},"review":"No Review","scoID":["2-s2.0-84957643979"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-bc8146b2-3886-40fc-8fe3-2780517b4243"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Penetration through the Skin Barrier","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-627253","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Adherence to antihypertensive medications is suboptimal. Hospital pharmacist interventions including motivational interviewing (MI) might assist in improving adherence in patients with hypertension. For an intervention to be useful, it is important to have tools that can easily identify potential adherence problems. OBJECTIVES: To evaluate process outcomes and patient- and pharmacist-reported outcomes of a pharmacist adherence intervention for hypertensive patients treated in hospital outpatient clinics. Secondly, to determine the agreement between two different adherence metrics: an adherence questionnaire used in the intervention and a prescription-based measure. METHODS: The development of the intervention was based on adherence and behavioral theories and evidence of effective interventions. This included a focused medication review, a patient interview, and follow-up telephone calls. Two tools were used to identify adherence problems: The Drug Adherence Work-up (DRAW) tool and an adherence questionnaire. Process data included drug-related problems (DRPs) with recommendations to the physicians, medication- and lifestyle problems identified at the patient interview, actions taken and time spent on the intervention. RESULTS: In total, 91 DRPs in 8 categories generated recommendations to the physicians; 56 recommendations were generated at the medication review and 35 at the patient interview. At the interview, 421 problems were identified, of which 60% were medication-related and 40% lifestyle-related. In connection with the interview, 528 actions were taken within 8 different categories. MI was a central technique applicable for most problems and was employed in nearly all patients (94%). About half of the patients reported increased focus on lifestyle change, and 21-39% reported increased knowledge, confidence and skills in relation to their medication as well as better quality of life. The pharmacists found that the intervention elements were meaningful pharmacist tasks, and that the DRAW tool was easy to use and helped them focus on addressing reasons for non-adherence. The mean total time spent by the pharmacist per patient was 2 h 14 min (SD 40 min). CONCLUSIONS: A pharmacist-led, multifaceted, tailored adherence intervention was feasible for identifying and addressing a wide range of potential adherence and lifestyle problems. Among the intervention procedures, MI was a central technique, applicable in most types of problems. The questionnaire showed relatively little value for identifying non-adherence. The intervention was well accepted both by the pharmacists and the patients, thereby increasing the likeliness of successful implementation in routine practice.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Hedegaard, Ulla","Hallas, Jesper","Ravn-Nielsen, Lene Vestergaard","Kjeldsen, Lene Juel"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-639717"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Aged","Antihypertensive Agents therapeutic use","Female","Health Knowledge Attitudes Practice","Humans","Hypertension drug therapy","Life Style","Male","Medication Adherence","Medication reconciliation","Middle Aged","Motivational interviewing","Outpatient Clinics Hospital","Patient Outcome Assessment","Pharmacists","Pharmacy Service Hospital","Secondary Care","Surveys and Questionnaires"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"process and patient reported outcomes of a multifaceted medication adherence intervention for hypertensive patients in secondary care","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.sapharm.2015.05.006","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-639717","identifier":[{"id":"26088274","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84959509082","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-e84c325b-24ec-433d-a407-2e3d86f126ad"],"keyword":["Aged","Antihypertensive Agents/therapeutic use","Female","Health Knowledge, Attitudes, Practice","Humans","Hypertension/drug therapy","Life Style","Male","Medication Adherence","Medication Reconciliation","Middle Aged","Motivational Interviewing","Outpatient Clinics, Hospital","Patient Outcome Assessment","Pharmacists","Pharmacy Service, Hospital","Secondary Care","Surveys and Questionnaires"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-FF8695D124CA562352C9235E0591D13F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.sapharm.2015.05.006","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Public Health, Clinical Pharmacology and Pharmacy","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Public Health, Dept. Clinical Biochemistry and Pharmacology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Ulla","key":"hedegaardpau","last":"Hedegaard","locid":"82b24eb2-1cc9-49b6-bd97-07f2cb6a6d2b","name":"Hedegaard, Ulla","orcid":"0000-0002-8958-2187","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jesper","key":"hallaspau","last":"Hallas","locid":"1fc2d4e1-080d-4c53-bd96-63186af28ae9","name":"Hallas, Jesper","orcid":"0000-0002-8097-8708","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lene Vestergaard","key":"ravnnielsenpau","last":"Ravn-Nielsen","name":"Ravn-Nielsen, Lene Vestergaard","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lene Juel","key":"kjeldsenpau","last":"Kjeldsen","locid":"b3d44e3d-440c-41f1-b52d-838b0338bf55","name":"Kjeldsen, Lene Juel","orcid":"0009-0008-2559-9319","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["26088274"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.sapharm.2015.05.006","issn":["15517411","19348150"],"issn_org":["15517411"],"issue":"2","journal":"Research in Social and Administrative Pharmacy","pages":"302-318","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Research in Social and Administrative Pharmacy","volume":"12"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84959509082"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-e84c325b-24ec-433d-a407-2e3d86f126ad"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Process- and patient-reported outcomes of a multifaceted medication adherence intervention for hypertensive patients in secondary care","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-639717","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Phosphorus scarcity requires improved recover and reuse of urban sources; the recycling of this nutrient from sewage sludge has become increasingly important in the last years. Using an innovative electrodialytic process, the present study shows the potential for P separation from Fe and Al precipitated sewage sludge ash using this technique, with a recovery rate of around 70%. Furthermore, heavy metals were removed from the phosphorous fraction, producing a pure and safe phosphorus source in the end.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Parés Viader, Raimon","Jensen, Pernille Erland","Ottosen, Lisbeth M.","Ahrenfeldt, Jesper","Hauggaard-Nielsen, Henrik"],"cuid":["S02-085.94-037292"],"event":{"end":"2015-09-02","place":"Ghent, be","start":"2015-08-30","title":"1st IWA Resource Recovery Conference","year":"2015"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Electrodialysis","Phosphorus","Sewage sludge ash"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-RUC"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-RUC"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"electrodialytic recovery of phosphorus from chemically precipitated sewage sludge ashes","src":["dtu","ruc"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-RUC"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/124961968/Electrodialytic_recovery_of_phosphorus_from_chemically_precipitated_sewage_sludge_ashes.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S02-085.94-037292","ids":["dtu-d059a323-8e43-4f97-a456-03129f0d9440","ruc-e2078901-1bf3-4465-9511-c34d40c41e6a"],"keyword":["Electrodialysis","Phosphorus","Sewage sludge ash"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-2-FFE10D3219601ED7D56CEEE17B129E51"],"oa_link":[{"acc