lborg University"],"title":"Dermal uptake of novel brominated flame retardants (NBFRs) using an ex vivo human skin uptake model.","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-115374","year":"2015","yearsub":"2015"}
{"abstract":"Sometimes it seems that architects do not make architecture for people, but for glossy magazines. Here photographs depict spectacular masterpieces before occupation, only allowing a strictly curated set of elements into the scene. After this, people move in and ‘undo’ architecture: hanging up curtains, lamps and family photographs on the walls; moving in their outof- style furniture and oriental rugs; relocating doors and partitioning walls; leaving traces of their footsteps on the floors. In the course of time, the space becomes theirs. Needless to say, architecture is more than simply two-dimensional images of spectacular shapes and compositions. First and foremost, it is to be occupied and used by people. In fact, the unspectacular and sometimes unregarded spaces (what I define as ‘infraordinary’) are often far more interesting. On a daily basis people coexist and interact through the physical dimension of the city as interface. Architecture itself instigates dialogues between people - it is the spatial structure of our collective existence. People coexist and correlate through deposits over time and events in real-time. This works in multiple scales from the collective memory of neighbourhoods, street names and (extraordinary) monuments to the everyday (non-)events and encounters through stairways, partitioning walls, windows, kiosks, bars, dry cleaners, shop-fronts, bus stops etc. The city is a living archive of present and past inhabitants and events, constantly transforming. As architects we need to engage with both the physical and the social dimension. In fact, the two can hardly be separated: the city and its inhabitants can be considered one organism, constantly shaping each other in a continuous dialogue. The inhabitants are co-authors of space. Hence, the definition of architecture as something superior to the built environment is debateable – and so is the role of the architect. In order to enter these dialogues, we need to immerse ourselves in the actual fabric of the city. We must question the usual tools and techniques of the architect, often applied while sitting behind a desk or drawing table. Here artistic and critical spatial practices can lend themselves to engage directly with the physical reality, beyond reductive representations. At times, we need to invent techniques to by-pass the usual hierarchies and frameworks of perception in order to see what is ‘worn half-invisible by use’ and draw out their inherent latent qualities or potentials, thus making way for spatial invention. These could take many forms: from intelligent aggregates and installations to performance and creative writing, but should all possess a situated agency and site-specific effect. By deploying these in-situ not only do we pose questions, we also simultaneously propose new spatiotemporal potentials and realities. Through using the city as an active laboratory in the scale of 1:1 we can enact a critical engagement with the built environment and its occupants: a dialogue, in which we can alternate between posing questions and answering back.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Lunde Nielsen, Espen"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-124616"],"filters":{"added":"2023-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-ART"],"level3":{"ART":["DI-ART-AAA","DI-ART-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-ART-AAA"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"dialogues the city as archive","src":["arka"],"src-code":["DI-ART-AAA"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-124616","ids":["arka-2973bb03-b0e6-47c9-ab28-5979e8309768"],"keyword":["No"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Local-1-1-8405D303694DAD676015737AA6CAD304"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aarhus school of architecture","l2":"DI-ART","l2n":"Artistic Higher Education Institutions","l3":"DI-ART-AAA","l3nn":"Aarhus School of Architecture","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Aarhus School of Architecture","namevar":["Aarhus School of Architecture","Aarhus School of Architecture, Arkitektonisk eksperiment"],"nora_code":"DI-ART-AAA","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Espen","key":"lundenielsenpau","last":"Lunde Nielsen","locid":"c270782e-46c5-45e4-9c36-1de74949a11b","name":"Lunde Nielsen, Espen","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Andriette","key":"ahrenkielpedh","last":"Ahrenkiel","name":"Ahrenkiel, Andriette","rank":2,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Morten","key":"daugaardpedh","last":"Daugaard","name":"Daugaard, Morten","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Johan","key":"verbekepedh","last":"Verbeke","name":"Verbeke, Johan","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Jesper","key":"rasmussenpedh","last":"Rasmussen","name":"Rasmussen, Jesper","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9788790979515"],"issue":"11","pages":"19-20","place":"Aarhus","series":"ARKIPELAGET","status":"p","title":"Engaging through Architecture"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Artistic Higher Education Institutions","All Danish Affiliations","Artistic Higher Education Institutions","DI-ALL","DI-ART","DI-ART-ALL"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["arka-2973bb03-b0e6-47c9-ab28-5979e8309768"],"src":["Aarhus School of Architecture"],"title":"Dialogues : The City as Archive","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-124616","year":"2015","yearsub":"2015"}
{"abstract":"PURPOSE: To assess the repeatability, reproducibility, and age dependency of dynamic Scheimpflug-based pneumotonometry (Corvis)-generated parameters and their correlation with dynamic bidirectional pneumotonometry device [Ocular Response Analyzer (ORA)] measurements [corneal hysteresis (CH) and corneal resistance factor (CRF)]. METHODS: Eyes of 29 healthy participants between 20 and 30 years old were measured using Corvis and ORA. Measurements were performed twice in a row and repeated after 1 week. Repeatability and reproducibility were examined by calculating intraday and interday coefficients of variation (CVs). Corvis measurements were compared with measurements from 19 participants older than 65 years. RESULTS: The most repeatable and reproducible Corvis-generated parameters, with intraday and interday CVs < 10% were as follows: intraocular pressure (IOP), pachymetry, time to first and second applanation (A1 time, A2 time), radius and amplitude at highest concavity (HC radius and HC deformation amplitude, respectively), time to highest concavity (HC time), deflection length and amplitude at highest concavity [HC deflection length and HC deflection amplitude (millimeter), respectively], and time to highest deflection amplitude [HC deflection amplitude (millisecond)]. However, 7 of 19 analyzed parameters showed CVs > 10%. HC-related parameters showed significant age dependency. The correlation between Corvis and ORA was weak (A2 time and CH, r = 0.36, A1 time and CRF, r = 0.41). Corvis-derived IOP was on average 2.2 ± 2.3 mm Hg higher than cornea-compensated IOP, with large differences in patients with high-average IOP. CONCLUSIONS: The considerable variation in repeatability and reproducibility should be taken into account when using the Corvis. Corvis parameters showed poor correlation with CH and CRF obtained by ORA measurements.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Bak-Nielsen, Sashia","Damgaard, Iben Bach","Ivarsen, Anders","Hjortdal, Jesper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-133257"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Adult","Aged","Aged 80 and over","Aging","Cornea","Elasticity","Humans","Intraocular Pressure","Reproducibility of Results","Tonometry Ocular","Young Adult"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"repeatability reproducibility and age dependency of dynamic scheimpflug based pneumotonometer and its correlation with a dynamic bidirectional pneumotonometry device","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84916917556","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-133257","identifier":[{"id":"25393092","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84916917556","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-06e178a1-39cf-46a1-8c8a-6005b2697994"],"keyword":["Adult","Aged","Aged, 80 and over","Aging","Cornea","Elasticity","Humans","Intraocular Pressure","Reproducibility of Results","Tonometry, Ocular","Young Adult"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-6C5924B872C757F9B067A12E716AFBC9"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2015","yearSub":"2015"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Ophthalmology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Sashia","key":"baknielsenpau","last":"Bak-Nielsen","locid":"4d778acb-8da2-45ce-a916-232721f4c529","name":"Bak-Nielsen, Sashia","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Iben Bach","key":"pedersenpau","last":"Pedersen","locid":"b39a447f-dfb7-4dfc-9492-c9abcc768cd1","name":"Damgaard, Iben Bach","name_org":"Pedersen, Iben Bach","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anders","key":"ivarsenpau","last":"Ivarsen","locid":"072c0ad1-1c62-4539-8b63-868ff8422866","name":"Ivarsen, Anders","orcid":"0000-0003-1466-4804","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jesper","key":"hjortdalpau","last":"Hjortdal","locid":"b69f284e-e209-49a7-9d0d-9eeff6a8feb3","name":"Hjortdal, Jesper","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["25393092"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1097/ico.0000000000000293","issn":["02773740","15364798"],"issn_org":["02773740"],"issue":"1","journal":"Cornea","pages":"71-7","publisher":"Lippincott Williams and Wilkins","status":"p","title":"Cornea","volume":"34"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84916917556"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-06e178a1-39cf-46a1-8c8a-6005b2697994"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Repeatability, reproducibility, and age dependency of dynamic Scheimpflug-based pneumotonometer and its correlation with a dynamic bidirectional pneumotonometry device","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-133257","year":"2015","yearsub":"2015"}
