{"abstract":"What. The chapter contributes to discussions on design processes in relation to educa- tion, presenting different notions of design research and demonstrating how professors and students are involved together in designing innovative and constructive study pro- cesses that can help foster students’ engagement, self-awareness, mutual evaluation, reflec- tion, and critical and creative thinking. The case presented and analyzed is the master’s program ICT and learning (MIL), where Roskilde University is partner in the cross-insti- tutional collaboration behind the program. Why. It is a challenge for the educational system to meet the new needs for competencies owing to the change from an industrial society to a knowledge-based society. The problem- based approach can be seen as an attempt to meet these challenges. Unlike a classic cur- riculum-oriented and teacher-steered model, project work is open-ended and directed at handling unknown and dynamic processes. Where. Problem-based approaches are relevant for master’s programs with a need for collaboration, flexibility, and implementation of technologies to overcome time, space, and geographical limitations, and for study programs, where students are involved in self- directed study practices, and where there is a need for continuing reflective processes of how to learn. In relation to the bachelor program in design, humanities, and technology at Roskilde University, it is relevant to reflect on designs for learning in relation to both experimental use and analytical dimensions of new technologies. How. The methodological approach deals with developing designs for learning that involve students as co-designers through problem- and project-based learning. The approach can be applied by professors and students who collaboratively wish to develop their study programs. In the situated context of problem-based learning, we discuss how students can plan and continually redesign their project work, how a balance of verbal and written activity can be maintained, and how social media can support group processes.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Nielsen, Jørgen Lerche","Andreasen, Lars Birch"],"cuid":["S02-080.49-004859"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Designs for learning","ICT and learning","PBL","Problem Based Learning","Project work","Situated learning"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-RUC"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-RUC"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"design and co design of project organized studies","src":["aau","ruc"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-RUC"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://rucforsk.ruc.dk/ws/files/40887127/Nielsen_and_Andreasen_final_Chapter_3_for_Situated_Design_Methods_100913_pre_print.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S02-080.49-004859","ids":["aau-642dec15-af23-4072-9ed3-2ba282db6a82","ruc-7b843726-c6f2-4e23-aff0-aa890f3fb9ac"],"keyword":["Designs for learning","ICT and learning","Networked learning environments","PBL","Problem-based learning","Professor-student relationship","Project work","Situated learning"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Local-1-2-93A9560F7A38F6BFB3C0064272404FFB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://mitpress.mit.edu/books/situated-design-methods","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://rucforsk.ruc.dk/ws/files/40887127/Nielsen_and_Andreasen_final_Chapter_3_for_Situated_Design_Methods_100913_pre_print.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ruc roskilde university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-RUC","l3nl":"Roskilde University","l3nn":"RUC Roskilde University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"RUC Roskilde University","namevar":["Roskilde University, Department of Communication and Arts, Dialogic communication","Roskilde University, The Department of Communication, Business and Information Technologies"],"nora_code":"DI-U-RUC","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Humanities","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Humanities, Department of Learning and Philosophy","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Humanities, Department of Learning and Philosophy, AAU Learning Lab (Learning)","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Humanities, Department of Learning and Philosophy, Higher Education Research Group","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Humanities, Department of Learning and Philosophy, ILD - Forskningslab i IT og Læringsdesign (Learning)","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Humanities, Department of Learning and Philosophy, PBL Research Unit (Learning)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Jørgen Lerche","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"ff100a8b-4cf2-4480-84ed-fff495d54567","name":"Nielsen, Jørgen Lerche","orcid":"0000-0002-3726-9367","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Lars Birch","key":"andreasenpau","last":"Andreasen","locid":"b4c80dc4-dc5a-4ae3-9b13-76fe67640147","name":"Andreasen, Lars Birch","orcid":"0000-0002-9805-9742","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Jesper","key":"simonsenpedh","last":"Simonsen","name":"Simonsen, Jesper","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Connie","key":"svabopedh","last":"Svabo","name":"Svabo, Connie","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Sara Malou","key":"strandvadpedh","last":"Strandvad","name":"Strandvad, Sara Malou","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Kristine","key":"samsonpedh","last":"Samson","name":"Samson, Kristine","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Morten","key":"hertzumpedh","last":"Hertzum","name":"Hertzum, Morten","rank":7,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Ole Erik","key":"hansenpedh","last":"Hansen","name":"Hansen, Ole Erik","rank":8,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["9780262320993"],"isbn":["9780262027632"],"issue":"[6]","pages":"43-58","place":"Cambridge, Massachusetts","publisher":"MIT Press","series":"Design thinking, design theory","status":"p","title":"Situated Design Methods"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Roskilde University","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-642dec15-af23-4072-9ed3-2ba282db6a82","ruc-7b843726-c6f2-4e23-aff0-aa890f3fb9ac"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","RUC Roskilde University"],"title":"Design and Co-Design of Project-Organized Studies","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-080.49-004859","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"Studies on percutaneous penetration are needed to assess the hazards after unintended occupational skin exposures to industrial products as well as the efficacy after intended consumer exposure to topically applied medicinal or cosmetic products. During recent decades, a number of methods have been developed to replace methods involving experimental animals. The results obtained from these methods are decided not only by the chemical or product tested, but to a significant degree also by the experimental set-up and decisions made by the investigator during the planning phase. The present MiniReview discusses some of the existing and well-known experimental in vitro and in vivo methods for studies of percutaneous penetration together with some more recent and promising methods. After this, some considerations and recommendations about advantages and limitations of the different methods and their relevance for the prediction of percutaneous penetration are given. Which method to prefer will depend on the product to be tested and the question asked. Regulatory guidelines exist for studies on percutaneous penetration, but researchers as well as regulatory bodies need to pay specific attention to the vehicles and solvents used in donor and sampling fluids so that it reflects in-use conditions as closely as possible. Based on available experimental data, mathematical models have been developed to aid predictions of skin penetration. The authors question the general use of the present mathematical models in hazard assessment, as they seem to ignore outliers among chemicals as well as the heterogeneity of skin barrier properties and skin conditions within the exposed populations.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Holmgaard, Rikke","Benfeldt, Eva","Nielsen, Jesper Boye"],"cuid":["S02-083.67-041292"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Animals","Environmental Pollutants","Humans","Microdialysis","Models Biological","Perfusion","Risk Assessment","Skin","Skin Absorption"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"percutaneous penetration methodological considerations","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/bcpt.12188","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-083.67-041292","identifier":[{"id":"84902086539","type":"scopus"},{"id":"24373389","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-31578fe6-ed92-4f5c-9315-c15fcf6ea60d","sdu-d98026de-7db5-4b21-b57f-a74c1679c983"],"keyword":["Animals","Environmental Pollutants","Humans","Microdialysis","Models, Biological","Perfusion","Risk Assessment","Skin","Skin Absorption"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-26CEBCF403EA0FD3844112768AF01443"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/bcpt.12188","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2014","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Public Health","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Public Health, Research Unit of General Pratice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Rikke","key":"holmgaardpau","last":"Holmgaard","name":"Holmgaard, Rikke","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Eva","key":"benfeldtpau","last":"Benfeldt","locid":"7c7d625b-398b-4c53-9762-02669d007af8","name":"Benfeldt, Eva","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"J. B.","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"75938cc2-43d6-4c8d-a506-6b1c0472a382","name":"Nielsen, Jesper Bo","name_org":"Nielsen, J. B.","orcid":"0000-0002-5764-5462","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["24373389"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/bcpt.12188","issn":["17427835"],"issue":"1","journal":"Basic & Clinical Pharmacology & Toxicology","pages":"101-109","publisher":"Wiley","status":"p","title":"Basic & Clinical Pharmacology & Toxicology","volume":"115"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84902086539"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-31578fe6-ed92-4f5c-9315-c15fcf6ea60d","sdu-d98026de-7db5-4b21-b57f-a74c1679c983"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Percutaneous Penetration - Methodological Considerations","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-083.67-041292","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: tendon and skeletal muscle function adapts to physical training of resistive nature, but it is unknown to what extent persons with genetically altered connective tissue - who have a higher than normal tendon extensibility - will obtain any effect upon their tendon and muscle when undergoing muscle strength training. We investigated patients with classical Ehlers Danlos Syndrome (EDS) (collagen type V defect) who display articular hypermobility, skin extensibility and tissue fragility. METHODS: subjects underwent strength training 3 times a week for 4 months and were tested before and after intervention in regards to muscle strength, tendon mechanical properties, and muscle function. RESULTS: three subjects completed the scheduled 48 sessions and had no major adverse events. Mean isometric leg extension force and leg extensor power both increased by 8 and 11% respectively (358 to 397 N, and 117 to 123 W). The tendon stiffness was tested and an average increase in response to physical training, from 1795 to 2519 N/mm was found. On average, the training loads both in upper and lower body exercises increased by around 30% over the training period. When testing balance, the average sway-area of the participants decreased by 26% (0.144 to 0.108 m(2)). On the subscale of CIS20 the participants lowered their average subjective fatigue score from 33 to 25. CONCLUSION: in this small pilot study, heavy resistance training was both feasible and effective in classic Ehlers Danlos patients, and the results indicated that both tendon and skeletal muscle properties can be improved also in this patient group when they are subjected to resistance training.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Møller, Mathias","Kjaer, Michael","Svensson, Rene","Andersen, Jesper","Magnusson, Peter","Nygaard, Rie Harboe"],"cuid":["S02-083.81-024952"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"functional adaptation of tendon and skeletal muscle to resistance training in three patients with genetically verified classic ehlers danlos syndrome","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4241422/","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-083.81-024952","identifier":[{"id":"84957665692","type":"scopus"},{"id":"25489549","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-a83c1ea1-8b0a-4fa5-8d1c-b28133614f30","ku-4e348210-35ec-4aad-93fb-67cfba46061c"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-6A51C1E1F714C110C6189B50E7A8BB43"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4241422/","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2014","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Center for Healthy Ageing, Web Track 1: Modifiable pathways of damage and repair in aging","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Bispebjerg og Frederiksberg Universitets Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of clinical biochemistry"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Mathias","key":"møllerpau","last":"Møller","name":"Møller, Mathias","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Michael","key":"kjærpau","last":"Kjær","locid":"7937b8ab-7820-4d25-982f-1d2f58b033a3","name":"Kjaer, Michael","name_org":"Kjær, Michael","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"kjaerpau","last":"Kjaer","name":"Kjaer, Michael","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Rene","key":"svenssonpau","last":"Svensson","name":"Svensson, Rene","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Jesper","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","name":"Andersen, Jesper","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Stig Peter","key":"magnussonpau","last":"Magnusson","locid":"6f430caa-ac01-4dfe-9a62-e3a49afba21a","name":"Magnusson, S. Peter","name_org":"Magnusson, Stig Peter","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Rie Harboe","key":"nygaardpau","last":"Nygaard","locid":"6cedf5b9-a77f-46f9-b584-004800d3e356","name":"Nygaard, Rie Harboe","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Rie Harboe","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Rie Harboe","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["25489549"],"pub":{"issn":["22404554"],"issue":"3","journal":"MLTJ  Muscles, Ligaments and Tendons Journal","pages":"315-323","status":"p","title":"MLTJ  Muscles, Ligaments and Tendons Journal","title_org":"M.L.T.J. Muscles, Ligaments and Tendons Journal","volume":"4"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84957665692"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-a83c1ea1-8b0a-4fa5-8d1c-b28133614f30","ku-4e348210-35ec-4aad-93fb-67cfba46061c"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Functional Adaptation of Tendon and Skeletal Muscle to Resistance Training in Three Patients With Genetically Verified Classic Ehlers Danlos Syndrome","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-083.81-024952","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"PURPOSE: To evaluate corneal biomechanical properties after LASIK, ReLEx flex, and the flap-free procedure ReLEx smile by Scheimpflug-based dynamic tonometry (Corvis ST) and non-contact differential tonometry (Ocular Response Analyzer, ORA). METHODS: Patients treated for high myopia (-10.5 to -5.5 diopters, spherical equivalent refraction) more than one year previously at Aarhus University Hospital were included. Treatments comprised LASIK (35 eyes), ReLEx flex (31 eyes), and ReLEx smile (29 eyes). A control group included 31 healthy eyes. Cornea-compensated IOP (IOPcc), corneal hysteresis (CH), and corneal resistance factor (CRF) were measured with ORA. Corneal applanation and deformation were registered with Corvis ST during an air-pulse. RESULTS: Multiple linear regression analysis showed that CH and CRF were significantly lower after all keratorefractive procedures compared to healthy controls (p < 0.05). No significant differences were observed in CH or CRF between the keratorefractive groups. Corvis ST showed no differences in radius at highest concavity (HC radius), time until first applanation (A1 Time), time until second applanation (A2 Time), and deflection length at highest concavity (HC deflection length) between groups. LASIK treated eyes had significantly shorter time until highest concavity than eyes treated with ReLEx smile (HC Time, p = 0.01). The A1 deflection length was significantly shorter in the keratorefractive groups compared to the healthy controls (p < 0.05). CONCLUSIONS: Keratorefrative procedures alter the corneal biomechanical properties with regard to corneal hysteresis and corneal resistant factor. The flap-based LASIK and ReLEx flex and the flap-free ReLEx smile result in similar reduction in corneal biomechanics when evaluated by Corvis ST and ORA.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Damgaard, Iben Bach","Bak-Nielsen, Sashia","Vestergaard, Anders Højslet","Ivarsen, Anders","Hjortdal, Jesper"],"cuid":["S02-098.80-022623"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Adult","Biomechanical Phenomena","Biomechanics","Cornea","Cornea physiopathology","Corneal Pachymetry","Corneal Stroma","Corvis ST","Elasticity","Elasticity physiology","Female","Femtosecond laser","Humans","Keratomileusis Laser In Situ","Keratomileusis Laser In Situ methods","LASIK","Laser In Situ Keratomileusis","Lasers Excimer","Lasers Excimer therapeutic use","Male","Myopia","Myopia physiopathology","Ocular Response Analyzer","ReLEx flex ReLEx smile","Refractive surgery","Surgical Flaps","Tonometry Ocular"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"corneal biomechanical properties after lasik relex flex and relex smile by scheimpflug based dynamic tonometry","src":["au","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00417-014-2667-6","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-098.80-022623","identifier":[{"id":"24862302","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84908357162","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-88b0d1f5-ec04-4d85-89f1-d02269d0f766","sdu-0b0d229c-e4f9-4690-a044-26aa6010a8b3"],"keyword":["Adult","Biomechanical Phenomena","Biomechanics","Cornea","Cornea/physiopathology","Corneal Pachymetry","Corneal Stroma","Corvis ST","Elasticity","Elasticity/physiology","Female","Femtosecond laser","Humans","Keratomileusis, Laser In Situ","Keratomileusis, Laser In Situ/methods","LASIK","Laser In Situ Keratomileusis","Lasers, Excimer","Lasers, Excimer/therapeutic use","Male","Myopia","Myopia/physiopathology","Ocular Response Analyzer","ReLEx flex & ReLEx smile","Refractive Surgery","Surgical Flaps","Tonometry, Ocular"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-218B7059C49E90D247DB8A2C2A3112A8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00417-014-2667-6","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2014","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Biomedicine, Department of Biomedicine - Forskning og uddannelse, Syd"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"central denmark region","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CDR","l3nl":"Central Denmark Region","l3nn":"Central Denmark Region","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Central Denmark Region","namevar":["Aarhus University Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CDR","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research unit of Ophthalmology (Odense)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Iben Bach","key":"pedersenpau","last":"Pedersen","locid":"b39a447f-dfb7-4dfc-9492-c9abcc768cd1","name":"Damgaard, Iben Bach","name_org":"Pedersen, Iben Bach","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Sashia","key":"baknielsenpau","last":"Bak-Nielsen","name":"Bak-Nielsen, Sashia","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders Højslet","key":"vestergaardpau","last":"Vestergaard","locid":"aea6d7ae-1e29-4ade-8d02-a4d5e689e5c3","name":"Vestergaard, Anders Højslet","orcid":"0000-0003-1687-4899","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anders","key":"ivarsenpau","last":"Ivarsen","locid":"072c0ad1-1c62-4539-8b63-868ff8422866","name":"Ivarsen, Anders","orcid":"0000-0003-1466-4804","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jesper","key":"hjortdalpau","last":"Hjortdal","locid":"b69f284e-e209-49a7-9d0d-9eeff6a8feb3","name":"Hjortdal, Jesper","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["24862302"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s00417-014-2667-6","issn":["0721832X","1435702X"],"issn_org":["0721832X"],"issue":"8","journal":"Graefe's Archive for Clinical and Experimental Ophthalmology","pages":"1329-35","publisher":"Springer Science and Business Media Deutschland GmbH","status":"p","title":"Graefe's Archive for Clinical and Experimental Ophthalmology","volume":"252"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84908357162"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-88b0d1f5-ec04-4d85-89f1-d02269d0f766","sdu-0b0d229c-e4f9-4690-a044-26aa6010a8b3"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Corneal Biomechanical Properties after LASIK, ReLEx flex, and ReLEx smile by Scheimpflug-Based dynamic Tonometry","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-098.80-022623","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"Background-Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) is associated with increased cardiovascular risk. We examined the effect of active IBD on major adverse cardiovascular outcomes after myocardial infarction (MI). Methods and Results-In nationwide registries, we identified 86 790 patients with first-time MI from the period 2002 to 2011. A total of 1030 patients had IBD, and we categorized their disease activity stages into either flare (120 days), persistent (>120 days) activity, or remission. Short-term mortality was estimated in a logistic regression-model, whereas risk of recurrent MI, all-cause mortality, and a composite of recurrent MI, cardiovascular death, and stroke were estimated by Cox regression-models. Odds ratio of death during hospitalization or within 30 days of discharge (n=13 339) corresponded to 3.29 (95% confidence interval [CI], 1.98-5.45) for patients in IBD flares, 1.62 (95% CI, 0.95-2.77) for persistent activity, and 0.97 (95% CI, 0.78-1.19) for remission when compared with the non-IBD group. Among 73 451 patients, including 863 with IBD, alive 30 days after discharge, IBD was associated with hazard ratios of 1.21 (95% CI, 0.99-1.49) for recurrent MI, 1.14 (95% CI, 1.01-1.28) for all-cause mortality, and 1.17 (95% CI, 1.03-1.34) for the composite end point. When compared with the non-IBD group, IBD flares, in particular, were associated with increased risks of recurrent MI (hazard ratio, 3.09; 95% CI, 1.79-5.32), all-cause mortality (hazard ratio, 2.25; 95% CI, 1.61-3.15), and the composite end point (hazard ratio, 2.04; 95% CI, 1.35-3.06), whereas no increased risk was identified in remission. Conclusions-Active inflammatory bowel disease worsens prognosis after MI, in particular, in relation with flares.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Kristensen, Søren Lund","Ahlehoff, Ole","Lindhardsen, Jesper","Erichsen, Rune","Lamberts, Morten","Khalid, Usman","Nielsen, Ole Haagen","Torp-Pedersen, Christian","Gislason, Gunnar Hilmar","Hansen, Peter Riis"],"cuid":["S04-092.03-665750"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":4,"corresponding_author":["Kristensen, Søren Lund"],"keyword":["Colitis","Crohn s disease","Inflammatory bowel disease","Myocardial Infarction","Prognosis","Ulcerative"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"prognosis after first time myocardial infarction in patients with inflammatory bowel disease according to disease activity nationwide cohort study","src":["aau","au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1161/CIRCOUTCOMES.114.000918","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S04-092.03-665750","identifier":[{"id":"84925876582","type":"scopus"},{"id":"25316773","type":"pmid"},{"id":"WOS:000345289300010","type":"wos"}],"ids":["aau-31ffcddb-13d1-4f7a-a52e-8dbc6ee8e3ed","au-1804facf-0146-40ce-a675-080be6bbdc2b","ku-84c7850b-b499-43db-8220-5f3377636815","sdu-abe2727d-ecb6-42af-b3a9-ebb6a1f5c136"],"keyword":["Colitis","Crohn's disease","Inflammatory bowel disease","Myocardial infarction","Prognosis","ulcerative"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-2ED45806BBDCCA4AA4A9DD6AB516852F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1161/CIRCOUTCOMES.114.000918","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2014","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capita