{"abstract":"AIMS: Using a large, contemporary primary care population we aimed to provide absolute long-term risks of cardiovascular death (CVD) based on the QTc interval and to test whether the QTc interval is of value in risk prediction of CVD on an individual level. METHODS AND RESULTS: Digital electrocardiograms from 173 529 primary care patients aged 50-90 years were collected during 2001-11. The Framingham formula was used for heart rate-correction of the QT interval. Data on medication, comorbidity, and outcomes were retrieved from administrative registries. During a median follow-up period of 6.1 years, 6647 persons died from cardiovascular causes. Long-term risks of CVD were estimated for subgroups defined by age, gender, cardiovascular disease, and QTc interval categories. In general, we observed an increased risk of CVD for both very short and long QTc intervals. Prolongation of the QTc interval resulted in the worst prognosis for men whereas in women, a very short QTc interval was equivalent in risk to a borderline prolonged QTc interval. The effect of the QTc interval on the absolute risk of CVD was most pronounced in the elderly and in those with cardiovascular disease whereas the effect was negligible for middle-aged women without cardiovascular disease. The most important improvement in prediction accuracy was noted for women aged 70-90 years. In this subgroup, a total of 9.5% were reclassified (7.2% more accurately vs. 2.3% more inaccurately) within clinically relevant 5-year risk groups when the QTc interval was added to a conventional risk model for CVD. CONCLUSION: Important differences were observed across subgroups when the absolute long-term risk of CVD was estimated based on QTc interval duration. The accuracy of the personalized CVD prognosis can be improved when the QTc interval is introduced to a conventional risk model for CVD.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Nielsen, Jonas B.","Graff, Claus","Rasmussen, Peter V.","Pietersen, Adrian","Lind, Bent","Olesen, Morten S.","Struijk, Johannes","Haunsø, Stig","Svendsen, Jesper H.","Køber, Lars","Gerds, Thomas A.","Holst, Anders G."],"cuid":["S02-098.91-006877"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Age Factors","Aged","Aged 80 and over","Cardiovascular Diseases","Electrocardiography","Female","Heart Rate","Humans","Male","Middle Aged","Risk Assessment","Sex Factors"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"risk prediction of cardiovascular death based on the qtc interval evaluating age and gender differences in a large primary care population","src":["aau","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/eurheartj/ehu081","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-098.91-006877","identifier":[{"id":"PMC4028611","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"24603310","type":"pmid"},{"id":"WOS:000337054900010","type":"wos"},{"id":"84901378801","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-8dda847b-fbb2-4041-9bd5-f1388c05429c","ku-ab5bd556-2159-4994-b58b-a827226f19dd"],"keyword":["Age Factors","Aged","Aged, 80 and over","Cardiovascular Diseases","Electrocardiography","Female","Heart Rate","Humans","Male","Middle Aged","Risk Assessment","Sex Factors"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-425988BEA054B804A4C70BAFB8D6AB12"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/eurheartj/ehu081","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2014","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Department of Health Science and Technology","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Department of Health Science and Technology, Medical Informatics Group (CT)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Biomedical Sciences, Molecular Cardiology and Membrane Proteins"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonas B.","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Jonas B.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Claus","key":"graffpau","last":"Graff","locid":"2312aabc-cecb-4598-aede-4aa22cc2f298","name":"Graff, Claus","orcid":"0000-0001-9650-9781","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Peter V.","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","name":"Rasmussen, Peter V.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Adrian","key":"pietersenpau","last":"Pietersen","name":"Pietersen, Adrian","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Bent","key":"lindpau","last":"Lind","name":"Lind, Bent","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Morten S","key":"olesenpau","last":"Olesen","locid":"60e76597-44c1-4838-bb63-9b8568adb06f","name":"Olesen, Morten S","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Johannes","key":"struijkpau","last":"Struijk","locid":"8a2065ed-29ac-4112-b2a4-e11eef44f918","name":"Struijk, Johannes","orcid":"0000-0002-8713-1913","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Stig","key":"haunsøpau","last":"Haunsø","locid":"2d7a08cf-874a-4f9e-8964-e9c473cecd38","name":"Haunsø, Stig","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Jesper H","key":"svendsenpau","last":"Svendsen","locid":"d80910cf-2f9c-457c-9e85-34b4b258bb81","name":"Svendsen, Jesper Hastrup","name_org":"Svendsen, Jesper H","orcid":"0000-0001-8466-8515","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Lars","key":"køberpau","last":"Køber","locid":"e3f0c57c-605a-431a-9c63-2213c46621e6","name":"Køber, Lars","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","first":"Thomas A","key":"gerdspau","last":"Gerds","locid":"b87ed711-194a-48dc-b83a-3c7aad18a00d","name":"Gerds, Thomas Alexander","name_org":"Gerds, Thomas A","orcid":"0000-0002-5955-816X","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders G.","key":"holstpau","last":"Holst","name":"Holst, Anders G.","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["24603310"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1093/eurheartj/ehu081","issn":["0195668X","15229645"],"issn_org":["0195668X"],"issue":"20","journal":"European Heart Journal","pages":"1335-1344","publisher":"Oxford University Press","status":"p","title":"European Heart Journal","volume":"35"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84901378801"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-8dda847b-fbb2-4041-9bd5-f1388c05429c","ku-ab5bd556-2159-4994-b58b-a827226f19dd"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Risk prediction of cardiovascular death based on the QTc interval : evaluating age and gender differences in a large primary care population","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-098.91-006877","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"Purpose: The aim of this study was to examine whether educational meetings and group detailing could increase the use of drugs from the ward lists or the drug formulary in hospitals. Methods: Twelve medical wards from two hospitals were randomized into three groups: control, basic and extended intervention. All wards had a ward list review before interventions. Moreover, the basic intervention consisted of an educational meeting, and the extended intervention included two group detailing sessions. The proportion of drugs used from the ward list or hospital drug formulary (HDF) was the primary outcome. Data (defined daily doses [DDDs], numbers and cost [Euros]) on drugs sold to the wards were retrieved from the two hospitals from 1 July 2011 to 31 August 2012. Baseline data: from July to September 2011, and follow-up data: from June to August 2012. Results: The proportion of formulary drugs used increased for the extended intervention group (0.04, range -0.02 to 0.09) and basic intervention group (0.03, range -0.03 to 0.09) in comparison with a decrease in the control group (-0.01, range -0.03 to -0.02). The interventions did not significantly change odds for selecting drugs from the formulary in comparison with the control group (basic intervention: OR 1.09 [95 % CI 0.81 to 1.46]; extended intervention: OR 1.00 [95 % CI 0.75 to 1.35]). Conclusions: In this study, educational meetings and group detailing do not significantly improve adherence to ward lists or HDF. The adherence to the formularies at baseline was relatively high, which may explain why the interventions did not have a significant effect.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Plet, Hanne T.","Kjeldsen, Lene J.","Christensen, René Depont","Nielsen, Gitte Søndergaard","Hallas, Jesper"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-004543"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Plet, Hanne T."],"keyword":["Cluster randomized controlled trial","Educational meetings","Group detailing","Hospital","Hospital drug formulary","Intervention"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["The Research Unit for General Practice, Copenhagen, Denmark"],"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-PR-OTH","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"do educational meetings and group detailing change adherence to drug formularies in hospitals a cluster randomized controlled trial","src":["aau","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00228-013-1589-2","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-004543","identifier":[{"id":"WOS:000329095900012","type":"wos"},{"id":"84891844788","type":"scopus"},{"id":"24077960","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["aau-5d902b25-d41f-4ee2-87ea-4e36012bac0b","sdu-0337667e-9132-4eef-8723-6aaf18f1cb40"],"keyword":["Cluster-randomized controlled trial","Educational meetings","Group detailing","Hospital","Hospital drug formulary","Intervention"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-E7517D4BE30FA1AE6E158F0948571765"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00228-013-1589-2","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2014","yearSub":"2013"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital, Klinik Akut",