{"abstract":"One of the main tools to construct secure two-party computation protocols are Yao garbled circuits. Using the cut-and-choose technique, one can get reasonably efficient Yao-based protocols with security against malicious adversaries. At TCC 2009, Nielsen and Orlandi [28] suggested to apply cut-and-choose at the gate level, while previously cut-and-choose was applied on the circuit as a whole. This idea allows for a speed up with practical significance (in the order of the logarithm of the size of the circuit) and has become known as the “LEGO” construction. Unfortunately the construction in [28] is based on a specific number-theoretic assumption and requires public-key operations per gate of the circuit. The main technical contribution of this work is a new XOR-homomorphic commitment scheme based on oblivious transfer, that we use to cope with the problem of connecting the gates in the LEGO construction. Our new protocol has the following advantages: It maintains the efficiency of the LEGO cut-and-choose. After a number of seed oblivious transfers linear in the security parameter, the construction uses only primitives from Minicrypt (i.e., private-key cryptography) per gate in the circuit (hence the name MiniLEGO). MiniLEGO is compatible with all known optimization for Yao garbled gates (row reduction, free-XORs, point-and-permute).","author_count":7,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Tore Kasper","Jakobsen, Thomas Pelle","Nielsen, Jesper Buus","Nordholt, Peter Sebastian","Orlandi, Claudio"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-274008"],"event":{"acronym":"EUROCRYPT","end":"2013-05-26","place":"Athens, gr","start":"2013-05-26","title":"Internationall Conference on Theory and Application of Cryptographic Techniques","year":"2013"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"minilego efficient secure two party computation from general assumptions","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-38348-9_32","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-274008","identifier":[{"id":"84883362608","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-fd973d76-e3e8-411d-85ae-af5243552a89"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-8FDB4E7AC2BED397E889FD081C000566"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-38348-9_32","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Tore Kasper","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"b96bd502-b384-44c2-9a99-2386d87d22b8","name":"Frederiksen, Tore Kasper","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas Pelle","key":"jakobsenpau","last":"Jakobsen","locid":"03cea827-2da0-4861-92c1-4162551c38c3","name":"Jakobsen, Thomas Pelle","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jesper Buus","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"f428c4b3-80b3-403a-ada6-9ecb0fad97b2","name":"Nielsen, Jesper Buus","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Peter Sebastian","key":"nordholtpau","last":"Nordholt","locid":"45ed0f2e-3f58-4839-b089-4ba3cd802323","name":"Nordholt, Peter Sebastian","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Claudio","key":"orlandipau","last":"