{"abstract":"Strongyle parasites are ubiquitous in grazing horses, with cyathostomins being the most prevalent, but the large strongyles having larger clinical impact. Strongylus vulgaris is considered most pathogenic nematode, with migrating larvae causing verminous endarteritis and potentially ischaemic infarction of intestinal segments. Developing anthelmintic resistance in equine parasites has put emphasis on less intensive treatment regimens to maintain efficacy of current anthelmintics. This has been associated with apparent re-emergence of S. vulgaris. Currently there are no methods for diagnosing the pathogenic migrating larval stages of S. vulgaris ante mortem. A cDNA library was constructed from RNA extracted from migrating S. vulgaris larvae. Excretory-secretory antigens from S. vulgaris adult specimens were used to immunise a rat. Hyperimmune serum was used to immunoscreen the cDNA library, an immunoreactive cDNA clone was subcloned into E. coli and the plasmid sequenced, the open reading frame encoding the mature protein was cloned into a pET22b expression vector and expressed as a His-tagged recombinant protein in BL21 expression cells. The recombinant protein was used in an indirect enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) measuring antigen-specific IgG(T) levels. Sera from 22 horses with necropsy-confirmed presence or absence of S. vulgaris were used in the ELISA. The best correlation was seen when analysing antigen-specific IgG(T) levels. Sera from horses with confirmed presence of larval stages of S. vulgaris (n=9) reacted against the recombinant protein, expressed as optic density (OD) readings of >24 % of a positive control, while sera from negative horses had OD readings <2.5 % of the positive control with a sensitivity and specificity of 1. The ELISA shows promise for diagnosing S. vulgaris infection in suspected clinical cases, measuring herd level exposure, and as a potentially useful research tool for investigating possible risk factors associated with development of disease.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Andersen, Ulla Vestergaard","Howe, Daniel K.","Olsen, Susanne Nautrup","Monrad, Jesper","Nejsum, Peter","Lyons, Eugene T.","Nielsen, Martin Krarup"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-480631"],"event":{"end":"2012-08-07","place":"San Diego, Californien, us","start":"2012-08-04","title":"’Amercian Association of Veterinary Parasitologists’ (AAVP)","year":"2012"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Gluck Equine Research Center"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"serological diagnosis of strongylus vulgaris infection use of a recombinant protein","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-480631","ids":["ku-91ec37a6-5c53-4c89-b523-13ad9cf876f6"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-CBBE30B09665146A2A60A4C4F6A3AF23"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Veterinary Clinical Sciences, Large Animal Medicine & Surgery"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"gluck equine research center","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Gluck Equine Research Center","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Ulla Vestergaard","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"48daa892-82af-42f4-8bed-f8d872f9cd4a","name":"Andersen, Ulla Vestergaard","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Daniel K.","key":"howepau","last":"Howe","name":"Howe, Daniel K.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Susanne Nautrup","key":"olsenpau","last":"Olsen","locid":"e791867b-8508-4bb1-a4b3-f0bdd2af41f1","name":"Olsen, Susanne Nautrup","orcid":"0000-0003-4327-4709","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jesper","key":"monradpau","last":"Monrad","locid":"5c679e36-e4e5-482b-b7a8-d3216bb1240e","name":"Monrad, Jesper","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Peter","key":"nejsumpau","last":"Nejsum","locid":"a21a2f45-b747-46f8-8b4b-b369c9aba7d1","name":"Nejsum, Peter","orcid":"0000-0002-6673-8505","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Eugene T.","key":"lyonspau","last":"Lyons","name":"Lyons, Eugene T.","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Martin Krarup","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"e55baed5-a4d1-42d4-b5ad-a86d9097837f","name":"Nielsen, Martin Krarup","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-91ec37a6-5c53-4c89-b523-13ad9cf876f6"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Serological diagnosis of Strongylus vulgaris infection: use of a recombinant protein","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-480631","year":"2012","yearsub":"2012"}
{"abstract":"PURPOSE: The study aim was to evaluate patient acceptance and content with remote follow-up (FU) of their implantable cardioverter defibrillator (ICD) and to estimate patients' wish for changes in remote follow-up routines. METHODS: Four hundred seventy-four ICD patients at the device follow-up clinic at Rigshospitalet using CareLink® (Medtronic) remote follow-up, who had made =2 transmissions, received a questionnaire. RESULTS: Three hundred eighty-five patients (81.2%) answered. Mean time with ICD was 56¿±¿45 months and mean age was 62¿±¿13 years; 80% was male. Diagnosis related to ICD implant was: ischemic heart disease in 56% and dilated cardiomyopathy in 21%. Twenty-six percent had primary prophylactic indication. Mean time on remote FU was 16.4¿±¿6.9 months. Mean time spent on in-clinic FU (two-way transport and FU) was 4 h and 36 min¿±¿7 h and 50 min, excluding 12 patients from Greenland and Faroe Islands. Ninety-five percent of the patients was very content or content with remote FU compared to in-clinic FU; 3% was less content and 2% was not content. For scheduled transmissions, 21% of the patients wished for a faster reply (sms or e-mail) compared to current practice with a letter. Eighty-four percent preferred more detailed information concerning ICD leads, battery status, and ICD therapies. A total of 96 patients (25%) had performed extra unscheduled remote transmissions: 20 due to shock, 20 due to alarm, 35 due to palpitations, and 18 for other or combined reasons. CONCLUSION: Ninety-five percent of the patients were content with the remote FU. Only 25% had unscheduled transmissions and most unscheduled transmissions were for appropriate reasons. Eighty-four percent of the patients wished for a more detailed response and 21% wished for a faster reply after routine transmissions.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Petersen, Helen Høgh","Larsen, Mie Christa Jensen","Nielsen, Olav Wendelboe","Kensing, Finn","Svendsen, Jesper Hastrup"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-512183"],"filters":{"added":"2022-04","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Design of Organizational IT"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"patient satisfaction and suggestions for improvement of remote icd monitoring","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10840-012-9675-4","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-512183","identifier":[{"id":"22418649","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84864722237","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-c6f339a7-3056-4db6-8669-7a49a1159778"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-9664E1FF4F89296A8F3660ED2EF1B448"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10840-012-9675-4","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Design of Organizational IT"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1},{"affno":2,"key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Section of Surgery and Internal Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU"}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Helen Høgh","key":"petersenpau","last":"Petersen","name":"Petersen, Helen Høgh","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Mie Christa Jensen","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","name":"Larsen, Mie Christa Jensen","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Olav Wendelboe","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Olav Wendelboe","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Finn","key":"kensingpau","last":"Kensing","name":"Kensing, Finn","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Jesper Hastrup","key":"svendsenpau","last":"Svendsen","locid":"d80910cf-2f9c-457c-9e85-34b4b258bb81","name":"Svendsen, Jesper Hastrup","orcid":"0000-0001-8466-8515","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["22418649"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s10840-012-9675-4","issn":["1383875X","15728595"],"issn_org":["1383875X"],"issue":"3","journal":"Journal of Interventional Cardiac Electrophysiology","pages":"317-24","status":"p","title":"Journal of Interventional Cardiac Electrophysiology","volume":"34"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84864722237"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-c6f339a7-3056-4db6-8669-7a49a1159778"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Patient satisfaction and suggestions for improvement of remote ICD monitoring","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-512183","year":"2012","yearsub":"2012"}
{"abstract":"Regular physical activity has a positive impact on cognition and brain function. Here we investigated if a single bout of exercise can improve motor memory and motor skill learning. We also explored if the timing of the exercise bout in relation to the timing of practice has any impact on the acquisition and retention of a motor skill. Forty-eight young subjects were randomly allocated into three groups, which practiced a visuomotor accuracy-tracking task either before or after a bout of intense cycling or after rest. Motor skill acquisition was assessed during practice and retention was measured 1 hour, 24 hours and 7 days after practice. Differences among groups in the rate of motor skill acquisition were not significant. In contrast, both exercise groups showed a significantly better retention of the motor skill 24 hours and 7 days after practice. Furthermore, compared to the subjects that exercised before practice, the subjects that exercised after practice showed a better retention of the motor skill 7 days after practice. These findings indicate that one bout of intense exercise performed immediately before or after practicing a motor task is sufficient to improve the long-term retention of a motor skill. The positive effects of acute exercise on motor memory are maximized when exercise is performed immediately after practice, during the early stages of memory consolidation. Thus, the timing of exercise in relation to practice is possibly an important factor regulating the effects of acute exercise on long-term motor memory.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Roig, Marc","Skriver, Kasper Christen","Lundbye-Jensen, Jesper","Kiens, Bente","Nielsen, Jens Bo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-519891"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a single bout of exercise improves motor memory","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0044594","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-519891","identifier":[{"id":"PMC3433433","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"22973462","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84866014979","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-d3af83b9-0456-4200-a188-c0545d977664"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-6FBF3D0617AA75221179988DFC713FA9"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0044594","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Nutrition, Exercise and Sports, Department of Exercise and Sport Sciences"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Marc","key":"roigpau","last":"Roig","locid":"ed3fcade-28fd-4368-b563-8f734fe00b9c","name":"Roig, Marc","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Kasper Christen","key":"skriverpau","last":"Skriver","locid":"ed29b37d-797b-4d09-9d32-e7337a1c9cf7","name":"Skriver, Kasper Christen","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Jesper","key":"lundbyejensenpau","last":"Lundbye-Jensen","locid":"337846ab-970f-43ed-907d-a3f06b03ce2d","name":"Lundbye-Jensen, Jesper","orcid":"0000-0003-3144-861X","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Bente","key":"kienspau","last":"Kiens","locid":"d0bf12e6-2968-49da-b7ce-9e6ab6434ba1","name":"Kiens, Bente","orcid":"0000-0001-5705-5625","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jens Bo","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"8b75713d-f9df-4123-8a0c-53b387d60776","name":"Nielsen, Jens Bo","orcid":"0000-0001-5568-2916","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["22973462"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0044594","issn":["19326203"],"issue":"9","journal":"PLOS ONE","paperid":"e44594","status":"p","title":"PLOS ONE","title_org":"P L o S One","volume":"7"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84866014979"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-d3af83b9-0456-4200-a188-c0545d977664"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A single bout of exercise improves motor memory","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-519891","year":"2012","yearsub":"2012"}
{"abstract":"Advances in proteomics are continually driven by the introduction of new mass spectrometric instrumentation with improved performances. The recently introduced quadrupole Orbitrap (Q Exactive) tandem mass spectrometer allows fast acquisition of high-resolution higher-energy collisional dissociation (HCD) tandem mass spectra due to the parallel mode of operation, where the generation, filling, and storage of fragment ions can be performed while simultaneously measuring another ion packet in the Orbitrap mass analyzer. In this study, data-dependent acquisition methods for \"fast\" or \"sensitive\" scanning were optimized and assessed by comparing stable isotope labeled yeast proteome coverage. We discovered that speed was the most important parameter for sample loads above 125 ng, where a 95 ms HCD scanning method allowed for identification and quantification of more than 2000 yeast proteins from 1 h of analysis time. At sample loads below 125 ng, a 156 ms HCD acquisition method improved the sensitivity, mass accuracy, and quality of data and enabled us to identify 30% more proteins and peptides than the faster scanning method. A similar effect was observed when the LC gradient was extended to 2 or 3 h for the analysis of complex mammalian whole cell lysates. Using a 3 h LC gradient, the sensitive method enabled identification of more than 4000 proteins from 1 µg of tryptic HeLa digest, which was almost 200 more identifications compared to the faster scanning method. Our results demonstrate that peptide identification on a quadrupole Orbitrap is dependent on sample amounts, acquisition speed, and data quality, which emphasizes the need for acquisition methods tailored for different sample loads and analytical preferences.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Kelstrup, Christian D","Young, Clifford","Lavallee, Richard","Nielsen, Michael L.","Olsen, Jesper V."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-522786"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"optimized fast and sensitive acquisition methods for shotgun proteomics on a quadrupole orbitrap mass spectrometer","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/pr3000249","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-522786","identifier":[{"id":"84861800006","type":"scopus"},{"id":"22537090","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-d88f56df-910e-4b1d-a966-d143d5b440a0"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-EA657FF317543162DE800031AFC6738D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/pr3000249","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Novo Nordisk Foundation Center for Protein Research, Proteomics Program"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Christian D","key":"kelstruppau","last":"Kelstrup","locid":"e7c7a8d5-601f-4618-b879-401f673271a8","name":"Kelstrup, Christian D","orcid":"0000-0003-4647-1425","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Clifford","key":"youngpau","last":"Young","locid":"ebf25d13-dfce-4008-9682-3649c411875b","name":"Young, Clifford","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Richard","key":"lavalleepau","last":"Lavallee","name":"Lavallee, Richard","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Michael Lund","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"b72d98f7-79fa-43a7-8066-783ed8034146","