,"issue":"43","journal":"Medicine and Science in Sports and Exercise","pages":"120","publisher":"Lippincott Williams and Wilkins","status":"p","title":"Medicine and Science in Sports and Exercise","volume":"01/2011"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All University Colleges","DI-ALL","DI-UC","DI-UC-ALL","KP","University Colleges"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["kp-08bf4b82-6c58-41b5-b19d-f8ecbe8975bd"],"src":["KP University College Copenhagen"],"title":"Ankle Muscle Activity during Dynamic and Static Exercises on Unstable Rehabilitation Equipment","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-385321","year":"2011"}
{"abstract":"Humans are capable of learning numerous motor skills, but newly acquired skills may be abolished by subsequent learning. Here we ask what factors determine whether interference occurs in motor learning. We speculated that interference requires competing processes of synaptic plasticity in overlapping circuits and predicted specificity. To test this, subjects learned a ballistic motor task. Interference was observed following subsequent learning of an accuracy-tracking task, but only if the competing task involved the same muscles and movement direction. Interference was not observed from a non-learning task suggesting that interference requires competing learning. Subsequent learning of the competing task 4 h after initial learning did not cause interference suggesting disruption of early motor memory consolidation as one possible mechanism underlying interference. Repeated transcranial magnetic stimulation (rTMS) of corticospinal motor output at intensities below movement threshold did not cause interference, whereas suprathreshold rTMS evoking motor responses and (re)afferent activation did. Finally, the experiments revealed that suprathreshold repetitive electrical stimulation of the agonist (but not antagonist) peripheral nerve caused interference. The present study is, to our knowledge, the first to demonstrate that peripheral nerve stimulation may cause interference. The finding underscores the importance of sensory feedback as error signals in motor learning. We conclude that interference requires competing plasticity in overlapping circuits. Interference is remarkably specific for circuits involved in a specific movement and it may relate to sensory error signals.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Lundbye-Jensen, Jesper","Petersen, Tue Hvass","Rothwell, John C","Nielsen, Jens Bo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-430159"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University College London"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"interference in ballistic motor learning specificity and role of sensory error signals","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/35306425/journal.pone.0017451_15_.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-430159","identifier":[{"id":"79952404027","type":"scopus"},{"id":"21408054","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC3052297","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-3d741fd1-bbfd-427e-ba36-4beec95b14fb"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-274E24BB8750377D1EF0B18F420BD3DE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0017451","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/35306425/journal.pone.0017451_15_.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Nutrition, Exercise and Sports, Department of Exercise and Sport Sciences"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university college london","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University College London","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Jesper","key":"lundbyejensenpau","last":"Lundbye-Jensen","locid":"337846ab-970f-43ed-907d-a3f06b03ce2d","name":"Lundbye-Jensen, Jesper","orcid":"0000-0003-3144-861X","r